window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["rinpas-2019"] = {"id": "rinpas-2019", "title": "RINPAS 2019", "fullTitle": "RINPAS Ranchi 2019, Ph.D / M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "100 real questions · printed worked key, checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "history": "History of Psychology and Psychiatry"}, "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following perspectives of psychology is most concerned with individuals reaching their maximum potential?", "options": {"a": "Behavioural", "b": "Cognitive", "c": "Humanistic", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the humanistic perspective is the one most concerned with people reaching their maximum potential. Humanistic psychology grew in the United States in the 1950s and 1960s as a \"third force\", a reaction against both psychoanalysis (people driven by unconscious instincts) and behaviourism (people shaped by rewards and punishments). Its founders argued that people have free will, are basically good and carry an inborn drive to grow. Abraham Maslow (1943) called this drive self-actualisation: once the lower needs in his hierarchy (physiological, safety, belonging and esteem) are met, a person strives to become everything he or she is capable of becoming. He developed the idea fully in Motivation and Personality (1954). Carl Rogers (1951) built client-centred therapy on the same assumption, the actualising tendency, and described the goal of growth as the fully functioning person, someone open to experience and living fully in the present. Example: Meera leaves a secure bank job to train as a classical dancer because dancing is what she feels she is meant to do; a humanistic psychologist sees this as self-actualisation, not as a defence or a conditioned habit. Option c is right because only the humanistic perspective puts growth towards one's full potential at the centre of its theory. Exam trap: the psychodynamic perspective is about inner forces beyond the person's control, and positive psychology (Martin Seligman, 1998) also studies strengths and flourishing, but the classic textbook pairing is fixed: maximum potential, free will and the third force all mean humanistic (Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers).", "topic": "Humanistic perspective and self-actualisation"}, {"q": "The basic difference between obsession and compulsion is:", "options": {"a": "The former involves preoccupation with certain thoughts while the latter involves preoccupation with certain actions", "b": "The former is a mood disorder whereas the latter is an anxiety disorder", "c": "Actually, there is no difference between the two disorders", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: an obsession is a preoccupation with certain thoughts, while a compulsion is a preoccupation with certain actions. Both are symptoms of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Obsessions are recurrent, persistent and unwanted thoughts, urges or images that intrude into the mind and cause marked anxiety or distress, such as a fear of contamination or a nagging doubt that the door is unlocked. The person usually recognises them as products of his or her own mind and tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (hand washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (counting, silently repeating a prayer) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or by rigid rules, to reduce distress or prevent a feared event. The relief is brief, so the cycle repeats and strengthens. Example: Arjun keeps thinking that his hands carry germs (obsession), so he washes them twenty times before every meal (compulsion). DSM-5 (2013) requires obsessions, compulsions or both that are time-consuming (for example more than one hour a day) or cause clinically significant distress or impairment. Option a is right because it states the classic distinction: the obsession is the thought, the compulsion is the act that answers it. Exam trap: obsession and compulsion are two symptoms of one disorder, not two separate disorders; and compulsions can be silent mental acts such as counting, so a ritual that happens in the head is still a compulsion if it is done to reduce anxiety. OCD was never a mood disorder; it sat with the anxiety disorders through DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) and got its own chapter only in DSM-5 (2013).", "topic": "Obsessions versus compulsions"}, {"q": "The process by which the egg is matured and released is:", "options": {"a": "Mitosis", "b": "Fertilisation", "c": "Ovulation", "d": "Implantation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: ovulation is the process by which a mature egg (ovum) is released from the ovary. Each month, follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the anterior pituitary ripens a follicle in one ovary, and a surge of luteinising hormone (LH) around the middle of a typical 28-day menstrual cycle (about day 14) makes the follicle burst and release its egg. The egg is swept into the fallopian tube, where it can be fertilised for only about a day. The order of early prenatal events is worth memorising. First comes ovulation (the egg is released). Next is fertilisation, when a sperm and the ovum unite, usually in the fallopian tube, to form a single cell, the zygote. The zygote then divides by mitosis as it travels down to the uterus. Finally comes implantation, when the blastocyst embeds in the uterine wall about 1 to 2 weeks after conception, which ends the germinal period. Example: if Riya has a regular 28-day cycle, she would usually ovulate around day 14. Option c is right because it is the only term that names the maturing and release of the egg. Exam trap: examiners place fertilisation and implantation beside ovulation because they come right after it in the sequence; ask whether the stem is about the egg leaving the ovary (ovulation), meeting the sperm (fertilisation) or attaching to the uterus (implantation). Also remember that eggs and sperm are formed by meiosis, not mitosis.", "topic": "Ovulation and early prenatal events"}, {"q": "The process by which we learn not to respond to similar stimuli in an identical manner is:", "options": {"a": "Generalisation", "b": "Extinction", "c": "Discrimination", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: discrimination is the process by which we learn not to respond to similar stimuli in the same way. Ivan Pavlov (1927) described it in his work on conditioned reflexes in dogs. A dog conditioned to salivate to one tone at first also salivates to other, similar tones; this spread is stimulus generalisation, and it follows a gradient (the more similar the tone, the stronger the response). If the original tone keeps being followed by food while the similar tones never are, the dog learns to salivate only to the original tone: stimulus discrimination. The same process works in operant conditioning, where a discriminative stimulus (B. F. Skinner, 1938) signals that a response will be reinforced, so a pigeon learns to peck only when a green light is on. Discrimination narrows responding; generalisation widens it. When Ivan Pavlov's co-worker made the discrimination impossible (an ellipse was made more and more like the circle), the dog became agitated, the experimental neurosis. Example: little Kabir was bitten by a large black dog and at first feared all dogs (generalisation); after many friendly meetings with his neighbour's small white dog, he now fears only large black dogs (discrimination). Option c is right because learning to respond differently to stimuli that resemble each other is the definition of discrimination. Exam trap: generalisation is the exact reverse (responding in the same way to similar stimuli), and examiners rely on the word \"similar\" appearing in both definitions; check whether the stem says the response is the same or different.", "topic": "Stimulus discrimination"}, {"q": "Which of the following gathers information about the brain and indicates overall activity of the brain?", "options": {"a": "Positron Emission Tomography", "b": "Electroencephalogram", "c": "Electrical Stimulation", "d": "Studying damage to the brain"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the electroencephalogram (EEG) gathers information about the brain and shows its overall level of activity. Metal electrodes placed on the scalp pick up the summed electrical activity of millions of cortical neurons firing together, and the machine displays it as wave patterns. Hans Berger (1929) published the first human EEG recordings. Because the EEG changes within milliseconds, it has excellent temporal resolution, but it cannot say precisely where the activity comes from (poor spatial resolution). Its wave bands track the brain's overall state: beta waves in alert waking, alpha waves in relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed, theta waves in light sleep and slow delta waves in deep sleep. That is why EEG is the standard tool in sleep research and in the diagnosis of epilepsy, where sudden spikes show abnormal bursts of activity. Example: when Meera is referred after a fit, the neurologist orders an EEG to look at the general pattern of her brain's electrical activity, not a detailed picture of one region. Option b is right because the stem asks for a method that indicates overall brain activity, which is exactly what scalp EEG provides. Exam trap: PET also measures activity, so it is the near-miss; but positron emission tomography injects a radioactive tracer and maps where glucose use or blood flow rises in specific regions, whereas EEG gives the global electrical state. Remember: EEG tells you when, imaging tells you where.", "topic": "EEG and brain recording methods"}, {"q": "The physiological and psychological response to stress is referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Transition", "b": "Stressors", "c": "Strain", "d": "Hypertension"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the physiological and psychological response to stress is called strain. The terms come from engineering. In physics, stress is the load or force applied to a material, and strain is the deformation it produces: a bridge under heavy traffic (stress) bends slightly (strain). Psychology borrowed the analogy. The demand or event is the stressor, and the person's reaction to it (racing heart, raised cortisol, tense muscles, worry, irritability, poor sleep) is the strain. Hans Selye (1936, 1956) described the bodily side of this reaction as the general adaptation syndrome: an alarm stage (fight-or-flight arousal), a resistance stage (the body copes but stays aroused) and an exhaustion stage (resources run down and illness becomes likely). Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984) added the psychological side: how much strain we feel depends on how we appraise the demand and our resources to meet it. Example: Kabir's board examinations are the stressor; his sleeplessness, headaches and constant worry through March are the strain. Option c is right because it is the only option that names the response rather than the cause. Exam trap: option b, stressors, is the demand itself (examinations, bereavement, traffic), and examiners count on students mixing up cause and response; hypertension is one possible long-term result of strain, not the response itself. Key note: many textbooks call the response itself \"stress\" and keep \"strain\" for its wearing effect on the person; \"strain\" is the only option that names the response, so c stands.", "topic": "Stressor, stress and strain"}, {"q": "The behavioural technique for reducing anxiety in which patients practise relaxation while visualising anxiety-provoking situations of increasing intensity is called:", "options": {"a": "Symptom substitution", "b": "Time-out", "c": "Systematic Desensitisation", "d": "Token Economy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the technique is systematic desensitisation, developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe (1958) in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition. It rests on reciprocal inhibition: relaxation and anxiety cannot occur at the same time, so if deep relaxation is paired with a feared stimulus, the relaxation inhibits the anxiety. This is a form of counterconditioning, replacing a fear response with a calm one. Therapy has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually a shortened form of Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive muscle relaxation, tensing and releasing one muscle group after another. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, often rated on Joseph Wolpe's Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each item in turn, moving up only when the current step no longer causes anxiety. Example: Riya fears flying; she first imagines looking at a photograph of a plane, then booking a ticket, then waiting at the gate and finally take-off, relaxing at every stage. Option c is right because the stem names all three parts: relaxation, visualisation and situations of increasing intensity. Exam trap: graded exposure while relaxed is desensitisation, but exposure to the most feared situation at full intensity from the start is flooding (implosion when the scene is imagined); and desensitisation works by reciprocal inhibition, not by operant reinforcement, extinction alone or catharsis.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation"}, {"q": "The ability to acquire information from the culture, such as vocabulary and the kind of information learned in schools, is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Fluid intelligence", "b": "Componential intelligence", "c": "Crystallised intelligence", "d": "Contextual intelligence"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: knowledge acquired from one's culture, such as vocabulary and school learning, is crystallised intelligence. Raymond Cattell (1943, tested 1963), later working with his student John Horn, split Charles Spearman's (1904) general factor g into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, for example spotting the rule in a series of shapes in John Raven's (1938) Progressive Matrices. It is closely tied to the efficiency of the brain, peaks in early adulthood and then slowly declines. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of facts, words, skills and strategies built up through education and experience in a culture. It is measured by vocabulary, general information and comprehension tests, and it stays stable or keeps rising into late adulthood. The two are linked, because fluid ability is invested in learning and so helps produce crystallised knowledge. Example: when Arjun, a retired teacher, beats his grandson at a crossword, he is using crystallised intelligence; when the grandson masters an unfamiliar puzzle game faster, he is using fluid intelligence. Option c is right because the stem's clue words, information from the culture, vocabulary and school learning, define crystallised intelligence. Exam trap: componential and contextual intelligence sound similar but belong to Robert Sternberg's (1985) triarchic theory, not to Raymond Cattell's model; and swapping fluid with crystallised is the commonest slip, so link \"crystallised\" to knowledge that has hardened through learning.", "topic": "Fluid versus crystallised intelligence"}, {"q": "Critics of Freudian psychoanalysis maintain that this form of therapy:", "options": {"a": "Does not put enough emphasis on sex", "b": "Can be harmful by tolerating or encouraging transference", "c": "Is good mostly for treating phobias", "d": "Is too quick to be effective"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: critics argue that psychoanalysis can be harmful because it tolerates or even encourages transference. Transference is the patient's unconscious redirection onto the analyst of feelings, wishes and attitudes first felt towards important people in childhood, usually the parents. The patient may come to love, depend on or resent the analyst as if he or she were that parent. Sigmund Freud (1912) saw transference as the engine of therapy: by interpreting it, the analyst shows the patient old conflicts being replayed in the present, and the resulting transference neurosis can be worked through. Critics see real dangers. Intense dependence can stretch therapy over years, blur professional boundaries, open the way to exploitation and leave the patient worse off when treatment ends, and the analyst's own feelings towards the patient (countertransference) can deepen the problem. More broadly, Hans Eysenck (1952) attacked psychoanalysis on outcome grounds, claiming that about two thirds of neurotic patients improved within two years with or without therapy. Example: Meera, in her third year of analysis, cannot make any decision without first asking her analyst, just as she once relied on her father; a critic would say the method has created a new problem. Option b is right because harm through fostered transference is a genuine and frequently cited criticism. Exam trap: option d is reversed, since the standard complaint is that psychoanalysis is too long and too costly, not too quick; and the usual charge is that Sigmund Freud put too much emphasis on sex, not too little.", "topic": "Transference and criticisms of psychoanalysis"}, {"q": "When Maya first saw a violent movie, she was scared and disgusted. But now that she has seen several such movies, she is barely affected by them. Maya has experienced:", "options": {"a": "Habituation", "b": "Displacement", "c": "Cultivation", "d": "Catharsis"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Maya has experienced habituation, a decrease in response to a stimulus that is presented again and again. Habituation is the simplest form of learning: the organism learns that a repeated stimulus is harmless or unimportant and stops reacting strongly to it. It is not simple fatigue, because a long break brings the response back (spontaneous recovery), and a new or sudden stimulus can restore it at once (dishabituation). Eric Kandel traced it, in work from the 1960s and 1970s, to weakened transmission at synapses in the sea slug Aplysia. In media research the same process is called desensitisation: repeated exposure to screen violence blunts both the emotional and the physiological arousal it first produced, so later scenes feel less shocking and attract less concern. That is exactly Maya's story: fear and disgust the first time, almost nothing after several such films. Infant researchers use habituation too: Robert Fantz (1964) showed that babies look less and less at a picture they have seen many times and look longer at a new one, which proves they can tell the two apart. Option a is right because the stem describes a strong first reaction that weakens with repeated exposure to the same kind of stimulus. Exam trap: cultivation (option c) is also about heavy media viewing, so it is the near-miss, but George Gerbner's cultivation theory (1970s) concerns changed beliefs about the world (heavy viewers think the world is more dangerous than it is), not a fading emotional reaction.", "topic": "Habituation and media desensitisation"}, {"q": "Which of the following approaches to personality is least deterministic?", "options": {"a": "The Psychoanalytic Approach", "b": "The Humanistic Approach", "c": "The Behaviourist Approach", "d": "The Social Learning Approach"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the humanistic approach is the least deterministic approach to personality. Determinism is the view that behaviour is fully caused by forces the person does not choose, such as instincts, childhood experiences or rewards from the environment; its opposite is free will. Duane Schultz and Sydney Ellen Schultz compare personality theories on exactly this question. Sigmund Freud's (1900, 1923) psychoanalysis is strongly deterministic: behaviour is driven by unconscious sexual and aggressive instincts and largely fixed by the first five years of life (psychic determinism). B. F. Skinner's (1953) behaviourism is just as deterministic, but the causes lie in the environment: reinforcement history shapes everything, and in Beyond Freedom and Dignity (1971) he called free will an illusion. Albert Bandura's (1977, 1986) social learning approach softens this with reciprocal determinism, in which person, behaviour and environment influence one another, so people have some self-regulation. Humanists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) go furthest: people have free will, are responsible for their choices, are shaped more by present experience than by the past, and can grow towards self-actualisation. Example: when Kabir leaves engineering to study music, a humanist explains it by his own choice and growth, not by his childhood or past rewards. Option b is right because the humanistic approach gives the most room to choice and the least to outside causes. Exam trap: social learning theory also stresses agency and self-efficacy, so it is the tempting near-miss, but it still explains behaviour through learning history and situations, and its own label is reciprocal determinism; only humanism puts free will at the centre.", "topic": "Free will versus determinism"}, {"q": "To Ellis, an \"irrational\" idea is typically:", "options": {"a": "An \"emotional truth\"", "b": "Repressed", "c": "Indisputable", "d": "Unrealistic or untrue"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: to Albert Ellis, an irrational idea is one that is unrealistic or untrue. Albert Ellis (1955, 1962) founded rational therapy, later called rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), on the claim that our emotions come mainly from our beliefs about events rather than from the events themselves. His ABC model sets this out: A is the activating event, B the belief about it, and C the emotional and behavioural consequence. Rational beliefs are flexible, logical and consistent with reality, and they help us reach our goals. Irrational beliefs are rigid, illogical, unrealistic and self-defeating. Albert Ellis grouped them around absolutist \"musts\" (\"I must succeed\", \"others must treat me fairly\"), awfulising (\"it would be terrible\"), low frustration tolerance (\"I can't stand it\") and global self-rating (\"I am worthless\"). Therapy adds D, disputing: the therapist actively challenges each belief by asking whether it is logical, whether there is evidence for it and whether it helps, which leads to E, a new and effective belief. Example: Arjun fails one test (A), believes \"I must never fail, so I am a total failure\" (B) and feels depressed (C); disputing shows him that one failure does not make him a failure, and his mood lifts. Option d is right because Albert Ellis defined irrational beliefs by their lack of realism, logic and evidence. Exam trap: option c is reversed, because irrational beliefs are exactly the ones that can and should be disputed; and they are conscious self-statements, not repressed material, which separates REBT from psychoanalysis.", "topic": "Ellis's irrational beliefs"}, {"q": "The release of emotions is called:", "options": {"a": "Behaviour change", "b": "Insight", "c": "Mastery", "d": "Catharsis"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: the release of emotions is called catharsis. The word is Greek for \"purging\" or \"cleansing\". Aristotle (about 335 BC), in the Poetics, used it for the way a tragedy purges the audience's pity and fear. In psychology it entered through Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895), Studies on Hysteria. Breuer's patient Anna O. (Bertha Pappenheim) found that her hysterical symptoms eased when, under hypnosis, she talked through the moment each one began and expressed the feelings she had held back at the time; she called this the \"talking cure\" and \"chimney sweeping\". The procedure became known as the cathartic method. Strictly, abreaction is the reliving and discharge of the emotion tied to a particular memory, and catharsis is the release and relief that follow. Irvin Yalom (1970) later listed catharsis among the therapeutic factors of group therapy. Example: Meera has held in her grief since her grandmother died; when she finally cries and talks about it in a session, she feels lighter, an experience of catharsis. Option d is right because catharsis is the standard term for the release of pent-up emotion. Exam trap: insight is understanding, not emotional release; psychoanalysis holds that lasting change needs insight, while catharsis alone brings only short-lived relief. Also, the catharsis hypothesis for anger (that venting drains aggression) is not supported: Brad Bushman (2002) found that hitting a punching bag while thinking about an insulter increased aggression.", "topic": "Catharsis and emotional release"}, {"q": "_____ was the first to cite the brain as the source of mental disorder.", "options": {"a": "Plato", "b": "Aristotle", "c": "Hippocrates", "d": "Galen"}, "correct": "c", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is c: Hippocrates was the first to cite the brain as the source of mental disorder. Hippocrates (about 460 to 377 BC), the Greek physician called the father of medicine, broke with the supernatural model that blamed gods, demons or sin. He taught that mental illness has natural causes in the brain and body. The Hippocratic text On the Sacred Disease argued that epilepsy is not sacred at all but a disorder of the brain, and that from the brain alone arise our joys and sorrows. His humoral theory held that health is a balance of four bodily fluids, blood, yellow bile, black bile and phlegm, and that an excess of black bile causes melancholia. He described mania, melancholia and phrenitis, one of the first classifications of mental disorder, and recommended rest, diet and a calm life instead of exorcism. Option c is right because Hippocrates is the standard textbook name for the first natural, brain-based explanation of mental disorder. Exam trap: Galen (about AD 129 to 216) is the strong near-miss because he developed the humoral theory into four temperaments, but he lived about six centuries later; Plato and Aristotle were philosophers who wrote about the soul, and Aristotle placed thought in the heart. Key note: Alcmaeon of Croton (about 500 BC) placed the mind in the brain even earlier, but he is not an option and Hippocrates is the standard answer.", "topic": "Hippocrates and natural causes"}, {"q": "In which year was the first mental hospital in the USA opened?", "options": {"a": "1693", "b": "1753", "c": "1793", "d": "1547"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: the first mental hospital in the USA, in the sense of the first hospital to take in mentally ill patients, opened in 1753. The Pennsylvania Hospital in Philadelphia was founded in 1751 by Benjamin Franklin and the physician Thomas Bond and opened its doors in 1752 to 1753, admitting the sick poor and \"lunatics\", who were kept in basement cells. Benjamin Rush (1745 to 1813), later called the father of American psychiatry, worked there from 1783 and wrote the first American psychiatry textbook, Medical Inquiries and Observations upon the Diseases of the Mind (1812). Link the European dates too: Philippe Pinel (1793) unchained patients at the Bicêtre in Paris, William Tuke (1796) opened the York Retreat in England, and Bethlem (Bedlam) in London was handed to the City by Henry VIII in 1547. Option b is right because 1753 falls in the period when Pennsylvania Hospital began admitting mentally ill patients. Exam trap: 1793 and 1547 are real dates attached to other events (Philippe Pinel's reform in France and Bethlem's transfer in England), and 1693 sits right after the Salem witch trials (1692 to 1693) in colonial Massachusetts, the era of demonology rather than hospitals. Key note: the first hospital built only for the mentally ill was Eastern State Hospital, Williamsburg, Virginia, 1773; that year is not an option, so 1753 stands.", "topic": "First US mental hospital"}, {"q": "Who established the first laboratory for the experimental study of psychology?", "options": {"a": "Galton", "b": "Weber", "c": "Wundt", "d": "Jung"}, "correct": "c", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is c: Wilhelm Wundt established the first laboratory for the experimental study of psychology, at the University of Leipzig in Germany, in 1879. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) was trained in medicine and physiology and wanted psychology to become an independent experimental science. In his Leipzig laboratory, students measured reaction times, sensations and feelings under controlled conditions and reported their conscious experience through trained introspection; the first dissertation from the room, Max Friedrich's study of the time needed to apperceive a stimulus, began that winter. Wilhelm Wundt had already written Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) and soon founded the first journal of experimental psychology, Philosophische Studien (1881). This is why 1879 is taken as the birth of scientific psychology and Wilhelm Wundt is called the father of experimental psychology. Option c is right because Leipzig 1879 is the accepted date of the first formal psychology laboratory. Exam trap: William James (1875) had a small demonstration laboratory at Harvard, which is why some sources argue for him, but it was used for teaching, not original research. Francis Galton (1884) set up an anthropometric laboratory in London to measure individual differences, and Ernst Weber (1834) gave his name to Weber's law of the just noticeable difference; both were pioneers, but neither founded the first psychology laboratory.", "topic": "Wundt's Leipzig laboratory"}, {"q": "The American Psychological Association (APA) was founded in:", "options": {"a": "1892", "b": "1893", "c": "1896", "d": "1897"}, "correct": "a", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is a: the American Psychological Association (APA) was founded in July 1892. A small group of psychologists met at Clark University in Worcester, Massachusetts, at the invitation of G. Stanley Hall (1844 to 1924), who became its first president; the first annual meeting followed that December at the University of Pennsylvania. It began with a few dozen members and is now the largest association of psychologists in the world. G. Stanley Hall is worth knowing well: he earned the first American PhD in psychology (Harvard, 1878), opened the first American research laboratory at Johns Hopkins (1883), founded the American Journal of Psychology (1887), became Clark's first president and invited Sigmund Freud and Carl Jung to lecture there in 1909. Option a is right because 1892 is the standard founding year of the APA. Exam trap: the nearby years are real dates for other events. 1893 is when Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud published their preliminary communication on hysteria, and 1896 is when Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania. Also do not confuse the American Psychological Association with the American Psychiatric Association (founded in 1844), which publishes the DSM.", "topic": "Founding of the APA"}, {"q": "Who published the mechanism of hysteria in 1893?", "options": {"a": "Freud and Charcot", "b": "Freud and Breuer", "c": "Freud and Adler", "d": "Freud and Jung"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1893) published the mechanism of hysteria. Their paper, \"On the psychical mechanism of hysterical phenomena: preliminary communication\", appeared in January 1893 and argued that \"hysterics suffer mainly from reminiscences\": symptoms come from traumatic memories whose emotion was never expressed, and they disappear when the memory is recalled and the feeling discharged under hypnosis. Josef Breuer (1842 to 1925), a Viennese physician, had developed this cathartic method with his patient Anna O. (Bertha Pappenheim) between 1880 and 1882. The paper became the first chapter of their book Studies on Hysteria (1895), usually taken as the starting point of psychoanalysis. Sigmund Freud later replaced hypnosis with free association, and the two men drifted apart over his growing emphasis on sexuality. Option b is right because the 1893 preliminary communication was written jointly by Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer. Exam trap: Jean-Martin Charcot (1825 to 1893) is the strong distractor, because Sigmund Freud studied with him at the Salpêtrière in Paris (1885 to 1886) and Charcot's work on hysteria and hypnosis inspired him, but he never co-authored with Freud. Alfred Adler and Carl Jung joined Sigmund Freud's circle only later (1902 and 1907) and both broke away.", "topic": "Breuer and Freud on hysteria"}, {"q": "Morton Prince used hypnosis to treat multiple personality disorder in:", "options": {"a": "1898", "b": "1900", "c": "1902", "d": "1905"}, "correct": "d", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is d: Morton Prince's use of hypnosis to treat multiple personality is dated 1905, the year usually printed for his famous case. Morton Prince (1854 to 1929) was a Boston neurologist and an early American student of dissociation. His patient, \"Miss Christine Beauchamp\" (really Clara Norton Fowler), was a young student who seemed to switch between several distinct personalities, including a timid, saintly one and a mischievous, childlike one called \"Sally\". Using hypnosis, Morton Prince reached the different personalities, mapped how much each knew of the others and tried to fuse them into one stable self. He reported the case in The Dissociation of a Personality, the classic early account of what DSM-IV (1994) renamed dissociative identity disorder. He also founded the Journal of Abnormal Psychology in 1906. Option d is right because 1905 is the printed date for Morton Prince's hypnotic treatment and its write-up. Exam trap: 1898, when the treatment actually began, is the strongest near-miss, while 1900 and 1902 are fillers. Do not confuse Morton Prince with Pierre Janet (1889), who explained hysteria as dissociation, or with the later famous cases The Three Faces of Eve (1957) and Sybil (1973). Key note: the treatment began in 1898 and the book is usually dated 1906; 1905 is the printed answer and stands.", "topic": "Morton Prince and multiple personality"}, {"q": "Ivan Pavlov demonstrated classical conditioning in:", "options": {"a": "1999", "b": "1900", "c": "1902", "d": "1905"}, "correct": "c", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is c: Ivan Pavlov demonstrated classical conditioning around 1902. Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936) was a Russian physiologist studying digestion. He collected his dogs' saliva through a cheek tube and noticed that they began to salivate before food reached their mouths, at the sight of the dish or the sound of the attendant's footsteps. He called these \"psychic secretions\" and, with his co-workers, began studying them systematically: a neutral stimulus such as a metronome was sounded just before meat powder was given, and after several pairings the sound alone produced salivation. In this classical (Pavlovian) conditioning, food is the unconditioned stimulus, salivation to food the unconditioned response, the metronome becomes the conditioned stimulus and salivation to it the conditioned response. Ivan Pavlov reported the findings publicly in a lecture in Madrid in 1903 and won the Nobel Prize in 1904, for his work on digestion rather than conditioning. Option c is right because 1902 is the printed date for his demonstration of conditioned reflexes. Exam trap: 1905 is the year of the Binet-Simon intelligence scale and 1900 the date on Sigmund Freud's The Interpretation of Dreams, so examiners scatter famous turn-of-the-century dates; option a (1999) is a misprint-looking distractor, impossible because Ivan Pavlov died in 1936. Key note: dates vary between 1897 and 1903 across books; 1902 is the printed answer and stands.", "topic": "Pavlov's classical conditioning"}, {"q": "Social Learning Theory was developed by:", "options": {"a": "P. V. Young", "b": "Coleman", "c": "Crutchfield", "d": "Bandura"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: social learning theory was developed by Albert Bandura. Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021), a Canadian-born psychologist at Stanford University, argued that people learn not only from their own rewards and punishments but also by watching others, a process called observational learning or modelling. In the Bobo doll experiment (Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied those acts, while children who saw a calm adult did not. Albert Bandura named four processes behind modelling: attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. He also showed vicarious reinforcement: seeing a model rewarded or punished changes whether the observer performs the act, even though the behaviour may be learned either way. He set out the theory in Social Learning Theory (1977), added self-efficacy (1977) and reciprocal determinism, and renamed the whole approach social cognitive theory in 1986. Example: Riya starts holding her pen exactly the way her favourite teacher does, without ever being rewarded for it. Option d is right because social learning theory is identified above all with Albert Bandura. Exam trap: Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941) wrote an earlier book called Social Learning and Imitation, and Julian Rotter (1954) also called his expectancy theory a social learning theory, but the standard answer is Albert Bandura. The other three options are names of well-known textbook authors and researchers, placed to test whether you know who wrote what.", "topic": "Bandura's social learning theory"}, {"q": "The first intelligence test was published in:", "options": {"a": "1904", "b": "1905", "c": "1906", "d": "1907"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the first intelligence test was published in 1905. The French education ministry asked Alfred Binet (1857 to 1911) to find a way of picking out Paris schoolchildren who needed special education. With the physician Theodore Simon, he produced the Binet-Simon scale (1905): 30 items of rising difficulty that tested judgement, comprehension and reasoning, rather than the simple sensory and reaction-time measures that Francis Galton (1884) and James McKeen Cattell (1890) had tried. The 1908 revision grouped the items by the age at which typical children pass them, introducing mental age, and the 1911 revision extended the scale to adults. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet (1916) multiplied the result by 100 to give the intelligence quotient (IQ). Example: a 10-year-old, Arjun, who passes the items typical of 8-year-olds has a mental age of 8 and a ratio IQ of 80. Option b is right because 1905 is the year of the first practical intelligence test. Exam trap: 1904 is close because that is when the ministry commission was set up and when Charles Spearman proposed his g factor; 1908 and 1911 are revisions, and 1916 is the Stanford-Binet, so choose 1905 for the first test.", "topic": "Binet-Simon scale"}, {"q": "The thyroid gland controls:", "options": {"a": "Glucose absorption", "b": "Emotions", "c": "Metabolism", "d": "Sexuality"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the thyroid gland controls metabolism. The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland in the front of the neck, below the larynx. It releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), hormones made with iodine, which set the body's metabolic rate: how fast cells burn fuel and produce heat and energy. The thyroid is controlled by a feedback loop: the hypothalamus releases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), which makes the anterior pituitary release thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), which drives the thyroid; high thyroid hormone levels then switch the loop down. Too much thyroid hormone (hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease) speeds everything up: weight loss, a racing heart, heat intolerance, restlessness and anxiety. Too little (hypothyroidism) slows everything down: weight gain, tiredness, feeling cold, slowed thinking and low mood, which is why doctors check thyroid function in depression. In infancy, untreated thyroid deficiency causes congenital hypothyroidism (once called cretinism), with stunted growth and intellectual disability; iodine deficiency is a major preventable cause, which is why salt is iodised in India. Example: Riya feels low, has gained weight and always feels cold; a blood test shows low thyroxine. Option c is right because setting the metabolic rate is the thyroid's main job. Exam trap: thyroid problems change mood, which tempts students towards \"emotions\", but emotion is organised by the limbic system and the adrenal stress hormones; and blood glucose is regulated by insulin and glucagon from the pancreas, not by the thyroid.", "topic": "Thyroid gland and metabolism"}, {"q": "The ability of the eye to distinguish fine details is called:", "options": {"a": "Visual dilation", "b": "Visual acuity", "c": "Visual sensitivity", "d": "Adaptation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the ability of the eye to distinguish fine detail is called visual acuity. Acuity depends on how sharply the image is focused on the retina and on how densely the receptors are packed. It is best at the fovea, the small pit at the centre of the retina that contains only cones. Each foveal cone connects to its own bipolar and ganglion cell, so fine detail is preserved; in the periphery many rods converge on a single ganglion cell, which gains sensitivity in dim light but loses detail. Acuity is measured with the Snellen chart (Herman Snellen, 1862). Normal vision is written 6/6 in metres (20/20 in feet): you read at 6 metres what a typical eye reads at 6 metres. A score of 6/12 means you must stand at 6 metres to read what a typical eye reads at 12. Refractive errors such as myopia, hypermetropia and astigmatism lower acuity and are corrected with lenses. Example: at the eye clinic Kabir can read only the top three lines of the chart, so his acuity is poor and he needs glasses. Option b is right because acuity literally means sharpness, the power to resolve fine detail. Exam trap: sensitivity is the near-miss, but it means the ability to detect faint light (rods and dark adaptation), which trades off against acuity; and adaptation is a change in sensitivity under steady stimulation, not detail vision.", "topic": "Visual acuity"}, {"q": "A disorder called _____ results when fluid pressure builds up inside the eye and causes damage to the optic nerve.", "options": {"a": "Prosopagnosia", "b": "Achromatopsia", "c": "Dyslexia", "d": "Glaucoma"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: glaucoma is the disorder in which fluid pressure builds up inside the eye and damages the optic nerve. The front of the eye is filled with a clear fluid, the aqueous humour, which is constantly produced and drained away. If drainage is blocked, the pressure inside the eye (intraocular pressure) rises and slowly damages the fibres of the optic nerve where they leave the eye at the optic disc. The commonest form, open-angle glaucoma, is painless and gradual: peripheral vision is lost first, leading to tunnel vision, and untreated it can end in blindness. Because damaged optic nerve fibres do not regrow, the loss is permanent, so treatment (eye drops, laser or surgery to lower the pressure) aims to stop it getting worse. Glaucoma is one of the leading causes of irreversible blindness worldwide, which is why eye checks after the age of 40 often include a pressure test. Example: Arjun's father keeps bumping into door frames; tests show high eye pressure and a shrinking field of vision. Option d is right because it is the only option that is a disease of the eye itself caused by raised pressure. Exam trap: prosopagnosia and achromatopsia sound like eye diseases, but in the classic exam sense they are brain disorders with healthy eyes: prosopagnosia is face blindness after damage to the fusiform face area, and achromatopsia is cerebral colour blindness after damage to visual area V4. Dyslexia is a reading disorder, not an eye disease.", "topic": "Glaucoma and visual disorders"}, {"q": "Our general method for dealing with the environment is known as:", "options": {"a": "Intelligence", "b": "Perceptual style", "c": "Personality", "d": "Cognitive style"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: our general, habitual method of dealing with the environment, the typical way we perceive, think, remember and solve problems, is called cognitive style. A cognitive style is a preferred way of processing information, not a level of ability. Two people with the same IQ can still differ in style: one takes in the whole scene, the other picks out the parts. The best known style is field dependence versus field independence, described by Herman Witkin (1962) in Psychological Differentiation. In his rod-and-frame test a person in a dark room sets a glowing rod to vertical inside a tilted glowing frame. Field-dependent people let the frame pull the rod off true; field-independent people trust their own body sense and set it nearly upright. In the Embedded Figures Test, field-independent people find a simple shape hidden inside a complex design faster. A second classic style is reflection versus impulsivity, studied by Jerome Kagan (1965) with the Matching Familiar Figures Test: reflective children answer slowly and accurately, impulsive children answer fast and make more errors. Example: Meera reads a whole case history before forming a view, while Kabir seizes one detail and decides; they may score the same on an intelligence test, yet their cognitive styles differ. Option d is right because cognitive style is the name for a person's consistent, general approach to taking in and handling information from the environment. Exam trap: examiners put 'perceptual style' beside it. Field dependence does begin with perception, but the umbrella term in textbooks covers thinking and problem solving too, so it is cognitive style. Intelligence is how well you can think (ability); style is how you prefer to think.", "topic": "Cognitive style"}, {"q": "Most psychologists believe that aggression is:", "options": {"a": "An innate biological response to frustration", "b": "Linked to sexual drive", "c": "A learned response", "d": "A drive that builds up over time and must be released"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: most psychologists believe that aggression is largely a learned response, picked up by watching models and kept up by reinforcement. Option a, an innate response to frustration, loosely reflects the frustration-aggression hypothesis put forward at Yale by John Dollard, Leonard Doob, Neal Miller, O. Hobart Mowrer and Robert Sears (1939). Frustration means the blocking of a goal. Their original claim was double: frustration always leads to some form of aggression, and aggression always comes from frustration. The Yale group made no assumption about whether this link was innate or learned (Neal Miller, 1941). If the source of frustration is too powerful or absent, the aggression is displaced onto a safer target. Example: Arjun's boss cancels his promotion; he cannot shout at the boss, so he snaps at his younger brother at home. Neal Miller (1941) soon softened the theory: frustration creates a readiness for aggression, but it can also lead to sadness or problem solving. Leonard Berkowitz (1989) reformulated it: frustration produces aggression to the extent that it causes negative feeling, and cues such as weapons make an aggressive response more likely (the weapons effect, Berkowitz and Anthony LePage, 1967). Option c is right because most psychologists see aggression as largely learned, as Albert Bandura's social learning view holds; frustration is only one possible trigger, and it does not always lead to aggression. Exam trap: option d also sounds biological, but it is the hydraulic model of Konrad Lorenz (1966) and the death instinct of Sigmund Freud (1920), where aggressive energy builds up on its own and must be released, with or without frustration. Key note: the coaching pack keys a, but the stem is from Charles Morris and Albert Maisto's Psychology: An Introduction, whose text says 'Most psychologists believe that aggression is largely a learned behavior', in line with Albert Bandura's social learning view (Bobo doll studies, 1961; Aggression: A Social Learning Analysis, 1973). So c is used here.", "topic": "Aggression: learned or innate"}, {"q": "According to Alfred Adler, the prime motivating force in a person's life is:", "options": {"a": "Physical gratification", "b": "Existential anxiety", "c": "Striving for superiority", "d": "The need for power"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Alfred Adler held that the prime motivating force in every life is striving for superiority. Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) was a Viennese physician who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1911 and founded individual psychology. Where Freud put sex and aggression at the core, Adler put a single upward drive. His starting point was organ inferiority (Study of Organ Inferiority and Its Psychical Compensation, 1907): a weak organ pushes a person to compensate, as the Greek orator Demosthenes overcame a stammer. He then generalised the idea. Every child begins small and dependent among powerful adults, so inferiority feelings are universal, and they spur us to compensate by striving for superiority, meaning completion and perfection, not domination of others. Late in life he spoke of 'striving for perfection' and tied healthy striving to social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), concern for the community. When compensation fails the result is an inferiority complex; when it overshoots, a superiority complex. Example: Riya, the shortest girl in her class, trains hard until she becomes the school's best badminton player; Adler would call this healthy striving for superiority born of inferiority feelings. Option c is right because Adler named striving for superiority (later perfection) as the fundamental fact of life, the master motive behind all others. Exam trap: 'the need for power' tempts because an earlier version of Adler's idea (after his aggression drive of 1908) was the 'will to power', borrowed from the philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche (1883). Adler later dropped that wording for striving for superiority, which is the answer examiners expect.", "topic": "Adler's striving for superiority"}, {"q": "Skinner's theory of learning advocates:", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Operant conditioning", "c": "Observational conditioning", "d": "Insight learning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: B. F. Skinner's theory of learning advocates operant conditioning. Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), an American behaviourist, set out the theory in The Behavior of Organisms (1938). He separated respondent behaviour, which is drawn out by a stimulus (as in Pavlov's salivating dogs), from operant behaviour, which the organism emits freely and which operates on the environment. In operant conditioning the likelihood of a response changes with its consequences. Reinforcement, positive (adding something pleasant) or negative (removing something unpleasant), makes the response more likely; punishment makes it less likely. Skinner built on the law of effect of Edward Thorndike (1898, 1911), studied rats and pigeons in the operant chamber or Skinner box, and showed how schedules of reinforcement control the rate and persistence of responding. Shaping, rewarding closer and closer steps towards a target, lets complex behaviour be built up. Example: whenever Kabir finishes his homework before dinner, his mother lets him play cricket, and finishing homework early becomes more frequent. That is operant conditioning by positive reinforcement. Option b is right because Skinner's learning theory explains how consequences control voluntary, emitted behaviour, which he named operant conditioning. Exam trap: Skinner did describe classical conditioning as respondent behaviour, so option a can look plausible, but classical conditioning is the work of Ivan Pavlov (early 1900s). Observational learning belongs to Albert Bandura (1961) and insight learning to Wolfgang Kohler (1925).", "topic": "Skinner and operant conditioning"}, {"q": "What is eclecticism?", "options": {"a": "Vagueness", "b": "Adherence to a specific \"school\" of therapy", "c": "A form of \"shock therapy\"", "d": "Using techniques from many different sources"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: eclecticism means using techniques from many different sources, choosing whatever methods best fit a client instead of staying inside one school. The word comes from the Greek eklegein, to pick out or select. An eclectic therapist might use a behavioural technique for one problem, a cognitive one for another and a warm, accepting stance throughout. Surveys of practising therapists have long found 'eclectic' or 'integrative' to be the most common self-description. The best known model is the technical eclecticism of Arnold Lazarus (1932 to 2013), whose multimodal therapy assesses seven areas summed up as BASIC I.D. (Behaviour, Affect, Sensation, Imagery, Cognition, Interpersonal relationships, Drugs and biology) and borrows a technique from any school that has evidence for that area, without having to accept the school's theory. Eclecticism sits under the wider heading of psychotherapy integration, which also includes theoretical integration (fusing theories, as in the cyclical psychodynamics of Paul Wachtel, 1977) and common factors approaches. Example: Meera, a counsellor, treats a student with exam panic. She teaches breathing and graded exposure (behaviour therapy), questions catastrophic thoughts (the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck, 1979) and listens with empathy (Carl Rogers, 1951). She is working eclectically. Option d is right because eclecticism is by definition the selection and combination of methods drawn from several theories or schools. Exam trap: option b gives the exact opposite, strict loyalty to one school, which is a purist approach. Critics say careless eclecticism can become a vague grab-bag (option a), but vagueness is a criticism of it, not its definition.", "topic": "Eclecticism in psychotherapy"}, {"q": "Which of the following did Carl Rogers believe fosters a congruent self-concept?", "options": {"a": "Conditional love", "b": "Appropriate role models", "c": "Immediate-need gratification", "d": "Unconditional love"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Carl Rogers believed that unconditional love, which he called unconditional positive regard, fosters a congruent self-concept. Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), the founder of person-centred therapy, set out his theory of personality in 1959. Every child has a need for positive regard: love and acceptance from important others. If parents give love only when the child behaves in approved ways, the child learns conditions of worth, such as 'I am worthwhile only when I come first in class' or 'only when I hide my anger'. The child then denies or distorts experiences that clash with these conditions, and the self-concept drifts away from actual experience. This gap is incongruence, which Rogers saw as the root of anxiety and maladjustment. When love is unconditional, the person is valued even when a particular behaviour is disapproved. With no conditions of worth to defend, the child can let all experiences into awareness, so self-concept and experience match. That match is congruence, the mark of Rogers' fully functioning person. Example: when Arjun fails his maths test, his mother says she is unhappy about the mark but still loves him. He can accept 'I failed' without feeling worthless, so his self-concept stays congruent. Option d is right because unconditional love is exactly the condition that prevents conditions of worth and so allows a congruent self. Exam trap: option a, conditional love, is the reverse, the very thing Rogers said creates incongruence. In therapy the counsellor supplies the same ingredient as one of the three core conditions, alongside empathy and genuineness (congruence).", "topic": "Rogers: unconditional positive regard"}, {"q": "_____ is distinguished for the major stages of cognitive development: the sensorimotor, the preoperational, the concrete operational and the formal operational stage.", "options": {"a": "Jean Piaget", "b": "John Bowlby", "c": "Sigmund Freud", "d": "Harry Harlow"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: Jean Piaget is known for the four major stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational. Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) was a Swiss biologist turned psychologist who studied how children think by watching and questioning them, including his own three children. He saw children as active little scientists who build knowledge through schemes, adapting them by assimilation (fitting a new experience into an existing scheme) and accommodation (changing the scheme), driven by equilibration. The four stages come in a fixed order. Sensorimotor (birth to about 2): the infant learns through senses and actions and gains object permanence. Preoperational (about 2 to 7): symbols and language appear, but thought is egocentric and the child cannot conserve. Concrete operational (about 7 to 11): logical thinking about concrete objects, with conservation and classification. Formal operational (about 11 onwards): abstract and hypothetical reasoning. Example: four-year-old Riya thinks a tall thin glass holds more juice than a short wide one (preoperational); at eight she knows the amount is the same (concrete operational). Option a is right because these four named stages are the core of Piaget's theory, set out in books such as The Origins of Intelligence in Children (1936). Exam trap: the other three options are famous developmental figures too, so examiners rely on recall. Sigmund Freud (1905) also had stages, but psychosexual ones (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital), not cognitive ones.", "topic": "Piaget's four stages"}, {"q": "Kohlberg's theory of moral development was criticised by Gilligan primarily because:", "options": {"a": "The data did not support his conclusions", "b": "It was based on a study of boys only", "c": "It was done so long ago", "d": "His subjects consisted of urban children only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Carol Gilligan criticised Lawrence Kohlberg's theory of moral development mainly because it was based on a study of boys only. Lawrence Kohlberg (1927 to 1987) built his six-stage theory from his 1958 doctoral study at the University of Chicago, in which he interviewed 72 boys aged 10, 13 and 16 about moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma, and then followed many of them for about 20 years. No girls or women were in the original sample. When the scoring system was later used with women, many scored at stage 3 (the good boy, good girl orientation), apparently below men, who more often reached stage 4. Carol Gilligan (born 1936), who had worked with Kohlberg at Harvard, argued in In a Different Voice (1982) that this showed bias, not a real deficit. Kohlberg's scale measured an ethic of justice (rights, rules, fairness), while many women reason from an ethic of care (relationships, responsibility, not hurting anyone). In her example, 11-year-old Jake solves the Heinz dilemma like a maths problem (life outweighs property), while Amy looks for a way for Heinz and the druggist to talk it out so nobody is hurt. Example: Meera decides whether to report her friend's cheating by thinking about how her friend and her friend's family will be affected; Gilligan would call this care reasoning, not a lower stage. Option b is right because Gilligan's central complaint was the all-male sample and the male standard it produced. Exam trap: a later review by Lawrence J. Walker (1984) found few real sex differences in Kohlberg scores, so option a can sound like a fair criticism; but that point was turned against Gilligan's claims, and the question asks why she criticised Kohlberg.", "topic": "Gilligan's critique of Kohlberg"}, {"q": "The rapid increase in weight and height that occurs around the onset of puberty is known as the:", "options": {"a": "Maturation stage", "b": "Physical development stage", "c": "Adolescent growth spurt", "d": "Menarche"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the rapid gain in height and weight around the start of puberty is called the adolescent growth spurt. Puberty is the period of biological change that brings sexual maturity. It begins when the hypothalamus signals the pituitary gland, which in turn switches on the ovaries or testes to release oestrogens or testosterone. One of the first visible results is a sudden speeding up of growth. After slow, steady growth through middle childhood, a teenager may grow about 8 to 10 cm in the peak year. The spurt starts about two years earlier in girls (around 10 to 11) than in boys (around 12 to 13), which is why many girls in Class 6 or 7 are taller than the boys; the boys catch up and usually end taller. Growth is also uneven: hands and feet grow first, then arms and legs, then the trunk, which can make young teenagers look gangly for a while. Weight rises too, with more muscle in boys and more fat in girls. Example: Kabir is the shortest boy in his class at 12, then shoots up 10 cm in the year after he turns 13; that is his adolescent growth spurt. Option c is right because 'adolescent growth spurt' is the standard textbook term for the rapid rise in height and weight at the onset of puberty. Exam trap: menarche (option d) also happens at puberty and is often listed next to it, but it is the first menstrual period, a single event that usually comes after a girl's peak growth, not the growth itself.", "topic": "Adolescent growth spurt"}, {"q": "The _____ stage is characterised by an ability to use abstract concepts.", "options": {"a": "Concrete operational", "b": "Formal operational", "c": "Preoperational", "d": "Operational"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the formal operational stage is characterised by the ability to use abstract concepts. It is the fourth and final stage in the theory of Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), beginning around age 11 or 12. Piaget and Barbel Inhelder described it in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence (1958). In the earlier concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11), children think logically, but only about real objects and events. In the formal operational stage thinking frees itself from the concrete. The adolescent can reason about abstract ideas such as justice, freedom or infinity, think about possibilities that do not exist, and use hypothetico-deductive reasoning: form a hypothesis, then test it systematically. In Inhelder and Piaget's pendulum task, the formal thinker varies one factor at a time (length of string, weight, height of release) to find what controls the speed of the swing, while a younger child changes several things at once. Formal thinkers can also judge an argument by its logic alone, even when its premises are false. Example: asked 'If all elephants were pink, and Raju is an elephant, what colour is Raju?', 14-year-old Riya answers 'pink' at once, while her 8-year-old brother insists that elephants are grey. Option b is right because abstract and hypothetical thought is the defining feature of Piaget's formal operational stage. Exam trap: 'concrete operational' sounds close because both stages are 'operational' and logical, but concrete operations are tied to real, tangible things. 'Operational' alone (option d) is not a stage name at all.", "topic": "Formal operational stage"}, {"q": "A therapist who encourages clients to relate their dreams and searches for the unconscious roots of their problems is drawing from:", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Humanistic therapies", "c": "Person-centred therapy", "d": "Cognitive therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: a therapist who asks clients to report their dreams and searches for the unconscious roots of their problems is drawing on psychoanalysis. Psychoanalysis was developed by Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) in Vienna from the 1890s, after his work with Josef Breuer on hysteria (Studies on Hysteria, 1895). Its core assumption is that symptoms come from unconscious conflicts, often formed in childhood, that are kept out of awareness by repression and other defences. Treatment aims to make the unconscious conscious, so that the patient gains insight. Freud's main tools were free association (saying whatever comes to mind without censoring it), dream analysis, and the interpretation of resistance and of transference (feelings about parents shifted onto the analyst). In The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) he called the interpretation of dreams 'the royal road to the unconscious'. He separated the manifest content (the dream as remembered) from the latent content (its hidden wish), disguised by dream work such as condensation, displacement and symbolism. Example: Arjun tells his analyst he keeps dreaming of missing a train; through Arjun's associations the analyst links it to a buried fear of disappointing his father. Option a is right because dream reports and the search for unconscious causes are the trademark techniques of Freud's psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung (after his break with Freud in 1913) and Fritz Perls (Gestalt Therapy, 1951) also used dreams, but neither is offered here. Humanistic, person-centred and cognitive therapies work with present, conscious experience or thoughts, not unconscious roots.", "topic": "Psychoanalysis and dream analysis"}, {"q": "Compared to psychoanalytic therapists, humanistic therapists are more likely to emphasise:", "options": {"a": "Hidden or repressed feelings", "b": "Childhood experiences", "c": "Psychological disorders", "d": "Self-fulfilment and growth"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: compared with psychoanalytic therapists, humanistic therapists put far more weight on self-fulfilment and growth. Humanistic psychology arose in the United States in the 1950s and 1960s as a 'third force' against psychoanalysis and behaviourism. Its two leaders were Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) and Abraham Maslow (1908 to 1970). They held that people have free will and an inborn tendency to grow towards their full potential: Rogers called it the actualising tendency, and Maslow put self-actualisation at the top of his hierarchy of needs (1943, 1954). Problems arise when this growth is blocked, for example by conditions of worth. Therapy therefore looks at the client's present, conscious experience, seen from the client's own point of view, and tries to free that growth. In person-centred therapy (Rogers, 1951) the therapist offers empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness and trusts the client to find the way. A psychoanalyst, by contrast, looks backwards and inwards: to repressed feelings, unconscious conflict and early childhood, and interprets these for the patient. Example: Riya feels stuck in a career her parents chose. A psychoanalyst might explore her childhood relationship with her father; a humanistic counsellor would help her discover what she herself values and wants to become. Option d is right because self-fulfilment and personal growth are the central aims of humanistic therapy and are much less stressed in psychoanalysis. Exam trap: options a and b are real features of therapy, but they are psychoanalytic emphases; examiners reverse the comparison so that the psychoanalytic features look like humanistic ones.", "topic": "Humanistic versus psychoanalytic therapy"}, {"q": "Behaviour therapies apply learning principles to:", "options": {"a": "Identify and treat the underlying causes of dysfunction", "b": "Improve learning and insight", "c": "Eliminate the unwanted behaviour", "d": "Improve communication and social sensitivity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: behaviour therapies apply the principles of learning to eliminate unwanted behaviour (and to build desirable behaviour in its place). Behaviour therapy treats problem behaviour as learned, so it can be unlearned or replaced. It grew in the 1950s from three roots: the reciprocal inhibition and systematic desensitisation of Joseph Wolpe (1958) in South Africa, the group of Hans Eysenck (1952) at the Maudsley Hospital in London, and the operant work of B. F. Skinner (1953) in the United States. Behaviour therapists focus on the symptom itself, here and now, instead of hunting for hidden causes; Eysenck's motto was that if you get rid of the symptom you have eliminated the neurosis. A functional analysis asks what triggers the behaviour and what consequences keep it going. Classical conditioning methods include systematic desensitisation, exposure, flooding and aversion therapy; operant methods include token economies, shaping, extinction and time out; modelling, from Albert Bandura (1969), adds learning by observation. Example: Kabir is terrified of dogs. His therapist builds an anxiety hierarchy from a picture of a puppy up to stroking a real dog, and pairs each step with relaxation until the fear response is gone. Option c is right because the stated goal of behaviour therapy is to change, and usually remove, the unwanted behaviour through relearning. Exam trap: option a, treating the underlying causes of dysfunction, is the aim of psychoanalysis. Behaviourists argued that the symptoms are the disorder, and research has found little evidence that removing a symptom simply produces a new one (symptom substitution).", "topic": "Aim of behaviour therapy"}, {"q": "An example of a cognitive therapy is rational-emotive therapy, which was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Carl Rogers", "b": "Joseph Wolpe", "c": "Albert Ellis", "d": "Allen Bergin"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: rational-emotive therapy was developed by Albert Ellis. Albert Ellis (1913 to 2007), an American psychologist first trained in psychoanalysis, grew frustrated with its slowness and introduced rational therapy in 1955. It was later called rational-emotive therapy (RET) and, from 1993, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). He set it out in Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy (1962), and it is usually counted as the first of the modern cognitive therapies. Its centre is the ABC model. A is an activating event, B is the belief about it, C is the emotional and behavioural consequence. People assume A causes C, but Ellis argued that B does. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('I must succeed', 'others must treat me fairly'), awfulising, low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it') and global self-rating. The therapist then actively Disputes the belief (D), which leads to an Effective new philosophy (E). Example: Arjun fails one test (A) and feels worthless (C). Ellis would expose the belief 'I must never fail, or I am a total failure' (B) and dispute it until Arjun holds a preference instead: 'I would like to pass, but failing once does not make me worthless.' Option c is right because Ellis created RET, the classic cognitive therapy named in the stem. Exam trap: Aaron T. Beck (1967) developed the other main cognitive therapy (cognitive triad, automatic thoughts), and exam setters often swap the two. Joseph Wolpe (1958) belongs to behaviour therapy and Carl Rogers (1951) to person-centred therapy.", "topic": "Ellis and rational-emotive therapy"}, {"q": "The explicit and implicit rules that a society establishes to govern conduct are referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Norms", "b": "Culture", "c": "Morality", "d": "Conventions"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the explicit and implicit rules a society sets to govern conduct are called norms. A social norm is a shared expectation about how people should behave in a given situation. Explicit norms are stated openly, often in writing: laws, school rules, a 'No Smoking' sign. Implicit norms are unspoken, yet everyone knows them: you do not talk loudly in a temple, you face the door in a lift, and in many Indian families you touch an elder's feet. Norms make social life predictable, and breaking them brings sanctions, from a disapproving look to a fine. Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936) showed a group norm forming in the laboratory: people judging how far a still dot of light seemed to move in a dark room (the autokinetic effect) converged on a shared estimate, which they kept when later tested alone. Robert Cialdini, Raymond Reno and Carl Kallgren (1990) separated descriptive norms (what most people do) from injunctive norms (what people approve of). Sociologists also split norms into folkways (mild customs) and mores (strong moral rules), terms from William Graham Sumner (1906). Example: no written rule tells Meera to silence her phone in the cinema, but when it rings people glare; she has broken an implicit norm. Option a is right because 'norms' is the standard term for both the explicit and implicit rules that govern conduct in a society. Exam trap: culture (option b) contains norms but is much wider, the whole way of life of a group. Remember too that violating norms is what the social-norm criterion of abnormality calls deviance.", "topic": "Social norms"}, {"q": "The violation of a society's explicit and implicit norms can be viewed as not normal in the sense that the violation is:", "options": {"a": "Deviant", "b": "Distressful", "c": "Dysfunctional", "d": "A danger to one's self or to others"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: behaviour that violates a society's explicit and implicit norms is called deviant. There is no single test for abnormality, so textbooks such as Abnormal Psychology by Ronald Comer (1992) sum up the usual features as the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Deviance means behaviour that is different, extreme, unusual or bizarre. It can be judged in two ways. The statistical criterion calls abnormal whatever is rare, far from the average; its flaw is that rare talent, such as an IQ of 150, would also count. The social-norm criterion calls abnormal whatever breaks a society's norms, both the explicit rules (laws) and the implicit ones (unspoken customs). Its flaw is cultural relativity: norms differ across cultures and eras. A possession trance at a temple festival may be accepted custom in one setting and a symptom in another, and homosexuality was listed as a disorder in DSM-II (1968) until 1973. So deviance alone never proves a disorder. Example: Kabir walks to college in winter wearing only shorts and talks loudly to himself on the bus. People stare because he is violating norms; his behaviour is deviant, but a clinician would still ask whether he is distressed, dysfunctional or dangerous. Option a is right because 'deviant' is the term for breaking a society's norms. Exam trap: the other three Ds are also features of abnormality, so all four options look correct. The test is which D the stem defines: norm violation is deviance, suffering is distress, inability to cope with daily life is dysfunction, and risk of harm is danger.", "topic": "Four Ds: deviance"}, {"q": "Which aspect of the definition of abnormality includes unhappiness?", "options": {"a": "Distress", "b": "Deviance", "c": "Dysfunction", "d": "Danger to self or others"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the part of the definition of abnormality that includes unhappiness is distress. Distress refers to unpleasant, upsetting feelings: sadness, unhappiness, anxiety, fear, guilt or emotional pain. It is one of the four Ds that Ronald Comer (1992) uses to sum up abnormality, along with deviance, dysfunction and danger. The personal distress criterion treats behaviour as abnormal when it causes the person great suffering. It matters because many disorders, such as depression and the anxiety disorders, are felt mainly as suffering, and DSM-5 (2013) builds 'clinically significant distress or impairment' into its definition of a mental disorder. But distress is neither necessary nor sufficient on its own. Some people with a disorder feel little distress: a person in a manic episode may feel wonderful, and someone with antisocial personality disorder may feel no guilt. And distress can be normal: grief after a parent's death, or nerves before a viva, is not a disorder. That is why clinicians weigh all four Ds together and ask whether the distress is out of proportion to its cause and lasting. Example: Riya has felt deeply unhappy and hopeless for two months since moving to a new city, cries most evenings and has lost interest in everything. Her unhappiness is the distress feature of abnormality. Option a is right because unhappiness is a form of personal suffering, which is exactly what the distress criterion describes. Exam trap: dysfunction can sound close, because unhappy people often function worse, but dysfunction refers to impaired daily functioning (work, study, relationships, self-care), not to the feeling itself.", "topic": "Four Ds: distress"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not true regarding people with a mental illness?", "options": {"a": "They frequently are in considerable distress", "b": "Society views such people as in need of help", "c": "They usually pose a danger to themselves or others", "d": "They usually have behaviour that is in some way different"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the statement that is not true is that people with a mental illness usually pose a danger to themselves or others. Danger is one of the four Ds of abnormality described by Ronald Comer (1992), with deviance, distress and dysfunction, but it applies to the fewest people. Most people with a mental illness are never violent. The MacArthur Violence Risk Assessment Study (Henry Steadman and colleagues, 1998) found that discharged psychiatric patients without substance abuse were no more violent than other people in their neighbourhoods; the strongest predictors of violence were substance misuse and past violence, not diagnosis itself. People with severe mental illness are in fact more often victims of violence than perpetrators. Suicide risk is real and raised in depression, but it still affects a minority. The image of the 'dangerous madman' comes largely from news stories and films, and it feeds stigma, which makes people delay seeking help. The other three statements are broadly true: many people with mental illness are in real distress; society, at least in principle, views them as in need of help and treatment; and their behaviour usually differs in some way from what is typical, which is the deviance feature. Example: Arjun's neighbours are frightened when they learn he has schizophrenia, though he has never harmed anyone and is far more likely to be bullied than to hurt someone. Option c is right because 'usually dangerous' is a well-known myth, contradicted by research on violence and mental illness. Exam trap: the word 'usually' is what matters; danger is a real criterion of abnormality, but it fits only a small number of people. Key note: The printed answer in the coaching copy is a, but distress is common in mental illness and the statement that is clearly not true is c, so c is used here.", "topic": "Myth of dangerousness"}, {"q": "Dementia is a condition that is characterised by:", "options": {"a": "General paresis", "b": "Physical ailments", "c": "A general intellectual decline", "d": "The loss of contact with reality"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: dementia is characterised by a general intellectual decline. Dementia is an acquired, usually progressive loss of cognitive abilities from a previously higher level, severe enough to interfere with independence in daily life. It is not present from birth (that is intellectual disability), and it is not a normal part of ageing. Memory is usually hit first, especially for recent events, followed by language (aphasia), skilled movement (apraxia), recognition (agnosia), judgement, abstract thinking and planning. In DSM-5 (2013) the term dementia was replaced by major neurocognitive disorder: a significant decline in one or more of six domains (complex attention, executive function, learning and memory, language, perceptual-motor function and social cognition) that costs independence; mild neurocognitive disorder is the milder form. The commonest cause is Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), with amyloid plaques and tau tangles; others include vascular, Lewy body and frontotemporal dementia. Unlike delirium, dementia develops slowly and consciousness stays clear. Example: Meera's grandfather, a retired teacher, first forgot recent conversations, then lost his way in his own lane and could no longer manage his pension papers; his doctor diagnosed Alzheimer's dementia. Option c is right because a broad, acquired decline in intellect and cognition is the defining feature of dementia. Exam trap: general paresis (option a) did cause dementia in the 1800s, but it is neurosyphilis, one specific cause, not the definition. Loss of contact with reality (option d) is psychosis, the hallmark of schizophrenia.", "topic": "Dementia"}, {"q": "The ancient Greek physicians characterised hysteria as:", "options": {"a": "Euphoric behaviour", "b": "Uncontrollable sobbing", "c": "An inability to control emotional responses", "d": "A physical ailment without any physical cause"}, "correct": "d", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is d: the ancient Greek physicians described hysteria as a physical ailment without any real physical cause. Hysteria meant dramatic bodily symptoms, such as fits, paralysis, loss of voice, a choking feeling or blindness, with no disease that could account for them. The word comes from the Greek hystera, the uterus. Hippocrates (born about 460 BC) and his followers, and later Plato in the Timaeus, thought the womb could wander through a woman's body, pressing on other organs and causing symptoms; treatments included marriage, pregnancy and scents to coax the womb back into place. So hysteria was pictured as a bodily illness, but with no actual damage to the part that seemed affected, and it was long thought to strike only women. In the 1800s Jean-Martin Charcot (1825 to 1893) at the Salpêtrière showed that hysterical symptoms could be produced and removed by hypnosis, and Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895) explained them as psychological conflict 'converted' into bodily form. Today the label is gone: DSM-5 (2013) calls these presentations conversion disorder, or functional neurological symptom disorder, and they occur in men too. Example: after a frightening accident, Riya suddenly cannot move her right arm, yet every scan and nerve test is normal; the Greeks would have called this hysteria. Option d is right because it captures the defining feature: real-seeming physical complaints without an underlying bodily disease. Exam trap: options b and c describe 'hysterical' in today's everyday sense, being emotionally out of control, which is not the classical medical meaning. Key note: the answer means physical complaints with no real bodily disease, which is how the term hysteria came to be used. Strictly, the Greeks did name a physical cause, the wandering womb, but no actual organ damage was ever found, so d stands.", "topic": "Hysteria in ancient Greece"}, {"q": "What model of mental illness did most people hold during the Middle Ages?", "options": {"a": "The moral model", "b": "The medical model", "c": "The psychogenic model", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is d: none of these, because in the Middle Ages most people held a supernatural, demonological model of mental illness, which is not among the options. Demonology is the belief that abnormal behaviour is caused by evil spirits, the devil or witchcraft. It is much older than the Middle Ages: some Stone Age skulls show trephining, holes cut perhaps to let a spirit out. Hippocrates (born about 460 BC) and later Galen (born AD 129) had replaced it with natural, bodily causes. But in medieval Europe, after the fall of Rome, the Church dominated thought and explanations swung back to possession and sin. Treatment was exorcism: prayer, holy water and at times starving or flogging to drive the demon out. There were outbreaks of mass madness, such as tarantism in Italy and St Vitus's dance. Late in the period came the witch hunts: the Malleus Maleficarum (1486) of Heinrich Kramer and Jacob Sprenger was a manual for finding and punishing witches, and many disturbed people were among the accused. The physician Johann Weyer (1563) argued that many so-called witches were mentally ill, an early step back towards a medical view. Modern historians point out that medieval views were mixed and that the worst witch hunts came in the 1500s and 1600s, but textbooks sum up the era as demonological. Example: in a medieval village, if Meera's grandmother began hearing voices, the priest would be called to cast out the demon, not a physician. Option d is right because the dominant medieval view, demonology, is not the moral, medical or psychogenic model. Exam trap: the medical model sounds historical because the Greeks used it, but the Middle Ages largely abandoned it; the moral model is moral treatment, which came much later with Philippe Pinel (1793).", "topic": "Demonology in the Middle Ages"}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled by the:", "options": {"a": "Medulla", "b": "Frontal lobe", "c": "Spinal cord", "d": "Hypothalamus"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan or the knee jerk when a doctor taps just below the kneecap. The pathway it follows is the reflex arc, analysed in detail by Charles Sherrington in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System (1906), work that later earned him a Nobel Prize (1932). In a typical spinal reflex the signal runs from a receptor in the skin, along a sensory (afferent) neuron entering the spinal cord through the dorsal root, to an interneuron in the grey matter of the cord, then out along a motor (efferent) neuron through the ventral root to the effector muscle. Because the loop closes inside the spinal cord, the brain is not needed to start the response, so the hand moves before you feel the pain; the message reaches the brain a moment later. The withdrawal reflex is polysynaptic (it uses interneurons), while the knee jerk, a stretch reflex, is monosynaptic: the sensory neuron synapses directly on the motor neuron. Example: Arjun touches a hot tawa while making rotis and jerks his hand away, and only then says 'ouch'. His spinal cord ran the reflex before his brain registered the pain. Option c is right because most simple body reflexes are organised in the spinal cord, without needing the brain. Exam trap: the medulla does run some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, swallowing and vomiting, and controls breathing and heart rate, so option a is tempting; but the word 'usually' points to the spinal cord, which handles the classic limb reflexes.", "topic": "Spinal reflex arc"}, {"q": "A part of the brain that sends 'alert' signals to higher centres of the brain in response to incoming messages is:", "options": {"a": "Limbic system", "b": "Reticular formation", "c": "Amygdala", "d": "Hippocampus"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the reticular formation sends 'alert' signals to the higher centres of the brain in response to incoming messages. The reticular formation is a net-like web of about 100 small nuclei running through the core of the brainstem, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain (reticulum means 'little net'). Its ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), receives branches from almost every sensory pathway and projects through the thalamus to the whole cortex. It controls arousal, wakefulness and attention, and acts as a filter, letting important signals rouse the cortex while screening out dull, repetitive ones. That is why a parent can sleep through traffic noise yet wake at the baby's cry. Damage to it can cause coma. The classic study came from Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949): electrically stimulating the reticular formation of drowsy or lightly anaesthetised cats switched their EEG from slow waves to the fast pattern of alertness. Hans Eysenck (1967) later built his biological theory of extraversion on differences in this arousal system. Example: Kabir is half asleep in a lecture, but when the teacher says his name, his reticular formation alerts his cortex and he sits up at once. Option b is right because arousing the higher brain in response to sensory input is the defining job of the reticular formation and its activating system. Exam trap: the limbic system, amygdala and hippocampus are real structures listed together to confuse. The amygdala handles fear and emotion, the hippocampus forms new memories, and the limbic system is the emotional circuit as a whole, not the alerting system.", "topic": "Reticular activating system"}, {"q": "Perceptual constancies are:", "options": {"a": "Illusions in which we perceive something that does not correspond to the sensory information", "b": "Confusing to an individual rather than helping him determine what really exists", "c": "Likely inborn and not subject to learning", "d": "An aid in perceiving a stable and consistent world"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: perceptual constancies help us perceive a stable and consistent world. Perceptual constancy is the tendency to see objects as keeping the same size, shape, brightness and colour even though the image they cast on the retina keeps changing. Without it, the world would seem to shrink, stretch and flicker every time we moved. Size constancy: a friend walking away casts a smaller and smaller retinal image, yet she does not seem to shrink, because the brain scales retinal size by perceived distance (size-distance invariance, shown in Emmert's law by Emil Emmert, 1881). Shape constancy: an opening door projects a trapezium on the retina, yet we see a rectangle. Brightness (lightness) constancy: coal in bright sunlight reflects more light than white paper in shade, yet still looks black, because we judge the ratio of an object's light to that of its surroundings. Colour constancy: a red apple looks red under yellow kitchen light and in blue daylight. Some constancy is present early in infancy (Tom Bower, 1966, found size constancy in babies of 6 to 8 weeks) and experience refines it. Example: as Meera watches her brother's bus drive away, its image on her retina gets tiny, but she still sees a full-sized bus moving farther off, not a toy bus. Option d is right because the function of perceptual constancies is to give a stable, consistent picture of a world whose sensory input is always changing. Exam trap: option a describes illusions. Constancy can produce illusions when distance cues mislead (the Ponzo and moon illusions), but constancy itself is an aid to accurate perception, not an error.", "topic": "Perceptual constancy"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a subdivision of the autonomic nervous system?", "options": {"a": "Both the sympathetic and the parasympathetic nervous system", "b": "Only the sympathetic nervous system", "c": "Only the parasympathetic nervous system", "d": "Brain and spinal cord"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the autonomic nervous system has two main subdivisions, the sympathetic and the parasympathetic nervous systems. The nervous system first divides into the central nervous system (CNS), the brain and spinal cord, and the peripheral nervous system (PNS), all the nerves outside them. The PNS has two parts: the somatic nervous system, which carries sensations in and commands out to the skeletal muscles under voluntary control, and the autonomic nervous system (ANS), which runs the internal organs, smooth muscle, heart and glands largely automatically. John Newport Langley (1898) coined the term autonomic and later named the parasympathetic division. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action, the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915): the heart speeds up, pupils widen, airways open, digestion slows and the liver releases glucose. Its fibres leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord. The parasympathetic division does the reverse, rest and digest: it slows the heart, narrows the pupils and restarts digestion, conserving energy. Its fibres leave the brainstem (mainly through the vagus nerve) and the sacral cord. The two usually work in opposition to keep balance (homeostasis). Example: Riya's heart pounds as she walks into her viva (sympathetic); afterwards, over lunch, her heart rate drops and she digests her food calmly (parasympathetic). Option a is right because the ANS is made up of both divisions, not just one. Exam trap: options b and c each name only one half, a trap of incompleteness. Option d, the brain and spinal cord, is the central nervous system, not part of the ANS at all. Many books add the enteric nervous system of the gut wall as a third autonomic division.", "topic": "Divisions of the ANS"}, {"q": "Reports on the sexual behaviour of men were published by:", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Alfred Kinsey", "c": "Anna Freud", "d": "Karen Horney"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: the reports on the sexual behaviour of men were published by Alfred Kinsey. Alfred Charles Kinsey (1948) was an American zoologist at Indiana University who had spent years classifying gall wasps before he turned to human sexuality. With Wardell Pomeroy and Clyde Martin he published Sexual Behavior in the Human Male (1948), based on detailed face-to-face interviews (sex histories) with thousands of men. A companion volume, Sexual Behavior in the Human Female (1953), added Paul Gebhard as an author. Together they are called the Kinsey Reports. Their main finding was that ordinary sexual behaviour was far more varied than people admitted. Same-sex experience, for example, was common and graded, which Kinsey captured in his 0 to 6 scale, from exclusively heterosexual (0) to exclusively homosexual (6). Critics pointed out that his samples were volunteers rather than random, so some percentages may be inflated, but the reports changed public debate and made sex research a science. Option b is right because the stem asks for published reports on men's sexual behaviour, which were survey data, not a theory. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud (1905) also wrote about sexuality, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, so examiners use him as bait; but Freud built a theory of infantile sexuality from clinical cases and never surveyed men's sexual behaviour. Do not confuse Kinsey's interview surveys with William Masters and Virginia Johnson (1966), who observed the physiology of the sexual response cycle in the laboratory.", "topic": "Kinsey Reports on sexual behaviour"}, {"q": "_____ proposed rational emotive therapy.", "options": {"a": "Wolpe", "b": "Ellis", "c": "Rogers", "d": "Sartre"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: rational emotive therapy was proposed by Albert Ellis. Albert Ellis (1955) was an American clinical psychologist who trained in psychoanalysis but found that insight alone rarely made his clients better. Drawing on Stoic philosophy, especially the first-century Stoic Epictetus (people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them), he began 'rational therapy' in 1955. It was later renamed rational emotive therapy (RET) and, in 1993, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), to recognise its behavioural homework. His classic book is Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy (1962), and REBT is usually called the first cognitive-behavioural therapy. Its core is the ABC model: A is the activating event, B the belief about it, and C the emotional and behavioural consequence. Common sense says A causes C; Ellis said B is the main cause. Therapy adds D, disputing the irrational belief, and E, the new effective belief. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('I must', 'others should'), awfulising and low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it'). Example: Arjun scores poorly in a mock test (A). He believes 'I must never fail, or I am worthless' (B), so he feels depressed and stops studying (C). His therapist disputes the 'must' until Arjun holds a flexible preference: 'I would like to do well, but one test does not define me.' Option b is right because RET and its ABC model are Ellis's own creation. Exam trap: examiners swap Ellis with Aaron Beck (1967), who developed cognitive therapy with the cognitive triad and automatic thoughts. Ellis disputes beliefs directly and forcefully; Beck tests thoughts collaboratively, like a scientist.", "topic": "Ellis's rational emotive therapy"}, {"q": "_____ published the cognitive theory and therapy for depression.", "options": {"a": "Aaron Beck", "b": "William Beck", "c": "Carl Beck", "d": "John Beck"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the cognitive theory and therapy for depression was published by Aaron Beck. Aaron Temkin Beck (1967) was a psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania, trained in psychoanalysis. In the late 1950s he tried to show that depressed patients' dreams contained anger turned inward, as Freudian theory predicted. Instead he found themes of loss and defeat, and he noticed a stream of quick, self-critical automatic thoughts that came just before his patients' sad feelings. He set out the theory in Depression (1967) and the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979), written with John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery. He also built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961), still a standard self-report scale. The model has three levels: core beliefs or schemas formed in childhood ('I am unlovable'), intermediate beliefs or rules ('If I am not perfect, I will be rejected'), and automatic thoughts. At its centre is the cognitive triad: a negative view of the self, the world and the future. Cognitive distortions keep it going, such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, arbitrary inference and personalisation. Example: Meera's friend forgets to call her back. Her automatic thoughts are 'Nobody really likes me' (self), 'people always let me down' (world) and 'it will always be like this' (future). In therapy she tests each thought against the evidence, a method Beck called collaborative empiricism. Option a is right because Aaron Beck is the only real cognitive therapist among the options. Exam trap: the other three are invented Becks built on the famous surname. Do not confuse Aaron with his daughter Judith S. Beck (Cognitive Therapy: Basics and Beyond, 1995), and remember the triad is self, world and future, not past, present and future.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive therapy of depression"}, {"q": "_____ developed a social adjustment rating scale to measure life stress.", "options": {"a": "Joseph Wolpe", "b": "Carl Rogers", "c": "Thomas Szasz", "d": "Holmes and Rahe"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: the life-stress scale was developed by Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe. Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe (1967), two American psychiatrists at the University of Washington, published the Social Readjustment Rating Scale (SRRS); the stem's 'social adjustment rating scale' is a slightly garbled version of that name. Their idea was that any life change, good or bad, forces a person to readjust, and that piling up readjustment strains the body and raises the risk of illness. The scale lists 43 life events, each weighted in life change units (LCU). Death of a spouse is the anchor at 100; divorce scores 73, marriage 50, being fired at work 47, a change in residence 20 and a minor violation of the law 11. Even pleasant events such as a holiday or Christmas count, because change itself is the stressor. A person adds up the units for the past year, and high totals (above about 300) were linked with a greater chance of illness in the following period. Example: in one year Kabir marries (50), loses his job (47) and moves house (20), so he scores 117 LCU, even though one of those events was happy. Critics note that the scale ignores how a person appraises an event, and Allen Kanner, Richard Lazarus and colleagues (1981) found that daily hassles predict symptoms better than major events. Option d is right because Holmes and Rahe built the best-known scale of life events. Exam trap: Thomas Szasz (1961) is tempting because he wrote on psychiatry and society, but his book was The Myth of Mental Illness and he built no stress scale; also, the SRRS measures major life events, not daily hassles.", "topic": "Holmes and Rahe life events scale"}, {"q": "Who is the founder of Positive Psychology?", "options": {"a": "Howard Gardner", "b": "Steven Pinker", "c": "Bandura", "d": "Seligman"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the founder of positive psychology is Martin Seligman. Martin Elias Peter Seligman (1998) launched positive psychology in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association in 1998. He argued that psychology had spent decades repairing damage (depression, anxiety, disorder) and had neglected what makes life worth living. Positive psychology is the scientific study of positive experiences (happiness, hope, flow), positive traits (character strengths and virtues) and positive institutions (families, schools and workplaces that help people thrive). With Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (2000), the researcher of flow, he edited a special issue of American Psychologist that set out the field. Seligman later proposed the PERMA model of well-being (2011): positive emotion, engagement, relationships, meaning and accomplishment. Earlier in his career he had discovered learned helplessness (1967) in dogs given inescapable shocks, and he moved from there to learned optimism. Example: instead of only treating Riya's exam anxiety, a positive psychologist would also build her strengths, gratitude and sense of meaning. Option d is right because Seligman founded the movement and named its agenda. Exam trap: Abraham Maslow (1954) had used the phrase 'positive psychology' as a chapter title in Motivation and Personality, and humanistic psychology was a forerunner, but the founder of the modern field is Seligman.", "topic": "Founder of positive psychology"}, {"q": "The theory of multiple intelligences was propounded by:", "options": {"a": "Seligman", "b": "Bandura", "c": "Gardner", "d": "(left blank in the paper)"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the theory of multiple intelligences was propounded by Howard Gardner. Howard Gardner (1983), a Harvard developmental psychologist, set out the theory in Frames of Mind. He argued that intelligence is not one general ability (the g of Charles Spearman, 1904) but several relatively independent intelligences. His original seven were linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal. He added naturalist intelligence in 1999 and considered, without fully accepting, an existential intelligence. Gardner did not rely on factor analysis. He used other evidence: abilities that are lost on their own after brain damage, savants and prodigies who excel in one area, separate developmental histories and distinct symbol systems. Critics reply that most of these abilities correlate with one another and with g, and that some are better called talents. Example: Meera struggles with algebra but sings perfectly in tune and reads her friends' moods well; Gardner would say her musical and interpersonal intelligences are strong even though her logical-mathematical intelligence is weaker. Option c is right because Frames of Mind and the multiple intelligences are Gardner's work. Exam trap: Louis Thurstone (1938) also listed seven abilities, his primary mental abilities, but he found them by factor analysis; and Robert Sternberg's (1985) triarchic theory has three parts (analytical, creative, practical), not multiple intelligences.", "topic": "Gardner's multiple intelligences"}, {"q": "The study of language and mind is related to:", "options": {"a": "Gessmann", "b": "Bovver", "c": "Pinker", "d": "Chomsky"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the study of language and mind is linked with Noam Chomsky. Noam Chomsky (1957) is an American linguist at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology whose Syntactic Structures (1957) began transformational generative grammar. His book Language and Mind (1968) argued that language is a window on the mind: the rules of grammar are mental structures, and the capacity to acquire them is innate. In his 1959 review of Burrhus Frederic Skinner's Verbal Behavior (1957) he argued that reinforcement cannot explain language, because children produce sentences they have never heard and make errors no adult models ('I goed'). He proposed a language acquisition device (LAD) holding a universal grammar, the principles shared by all human languages, and argued from the 'poverty of the stimulus': the speech children hear is too sparse to teach them the grammar they reach. This attack on behaviourism helped launch the cognitive revolution. Example: three-year-old Kabir says 'two foots', a form no one taught him; Chomsky would say he is applying a rule his mind has extracted. Option d is right because Language and Mind is the title of Chomsky's book and the idea behind his whole programme. Exam trap: Steven Pinker (1994) is the near-miss, because his The Language Instinct also defends an innate language faculty. Key note: Pinker also works on language and mind, but 'Language and Mind' is Chomsky's book title, so d stands.", "topic": "Chomsky, language and mind"}, {"q": "_____ is called the Marco Polo of Neuroscience.", "options": {"a": "Kaplan", "b": "Ramachandran", "c": "Kuppuswamy", "d": "Udai Pareek"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the 'Marco Polo of neuroscience' is V. S. Ramachandran. Vilayanur Subramanian Ramachandran (born 1951) is an Indian-born neuroscientist who directs the Center for Brain and Cognition at the University of California, San Diego. The biologist Richard Dawkins (The Selfish Gene, 1976) called him 'a latter-day Marco Polo', because, like the explorer, he travels into strange territory, here the odd syndromes of the brain, and comes back with simple, striking findings. His best-known work is on phantom limbs. In the 1990s he touched the face of an arm amputee with a cotton bud, and the man felt the touch on specific fingers of the missing hand. Ramachandran explained this as cortical reorganisation: in the somatosensory cortex the face area lies next to the hand area, so it invades the hand's unused territory. He then invented the mirror box, in which the reflection of the intact hand makes the brain 'see' the phantom move, easing phantom pain. He also studied synaesthesia and Capgras delusion and wrote Phantoms in the Brain (1998, with Sandra Blakeslee). Example: after losing his left arm in an accident, Arjun feels his missing thumb whenever his cheek is touched; this is the remapping Ramachandran described. Option b is right because the Marco Polo description belongs to him. Exam trap: the other three are names Indian students also meet in exams, but in very different fields: psychiatry textbooks or sex therapy (Kaplan), an Indian socio-economic status scale (Kuppuswamy) and organisational psychology (Udai Pareek).", "topic": "Ramachandran, Marco Polo of neuroscience"}, {"q": "In which year was the first department of psychology in India established?", "options": {"a": "1912", "b": "1916", "c": "1922", "d": "1924"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: the first department of psychology in India was set up in 1916. It was the Department of Experimental Psychology at the University of Calcutta, founded under the vice-chancellor Sir Ashutosh Mukherjee with support from the philosopher Brajendranath Seal. It was led by Narendra Nath Sengupta (1889 to 1944), who had trained at Harvard under Hugo Munsterberg (brought to Harvard in 1892), himself a student of Wilhelm Wundt (1879, the Leipzig laboratory). So the department began with experimental, laboratory psychology on the German and American model. Sengupta set up India's first psychology laboratory there in 1915, and the separate department followed. Girindrasekhar Bose, later the father of psychoanalysis in India, joined it and later headed it. Sengupta is called the father of modern psychology in India. Other universities followed: Mysore began psychology in 1924 under M. V. Gopalaswamy, then Madras and Pune. Option b is right because 1916 fits the founding of the Calcutta department and no other option comes near it. Exam trap: 1924 is the Mysore date (and close to the founding of the Indian Psychological Association), and 1922 is when Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society; examiners swap these dates. Key note: sources differ, many giving 1915 (the year the laboratory opened) and some 1916; 1916 is the printed answer and the closest option, so it stands.", "topic": "First psychology department in India"}, {"q": "The Indian Psychological Association was formed in:", "options": {"a": "1918", "b": "1922", "c": "1924", "d": "1928"}, "correct": "c", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Indian Psychological Association was formed in 1924. It grew out of the Indian Science Congress, and its first president was Narendra Nath Sengupta (1889 to 1944), head of the Calcutta department and the father of modern psychology in India. The association gave Indian psychologists a national body for meetings and publication, and its Indian Journal of Psychology appeared in the mid-1920s (1925 or 1926, sources differ), closely linked with Girindrasekhar Bose (1887 to 1953) of Calcutta. Keep the early Indian timeline in order: Calcutta laboratory 1915, Calcutta department 1916, Indian Psychoanalytical Society (Bose) 1922, Mysore department (M. V. Gopalaswamy) 1924, Indian Psychological Association 1924 to 1925, and its journal soon after. A useful memory hook is that Calcutta leads every early first: the first laboratory, the first department, the first psychoanalytical society and the first national association all came from the same small group of Calcutta scholars. Option c is right because the association was founded in the mid-1920s and 1924 is the only option in that window. Exam trap: 1922 is the Indian Psychoanalytical Society, a different body with a similar-sounding name, while 1918 and 1928 are near-miss years on either side of the true period. Key note: some sources, including many textbooks, date the founding to 1925; 1924 is the printed answer and stands.", "topic": "Indian Psychological Association founding"}, {"q": "The first edition of DSM was published by the APA in:", "options": {"a": "1951", "b": "1952", "c": "1953", "d": "1966"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: the first edition of the DSM was published in 1952. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM) is the classification of mental disorders published by the American Psychiatric Association (APA). DSM-I (1952) was a slim manual of about 130 pages listing 106 disorders. It grew out of a US Army scheme from the Second World War and used the psychobiological language of Adolf Meyer (dominant in American psychiatry in the early twentieth century), so many disorders were called 'reactions', such as 'schizophrenic reaction'. Like DSM-II, it gave short prose descriptions rather than explicit criteria, so two psychiatrists often disagreed on a diagnosis. The editions to learn in order are DSM-I 1952, DSM-II 1968, DSM-III 1980 (explicit criteria and the multiaxial system, under Robert Spitzer), DSM-III-R 1987, DSM-IV 1994, DSM-IV-TR 2000, DSM-5 2013 and DSM-5-TR 2022. Its international partner is the World Health Organization's ICD, whose sixth revision (ICD-6, 1948) was the first to include a section on mental disorders. Option b is right because DSM-I appeared in 1952. Exam trap: the APA here is the American Psychiatric Association, not the American Psychological Association; and examiners place one-year near misses (1951, 1953) beside the true date to test exact recall.", "topic": "DSM editions: DSM-I 1952"}, {"q": "DSM-5 was published in:", "options": {"a": "2013", "b": "2015", "c": "2017", "d": "2010"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: DSM-5 was published in 2013. The American Psychiatric Association released it on 18 May 2013 at its annual meeting in San Francisco, nineteen years after DSM-IV (1994). It dropped Roman numerals for an Arabic 5 so that later revisions could be numbered more simply, and the first full text revision, DSM-5-TR, followed in 2022. The main changes were these. DSM-5 abolished the five-axis system, listing clinical, personality and medical conditions together. It ordered its chapters roughly along the lifespan, starting with neurodevelopmental disorders. It merged autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder and related diagnoses into one autism spectrum disorder; moved obsessive-compulsive disorder and posttraumatic stress disorder out of the anxiety chapter into chapters of their own; removed the bereavement exclusion for major depression; dropped the classic subtypes of schizophrenia; and replaced the Global Assessment of Functioning with the WHO Disability Assessment Schedule. Example: a child called Riya might have been diagnosed with Asperger's disorder under DSM-IV-TR; under DSM-5 she would receive autism spectrum disorder with a severity level. Option a is right because 2013 is the publication year. Exam trap: the draft criteria were posted online for public comment in 2010, so 2010 looks plausible; and DSM-5-TR (2022) is a text revision, not a new edition.", "topic": "DSM-5 publication year"}, {"q": "Who coined the term neurosis?", "options": {"a": "Esquirol", "b": "William Cullen", "c": "Benjamin Rush", "d": "William Sargant"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: the term neurosis was coined by William Cullen. William Cullen (1769) was a Scottish physician and teacher at the University of Edinburgh, one of the leading medical schools of the eighteenth century. In his classification of diseases, Synopsis Nosologiae Methodicae (1769), he used 'neurosis' for disorders of sense and motion caused by a general affection of the nervous system, without fever or any local disease of an organ. His class was very broad: it took in conditions now seen as neurological, such as palsies and epilepsy, as well as melancholia and hysteria. Later, Sigmund Freud (1895) narrowed the word to non-psychotic disorders arising from unconscious conflict, with insight preserved, as in his 'anxiety neurosis' (1895). DSM-III (1980) dropped neurosis as a formal category because it implied Freud's theory, though ICD-10 kept it in the block 'neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders'. Option b is right because Cullen introduced the word in 1769. Exam trap: neurosis came first; the word psychosis came about seventy years later and was popularised by Ernst von Feuchtersleben (1845). Examiners also offer famous early psychiatrists such as Benjamin Rush (1812) or Jean-Etienne Dominique Esquirol (1838), who are known for other firsts.", "topic": "Cullen coined neurosis"}, {"q": "Who coined the term psychiatry?", "options": {"a": "Johann Weyer", "b": "Johann Reil", "c": "John Conolly", "d": "Philippe Pinel"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: the term psychiatry was coined by Johann Christian Reil. Johann Christian Reil (1808) was a German physician and anatomist at Halle who introduced the word 'Psychiaterie' in 1808, soon shortened to 'Psychiatrie'. It joins the Greek psyche (soul or mind) and iatreia (healing), so it means the medical treatment of the mind. Reil argued that physicians, not jailers or clergy, should treat mental illness, and that they should use psychological methods. His earlier book, Rhapsodies on the Application of Psychological Methods of Cure to Mental Disturbances (1803), proposed humane and even theatrical psychological treatment in asylums. Anatomists also know him for the insula, once called the 'island of Reil'. Psychiatry then grew into a medical speciality during the nineteenth century. Option b is right because Reil introduced the word itself. Exam trap: examiners mix up 'coined the term' with 'father of' titles. Johann Weyer (1563) is called by some the father of modern psychiatry for arguing that many accused witches were mentally ill; Philippe Pinel (1793) is famous for unchaining patients at Bicêtre; and John Conolly (1839) championed non-restraint in England. None of them coined the word.", "topic": "Reil coined psychiatry"}, {"q": "Who was the founder of Clinical Psychology?", "options": {"a": "Lightner Witmer", "b": "Galton", "c": "William James", "d": "Griesinger"}, "correct": "a", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is a: the founder of clinical psychology is Lightner Witmer. Lightner Witmer (1896) was an American psychologist who earned his PhD under Wilhelm Wundt (1879, the Leipzig laboratory) and then taught at the University of Pennsylvania. In 1896 he opened the first psychological clinic there, mainly for children with learning and behaviour problems referred by schools. His first case was a fourteen-year-old 'chronic bad speller'. Instead of labelling the boy, Witmer tested his vision, memory and reading, found poor eyesight behind part of the problem, and helped him through corrected vision and individual teaching. In 1907 he founded the journal The Psychological Clinic and in it first used the term 'clinical psychology' in print. His model, careful assessment first and then a practical remedy, is the root of the profession. Option a is right because Witmer both founded the first clinic and named the field. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud (psychoanalysis, 1896) and Emil Kraepelin (psychiatric classification, 1883) are often offered as 'fathers' of clinical work, but they founded psychoanalysis and modern psychiatric classification; and do not swap Witmer's clinic (1896) with Wundt's laboratory (1879).", "topic": "Witmer founds clinical psychology"}, {"q": "Who coined the terms 'catatonia', 'cyclothymia', 'verbigeration' and 'symptom complex'?", "options": {"a": "K. L. Kahlbaum", "b": "Kraepelin", "c": "Hecker", "d": "Esquirol"}, "correct": "a", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is a: these terms were coined by Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum. Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum (1874) was a German psychiatrist who ran a private asylum at Görlitz. He argued that mental illnesses should be classified not by single symptoms but by the whole clinical picture over time: the pattern of symptoms that occur together (the 'symptom complex') and the course and outcome of the illness. In his monograph on catatonia (1874), which he called 'tension insanity', he described patients with stupor, mutism, rigid posturing, waxy flexibility and bouts of excitement. Verbigeration, the repetition of meaningless words or phrases, comes from his descriptions of these patients, and cyclothymia, a milder cyclic mood disorder, is credited to him and his colleague Ewald Hecker. Example: Kabir sits motionless for hours, keeps his arm wherever the doctor places it, and repeats one phrase again and again; these are stupor, waxy flexibility and verbigeration. Option a is right because catatonia, verbigeration, cyclothymia and the symptom-complex approach all go back to Kahlbaum. Exam trap: Ewald Hecker (1871) described hebephrenia, and Emil Kraepelin (1893) later folded both catatonia and hebephrenia into dementia praecox, so both names sit close to Kahlbaum in history questions. Today catatonia is a specifier that can occur in schizophrenia, mood disorders and medical conditions.", "topic": "Kahlbaum and catatonia"}, {"q": "Who coined the term dementia praecox?", "options": {"a": "Emil Kraepelin", "b": "Morel", "c": "Wernicke", "d": "Karl Kleist"}, "correct": "a", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is a: the term dementia praecox is credited to Emil Kraepelin. Emil Kraepelin (1893) was a German psychiatrist, once a student of Wilhelm Wundt (1879), who classified mental disorders by their symptoms, course and outcome. In the fourth edition of his textbook (1893) he used the Latin dementia praecox, 'premature dementia', and in the sixth edition (1899) he made it one of psychiatry's two great psychoses. He united Ewald Hecker's hebephrenia (1871), Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum's catatonia (1874) and paranoid forms into one illness that began in youth and ran a deteriorating course. He set it against manic-depressive insanity, which came in episodes with recovery in between. This 'Kraepelinian dichotomy' still divides schizophrenia from bipolar disorder in DSM and ICD. Eugen Bleuler (1908, 1911) later renamed the illness schizophrenia, because not every patient deteriorated or fell ill young. Option a is right because Kraepelin made dementia praecox a diagnosis and is the standard answer for the term. Exam trap: Benedict Morel's (1850s) French démence précoce sounds identical, so examiners use Morel as bait, and some exams key him. Key note: Benedict Morel used the French 'démence précoce' (1852 to 1860) for young people with early deterioration; Arnold Pick used the Latin 'dementia praecox' in 1891, and Emil Kraepelin made it a diagnosis (1893, then 1899). Exams that want 'coined the French term' key Morel; the printed answer is Kraepelin and stands.", "topic": "Kraepelin and dementia praecox"}, {"q": "Who coined the term hypnotism?", "options": {"a": "James Braid", "b": "John Elliotson", "c": "J. M. Charcot", "d": "Cohen"}, "correct": "a", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is a: the term hypnotism was coined by James Braid. James Braid (1843) was a Scottish surgeon working in Manchester. In 1841 he watched a stage demonstration of mesmerism and set out to explain it without any magnetic fluid. He found he could induce the trance simply by having a person stare fixedly at a bright object, which he put down to fatigue of the eyes and nervous system. In Neurypnology (1843) he called it neuro-hypnotism, soon shortened to hypnotism, from the Greek hypnos, sleep. He later realised that hypnosis is not sleep but intense concentration on a single idea, and tried to rename it 'monoideism', but the word hypnotism had stuck. Before him, Franz Anton Mesmer (1770s) had claimed an 'animal magnetism' that a French royal commission in 1784 put down to imagination. After him, Jean-Martin Charcot (1880s) treated hypnotisability as a sign of hysteria at the Salpêtrière, and the Nancy school of Ambroise-Auguste Liebeault and Hippolyte Bernheim (1880s) showed it was normal suggestion. Option a is right because Braid introduced both the word and a natural, non-magnetic account of the trance. Exam trap: mesmerism is named after Mesmer, but hypnotism was named by Braid; John Elliotson (1830s and 1840s), who used mesmerism in London, and Charcot, who used hypnosis on hysteria, are the usual near-miss names.", "topic": "Braid coined hypnotism"}, {"q": "Who is the father of psychoanalysis?", "options": {"a": "Bernheim", "b": "Sigmund Freud", "c": "Adler", "d": "Jung"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: the father of psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud. Sigmund Freud (1896) was an Austrian neurologist in Vienna who first used the word 'psychoanalysis' in 1896. His path ran through hysteria. He studied with Jean-Martin Charcot in Paris (1885 to 1886), saw Hippolyte Bernheim's work on suggestion at Nancy (1889), and with Josef Breuer published Studies on Hysteria (1895), built around Anna O. and the 'talking cure'. Freud then dropped hypnosis for free association, in which the patient says whatever comes to mind, and added dream analysis (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), the analysis of slips, and the handling of resistance and transference. Psychoanalysis is at once a theory of personality (unconscious conflict among id, ego and superego, and the psychosexual stages), a method of investigation and a form of therapy. Example: when Meera, secretly angry at her sister, says 'I'm glad you're leaving' when she meant 'sad', a psychoanalyst would read the slip as a hidden wish breaking through. Option b is right because Freud created the theory, the method and the name. Exam trap: Alfred Adler and Carl Jung were early followers who broke away (1911 and 1913) to found individual psychology and analytical psychology; they are not founders of psychoanalysis. In India the father of psychoanalysis is Girindrasekhar Bose (Indian Psychoanalytical Society, 1922), a separate question.", "topic": "Freud, father of psychoanalysis"}, {"q": "The one who coined the term schizophrenia is:", "options": {"a": "Eugen Bleuler", "b": "Emil Kraepelin", "c": "Morel", "d": "Esquirol"}, "correct": "a", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is a: the term schizophrenia was coined by Eugen Bleuler. Eugen Bleuler (1908) was a Swiss psychiatrist who directed the Burghölzli hospital in Zurich, where Carl Jung (from 1900) trained under him. He introduced 'schizophrenia' in a 1908 lecture and developed it in his 1911 monograph, Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. He rejected the older name of Emil Kraepelin (1893) because many patients did not deteriorate, and some fell ill later in life. The new word comes from the Greek schizein (to split) and phren (mind): a splitting of the links between thought, emotion and behaviour, not a split or multiple personality. Bleuler's fundamental symptoms, the four As, were loosened associations, blunted or incongruous affect, ambivalence and autism (withdrawal into an inner world); he coined ambivalence and used autism in this sense. He counted hallucinations and delusions as only accessory symptoms, because they occur in other illnesses too. He also spoke of a 'group' of schizophrenias, an early version of today's spectrum idea. Option a is right because Bleuler coined the word. Exam trap: 'split mind' is often wrongly read as dissociative identity disorder; and keep the chain of names in order: Benedict Morel's démence précoce (1850s), Kraepelin's dementia praecox (1893, 1899), Bleuler's schizophrenia (1908, 1911).", "topic": "Bleuler coined schizophrenia"}, {"q": "Who pioneered insulin coma therapy for schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Ugo Cerletti", "b": "Manfred Sakel", "c": "Meduna", "d": "Wagner"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: insulin coma therapy for schizophrenia was pioneered by Manfred Sakel. Manfred Sakel (1933) was an Austrian physician who had used insulin to ease morphine withdrawal and noticed that low blood sugar seemed to calm some patients. In Vienna in 1933 he began giving patients with schizophrenia large doses of insulin to produce a hypoglycaemic coma, repeated day after day for weeks, ending each coma with glucose. Insulin coma therapy spread across the world in the 1930s and 1940s, even though it was dangerous and demanding to run, and some patients died. Later work suggested that much of its benefit came from the intense nursing care and attention the patients received rather than from the coma itself, and after chlorpromazine (1952) it was abandoned. It was one of a cluster of 1930s 'shock' treatments. Ladislas von Meduna (1934) induced convulsions with camphor and then Metrazol (cardiazol), wrongly believing that epilepsy and schizophrenia were antagonistic, and Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini (1938) introduced electroconvulsive therapy. Option b is right because the insulin coma method is Sakel's. Exam trap: the three shock therapies are easily swapped: insulin coma is Sakel (1933), chemical convulsions Meduna (1934) and electrical convulsions Cerletti and Bini (1938). Julius Wagner-Jauregg's (1917) malaria fever therapy, for general paresis rather than schizophrenia, came before all three.", "topic": "Sakel's insulin coma therapy"}, {"q": "Who is the founder of the electrical apparatus for producing convulsions?", "options": {"a": "Von Meduna", "b": "Ugo Cerletti and Bini", "c": "Scribonius Largus", "d": "Egas Moniz"}, "correct": "b", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is b: the electrical method of producing convulsions was founded by Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini. Ugo Cerletti (1938), a neuropsychiatrist at the University of Rome, and his assistant Lucio Bini (1938) gave the first electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) in April 1938. Convulsions were already being used to treat schizophrenia after Ladislas von Meduna (1934) induced them with camphor and Metrazol, but drug-induced fits were frightening and unpredictable. Cerletti, who had studied electrically induced seizures in animals, wanted a cleaner and more controllable method; Bini built the apparatus, which passed a brief current between electrodes placed on the temples. Their first patient, a man with schizophrenia, improved after a series of treatments, and ECT spread quickly. Modern ECT is 'modified': it is given under general anaesthesia with a muscle relaxant, so there is no violent convulsion, and it is used mainly for severe or treatment-resistant depression, catatonia and some cases of mania. India's Mental Healthcare Act (2017) forbids unmodified ECT. Option b is right because Cerletti and Bini invented the electrical apparatus and method. Exam trap: Meduna is the tempting option because he started convulsive therapy, but his convulsions were chemical, not electrical; and Scribonius Largus (about AD 46) used electricity from a torpedo fish for headache, not to produce convulsions.", "topic": "Cerletti and Bini's ECT"}, {"q": "Illnesses that seem to result from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called:", "options": {"a": "Hysterical", "b": "Psychosomatic", "c": "Somatic", "d": "Conversion disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: illnesses that result from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called psychosomatic. The word joins the Greek psyche (mind) and soma (body) and is usually traced to the German psychiatrist Johann Christian August Heinroth (1818). A psychosomatic disorder is a real physical illness, with real tissue damage or measurable bodily change, whose onset or course is shaped by psychological factors such as stress, emotion and coping. Franz Alexander (1950), in Psychosomatic Medicine, named seven classic examples, the 'holy seven': peptic ulcer, essential hypertension, bronchial asthma, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis, neurodermatitis and thyrotoxicosis. His idea that each is caused by a specific unconscious conflict failed (most ulcers proved to be caused by the bacterium Helicobacter pylori), but the broader point survived: stress acts on the body through the autonomic nervous system, stress hormones and the immune system. George Engel's (1977) biopsychosocial model extended this to all illness. DSM-II called these psychophysiological disorders, and DSM-5 uses 'psychological factors affecting other medical conditions'. Example: Arjun has genuine asthma, and his attacks become more frequent and severe in the weeks before his board examinations. Option b is right because only psychosomatic names an illness produced by mind and body acting together. Exam trap: psychosomatic does not mean imaginary. In conversion disorder (the old hysteria) there is no physical disease behind the symptom, whereas in a psychosomatic illness the disease is real.", "topic": "Psychosomatic disorders"}, {"q": "Freud published The Interpretation of Dreams in:", "options": {"a": "1900", "b": "1898", "c": "1902", "d": "1904"}, "correct": "a", "section": "history", "explanation": "The answer is a: Sigmund Freud published The Interpretation of Dreams in 1900. Sigmund Freud (1900) wrote the book, Die Traumdeutung, after years of analysing his own dreams, a self-analysis he pursued after his father's death in 1896. It was actually printed in November 1899, but the publisher, Franz Deuticke, dated it 1900 so that it would belong to the new century; exams therefore use 1900. Only about 600 copies were printed and they took years to sell, yet the book became the founding text of psychoanalysis. Freud called the interpretation of dreams 'the royal road to a knowledge of the unconscious'. Each dream is the disguised fulfilment of a repressed wish. The manifest content is the dream as remembered; the latent content is its hidden meaning; and the dream work turns one into the other by condensation, displacement, symbolisation and secondary revision. Example: Riya dreams that she keeps missing a train; a Freudian would look past the train (manifest content) for the hidden wish it disguises (latent content). Option a is right because 1900 is the publication date printed on the book. Exam trap: the near-miss years belong to other events: 1902 is when Freud's Wednesday Psychological Society began meeting, and 1904 is when The Psychopathology of Everyday Life appeared as a book (it was first published in a journal in 1901).", "topic": "Freud's Interpretation of Dreams"}, {"q": "In which way is behaviour different from mental processes?", "options": {"a": "Introspection", "b": "Observation", "c": "Research", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: behaviour differs from mental processes in that behaviour can be observed directly. Psychology today is defined as the scientific study of behaviour and mental processes. Behaviour is anything an organism does that others can see, hear or record: speaking, walking, blushing, a change in heart rate on a monitor. It is overt, public and open to observation and measurement. Mental processes, such as perceiving, thinking, remembering, feeling and dreaming, are internal and covert. No one else can watch them directly; they are known only through the person's own report (introspection) or inferred from behaviour, such as reaction times, test answers or brain activity. This difference shaped the history of psychology. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) and Edward Bradford Titchener (1898) studied conscious experience through trained introspection, but reports varied between laboratories. John B. Watson (1913) therefore argued in his behaviourist manifesto that psychology should study only observable behaviour. After the cognitive revolution of the 1950s, psychologists brought mental processes back, studying them through careful observation of behaviour. Example: when Riya bites her nails before an exam, the nail-biting is behaviour her friend can observe; her worry that she will forget the answers is a mental process only she experiences directly. Option b is right because observability is what separates behaviour from mental processes. Exam trap: introspection is tempting because it is linked with the mind, but it is the route to mental processes, so it marks them off from behaviour rather than describing behaviour.", "topic": "Behaviour versus mental processes"}, {"q": "Who is particularly interested in studying patterns of behaviour, beliefs and values which are shared by people?", "options": {"a": "Social psychologist", "b": "Experimental psychologist", "c": "Clinical psychologist", "d": "Psychoanalyst"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: a social psychologist is the specialist most interested in the patterns of behaviour, beliefs and values that people share. Social psychology is the scientific study of how individuals think, feel and act in relation to other people. Gordon Allport (1954) gave the classic definition: it studies how a person's thoughts, feelings and behaviour are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of others. Shared patterns of behaviour, beliefs and values are what social psychologists call norms, attitudes and culture. They ask how such patterns form, how they spread through a group and why people go along with them. The classic evidence is Muzafer Sherif's (1936) autokinetic study: in a dark room a still point of light seems to move, and when people judged its movement together, their separate estimates soon converged on a shared group norm, which they kept even when later tested alone. Think of Riya, who joins a new college where everyone dresses formally on Mondays; within weeks she does too, without anyone telling her. A social psychologist would study how that shared habit arose and why she adopted it. Subfields such as cultural and cross-cultural psychology push the same question across whole societies. Option a is right because only social psychology takes shared, group-level patterns of thought and action as its central subject. Exam trap: the wording about shared beliefs and values sounds like a definition of culture, and students drift towards a familiar individual-focused field; clinical psychology and psychoanalysis study one person's problems or unconscious, and experimental psychology is defined by its method, not by this topic.", "topic": "Scope of social psychology"}, {"q": "Which area of the hypothalamus is responsible for satiety behaviour?", "options": {"a": "Lateral hypothalamus", "b": "Ventromedial hypothalamus", "c": "Hyper Phobia", "d": "Antidiuretic hormone"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) is the classic satiety centre, the area that signals 'stop eating'. The hypothalamus is a small structure below the thalamus that controls hunger, thirst, body temperature, sexual behaviour and the pituitary gland. The classic evidence came from lesion studies in rats. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) found that destroying the VMH made rats overeat (hyperphagia) and become very obese. Then the Indian physiologist Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck (1951) found the opposite: lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (LH) made rats stop eating (aphagia) and drinking (adipsia), sometimes until they starved. Eliot Stellar (1954) joined the two findings in the dual-centre theory: the LH is the hunger or feeding centre and the VMH is the satiety centre. Picture Kabir finishing a large lunch: as blood glucose and gut signals rise, the satiety system tells him he is full; a rat with a damaged VMH never gets that stop message and keeps eating. Option b is right because damage to the VMH removes satiety and produces overeating, which shows that its normal job is to end a meal. Exam trap: students reverse the pair. Lateral lesion, lose appetite; ventromedial lesion, very hungry. Key note: the modern view is more complex, because VMH lesions also raise insulin and fat storage, and signals such as leptin from fat cells act on the arcuate and paraventricular nuclei, so no single centre controls eating. The classic dual-centre answer still stands, and the VMH is the satiety area.", "topic": "Hypothalamus and satiety"}, {"q": "Which perspective of psychology focuses on determining the extent to which psychological characteristics such as memory and intelligence are influenced by heredity?", "options": {"a": "Neuroscience", "b": "Biogenic", "c": "Cognitive", "d": "Socio-cultural"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the neuroscience (biological) perspective asks how far traits such as memory and intelligence are shaped by heredity. This perspective explains behaviour through the body: genes, the brain, hormones and evolution. Its branch called behaviour genetics measures heredity's share by comparing people who differ in how many genes they share. Sir Francis Galton (1869, Hereditary Genius) first argued that ability runs in families and later turned to twins. Identical (monozygotic) twins share all their genes; fraternal (dizygotic) twins share about half, like ordinary siblings. If identical twins are much more alike in IQ than fraternal twins, heredity matters. Adoption studies add a second test: are adopted children more like their birth parents or the parents who raised them? The Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart, led by Thomas Bouchard (from 1979), found that identical twins raised in different homes were still strikingly alike in intelligence and personality. The result is a heritability estimate, the share of variation in a group that is linked to genetic differences; it describes groups, not one person. For example, Meera and her identical twin score within a few points of each other on an IQ test, while Meera and her fraternal brother differ more. Option a is right because the stem follows Benjamin Lahey's account of the neuroscience perspective, the biological view that also asks how far psychological characteristics are inherited. Exam trap: 'biogenic' sounds like the right biological word, but it means 'of biological origin' and is the abnormal psychology label for biological causes of disorders (as against psychogenic causes), not one of the perspectives of psychology. Key note: the coaching pack keys b (biogenic), but the stem follows Benjamin Lahey's Psychology: An Introduction, where neuroscientists 'also seek to determine the extent to which our psychological characteristics, such as intelligence and emotional stability, are influenced by heredity', so a is used here.", "topic": "Biological perspective and heredity"}, {"q": "Which of the following relates to disorders in the comprehension or production of speech?", "options": {"a": "Dysphasia", "b": "Aphasia", "c": "Broca's aphasia", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: dysphasia, aphasia and Broca's aphasia all describe disorders in understanding or producing speech, so 'All of these' fits. Aphasia is a loss of language caused by brain damage, usually a stroke in the left hemisphere. It is a language problem, not a problem with the muscles of the mouth or with hearing. Dysphasia is the partial or milder form; many clinicians, especially in Britain, use the two words almost as synonyms. The classic evidence comes from two nineteenth-century cases. Paul Broca (1861) studied a patient nicknamed 'Tan', who could say little more than that one syllable but understood speech; at autopsy he had damage in the left inferior frontal lobe, now called Broca's area. Broca's aphasia is therefore expressive or non-fluent aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with fairly good comprehension. Carl Wernicke (1874) then described the reverse: fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension, after damage to the left posterior superior temporal lobe (Wernicke's area). Imagine Arjun's grandfather after a stroke: he understands his family but can only say 'want... tea... now', which is Broca's aphasia. Option d is right because each term names a disorder of the comprehension or production of speech: aphasia (the general loss), dysphasia (the partial form) and Broca's aphasia (a specific production type). Exam trap: look-alike words. Dysphagia (with a g) is difficulty in swallowing, dysarthria is slurred speech from weak or poorly controlled speech muscles, and dyslexia is a specific difficulty with reading; none of them belongs to the aphasia family.", "topic": "Aphasia and dysphasia"}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is involved in planning, decision-making and some aspects of language?", "options": {"a": "Frontal", "b": "Parietal", "c": "Temporal", "d": "Occipital"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the frontal lobe handles planning, decision-making and some aspects of language. The frontal lobe is the largest lobe, lying in front of the central sulcus. Its front part, the prefrontal cortex, runs the executive functions: setting goals, planning steps, weighing choices, holding information in working memory and holding back impulses. Just in front of the central sulcus lies the primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus), which commands voluntary movement. In the left hemisphere of most people, the inferior frontal gyrus contains Broca's area, named after Paul Broca (1861), which produces fluent speech; that is the 'some aspects of language' in the stem. The classic case is the railway foreman Phineas Gage (1848): an iron rod passed through his frontal lobes and he survived, but his doctor John Harlow (1868) reported that he became impulsive, rude and unable to keep to his plans. For example, when Kabir draws up a revision timetable, chooses which subject to study first and resists checking his phone, his prefrontal cortex is doing the work. Option a is right because planning, decision-making and speech production (Broca's area) all sit in the frontal lobe. Exam trap: language is spread over two lobes. Speech production (Broca's area) is frontal, while understanding speech (Wernicke's area) is temporal, so the word 'language' can tempt students to choose the temporal lobe.", "topic": "Frontal lobe functions"}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is involved in listening?", "options": {"a": "Frontal", "b": "Parietal", "c": "Temporal", "d": "Occipital"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the temporal lobe is the lobe most involved in listening (hearing). The temporal lobe lies below the lateral (Sylvian) fissure, on each side of the head near the ears. Sound waves are turned into nerve signals in the cochlea of the inner ear; the auditory nerve carries them through the brainstem and the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus (Brodmann areas 41 and 42), on the upper surface of the temporal lobe. This cortex is tonotopic: like a piano keyboard, neighbouring cells respond to neighbouring pitches. Next to it, in the left hemisphere, lies Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874), which lets us understand spoken words; damage there leaves hearing intact but makes speech meaningless to the listener. Classic evidence came from Wilder Penfield (from the 1930s), who electrically stimulated the temporal lobes of awake patients during epilepsy surgery; many reported hearing sounds, music or voices. When Meera listens to a raga and recognises it, her temporal lobes hear the notes and match them to memory, since the inner part of the temporal lobe (the hippocampus) also supports memory. Option c is right because the primary auditory cortex and the area for understanding speech both lie in the temporal lobe. Exam trap: this paper runs a series of lobe questions with the same four options. Use a simple map: frontal plans and speaks, parietal feels touch and space, temporal hears and remembers, occipital sees.", "topic": "Temporal lobe and hearing"}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is involved in vision?", "options": {"a": "Frontal", "b": "Parietal", "c": "Temporal", "d": "Occipital"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, is the lobe devoted to vision. Light is turned into nerve signals by the rods and cones of the retina. The two optic nerves meet at the optic chiasm, where fibres from the inner half of each retina cross, so each side of the brain receives the opposite half of the visual field. The signals pass to the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus and then, through the optic radiations, to the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) along the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe. Classic evidence came from David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959), who recorded single cells in the cat's visual cortex and found feature detectors that fire to lines and edges at particular angles; they shared the Nobel Prize in 1981. From V1, information flows along two streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe and a dorsal 'where' stream into the parietal lobe. If Riya is hit hard on the back of her head, she may 'see stars' or lose part of her visual field even though her eyes are unharmed. Option d is right because the primary visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: the eyes are at the front of the head but vision is processed at the back; and recognising faces and objects (the ventral stream) uses the temporal lobe, so do not confuse seeing with recognising.", "topic": "Occipital lobe and vision"}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter plays an important role in the experience of pleasure?", "options": {"a": "Serotonin", "b": "Dopamine", "c": "Norepinephrine", "d": "Acetylcholine"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: dopamine plays the central role in the experience of pleasure and reward. Dopamine is a neurotransmitter made mainly in two midbrain areas: the substantia nigra, which serves movement, and the ventral tegmental area (VTA). The VTA sends fibres along the mesolimbic pathway to the nucleus accumbens, the brain's reward hub, and on to the prefrontal cortex. When something good happens, such as food when hungry, praise, sex, music or a drug, dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens rises and marks the experience as worth repeating. The classic evidence came from James Olds and Peter Milner (1954), who found that rats would press a lever again and again, even ignoring food, to receive electrical stimulation in this reward system. Addictive drugs act on the same circuit: cocaine and amphetamine raise dopamine at the synapse. For example, when Arjun's phone buzzes with a 'like' on his post, a small burst of dopamine makes checking the phone feel rewarding, so he checks again. Kent Berridge and Terry Robinson (1998) argued that dopamine drives 'wanting' (the urge to get a reward) more than 'liking' itself, but textbooks still link dopamine with pleasure and reward. Option b is right because dopamine in the mesolimbic reward pathway is the neurotransmitter most tied to pleasure. Exam trap: serotonin is often called the 'happy chemical' because SSRIs treat depression, but serotonin regulates mood, sleep and impulse control; reward and pleasure belong to dopamine.", "topic": "Dopamine and reward"}, {"q": "The hormone cortisol produced by the adrenal gland is responsible for:", "options": {"a": "Reducing the body activity to take rest", "b": "Activating the body activity to prepare for a stressful situation", "c": "Balancing the homeostasis", "d": "Planning and decision making"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: cortisol activates the body to prepare for and cope with a stressful situation. The body has two stress systems. The fast one is the sympathetic nervous system with the adrenal medulla, which releases adrenaline within seconds (the fight-or-flight response named by Walter Cannon, 1915). The slower one is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), the pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and ACTH makes the adrenal cortex release cortisol. Cortisol raises blood glucose by breaking down stored protein and fat, keeps energy flowing through a long stressor, and damps inflammation and immune activity. Hans Selye (1936, 1956) described the general adaptation syndrome: alarm, resistance and exhaustion. Cortisol carries the body through the resistance stage, but if stress never ends, high cortisol harms memory, sleep and immunity. Cortisol also feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary to switch the axis off once the threat passes. Suppose Meera wakes on the morning of her entrance exam: her cortisol peaks, giving her the energy and alertness to face the paper. Option b is right because the main job of cortisol is to mobilise energy and keep the body ready for a stressor. Exam trap: cortisol and adrenaline both come from the adrenal gland, but adrenaline comes from the inner medulla (fast) and cortisol from the outer cortex (slow); a question naming the adrenal cortex or the HPA axis means cortisol.", "topic": "Cortisol and the HPA axis"}, {"q": "The process through which a learned response stops occurring due to the non-availability of reinforcement is called:", "options": {"a": "Generalisation", "b": "Punishment", "c": "Spontaneous recovery", "d": "Extinction"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: extinction is the process by which a learned response stops occurring because reinforcement is no longer given. In classical conditioning, Ivan Pavlov (1927, Conditioned Reflexes) found that once a dog salivated to a bell paired with food, ringing the bell again and again without food made the salivation fade. Pavlov called the food the 'reinforcement', so extinction there means the conditioned stimulus (CS) appears without the unconditioned stimulus (UCS). In operant conditioning, B. F. Skinner (1938) showed that a rat stops pressing a lever once presses no longer bring food. Often there is first an extinction burst, a short spurt of harder or more varied responding, before the behaviour fades. Extinction is not forgetting: it is new learning that the cue or action no longer predicts the reward. The proof is spontaneous recovery, when the extinguished response suddenly returns after a rest. Example: Riya's dog begs at the dinner table; when the whole family stops giving it scraps, it begs harder for a few days (the burst), then gives up. Option d is right because the stem describes a learned response dying out when its reinforcement is withdrawn. Exam trap: punishment also makes behaviour decrease, but punishment adds an unpleasant consequence or removes a pleasant one; extinction simply stops delivering the reinforcer that kept the behaviour going.", "topic": "Extinction in conditioning"}, {"q": "When a teacher appreciates his students every time they ask questions, with the hope that this will enable them to handle more technical questions in the future, he is using:", "options": {"a": "Desensitisation", "b": "Shaping", "c": "Modelling", "d": "Reinforcement"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: praising students every time they ask a question is reinforcement, specifically positive reinforcement. Reinforcement is any consequence that makes a behaviour more likely to happen again. B. F. Skinner (1938, The Behavior of Organisms) built operant conditioning on this idea, extending Edward Thorndike's (1898) law of effect, which said that responses followed by satisfying results are stamped in. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, marks, a sweet); negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache goes after a tablet), and both increase behaviour. When every single response is rewarded, the schedule is continuous reinforcement, also written FR-1. It builds a new habit fastest, though the habit also dies out fastest if the praise stops; partial schedules (fixed or variable ratio and interval) build behaviour that lasts longer. Here the teacher praises Kabir and his classmates each time they raise a question, so asking questions becomes more frequent, and the teacher hopes the confidence will carry over to harder, technical questions later. Option d is right because the teacher strengthens question-asking by following it, every time, with a pleasant consequence. Exam trap: the phrase about handling more technical questions in future tempts students towards shaping, but the stem never describes rewarding closer and closer steps; and reinforcement always increases behaviour, so do not confuse it with punishment. Key note: praising every question is continuous positive reinforcement. Shaping would mean reinforcing closer and closer approximations to the harder questions; the stem hints at that goal but describes praising every question, so d stands.", "topic": "Positive reinforcement"}, {"q": "In classical conditioning, the organism creates an association between:", "options": {"a": "Two situations", "b": "Two consequences", "c": "Two stimuli", "d": "Two responses"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in classical conditioning the organism learns an association between two stimuli. Ivan Pavlov (1927, Conditioned Reflexes) discovered it while studying digestion in dogs. Food in the mouth is an unconditioned stimulus (UCS) that triggers salivation, an unconditioned response (UCR), with no learning needed. A bell is at first a neutral stimulus. After the bell is repeatedly sounded just before the food, the bell alone makes the dog salivate: the bell is now a conditioned stimulus (CS) and the salivation a conditioned response (CR). What has been learned is that one stimulus (the bell) signals another (the food). Robert Rescorla (1968) sharpened this: the CS must predict the UCS, not merely occur near it; if food comes just as often without the bell, little conditioning happens. Timing matters too: the CS should come shortly before the UCS (forward conditioning). For example, Meera once ate prawns and fell ill; now even the smell of prawns makes her feel sick, because she linked the smell (CS) with the illness the food caused (UCS). Option c is right because classical conditioning pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus until the first comes to predict the second. Exam trap: operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner, 1938) links a response with its consequence, so options about responses or consequences belong to the operant side. Remember: classical is stimulus with stimulus, before the behaviour; operant is behaviour with consequence, after the behaviour.", "topic": "Classical conditioning basics"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of self-actualised people according to Maslow?", "options": {"a": "They live creatively and fully using their potentials", "b": "They have efficient perceptions of reality, self and others", "c": "They are sensitive to the fake and dishonest", "d": "They feel ashamed of their negative emotions"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: self-actualised people do not feel ashamed of their negative emotions, so this is not one of their characteristics. Abraham Maslow (1943, 1954) placed self-actualisation at the top of his hierarchy of needs: becoming everything one is capable of becoming. To describe it he studied people he judged psychologically healthy and fulfilled, including historical figures such as Abraham Lincoln, Eleanor Roosevelt and Albert Einstein, first in a 1950 study and later in Motivation and Personality (1970 edition). He listed about fifteen traits. They have an efficient perception of reality: they see people and events accurately and are quick to detect the fake, the phony and the dishonest. They accept themselves, others and nature, including their own flaws and weaknesses, without guilt, anxiety or shame. They are spontaneous, problem-centred rather than self-centred and autonomous; they need privacy, keep a fresh appreciation of ordinary things, have peak experiences, form few but deep friendships, hold democratic attitudes, enjoy an unhostile philosophical humour, are creative and resist blind conformity to their culture. Imagine Kabir, a mature doctor, who feels angry at an unfair decision: he admits the anger openly and deals with it, rather than hiding it in shame. Option d is right because self-acceptance, the opposite of shame about one's feelings, is a core feature of self-actualisers. Exam trap: option c sounds like a suspicious or negative trait, so students pick it as the odd one out, but sensitivity to the fake and dishonest is Maslow's own description of their efficient perception of reality.", "topic": "Maslow's self-actualisers"}, {"q": "According to Freud, the process through which individuals release their excessive aggressive energy is called:", "options": {"a": "Dream work", "b": "Psychoanalysis", "c": "Catharsis", "d": "Slips of tongue"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: catharsis is the process through which, in the Freudian view, a person releases pent-up emotional and aggressive energy. The word is Greek for purging or cleansing. Aristotle (Poetics, about 335 BC) used it for the way watching a tragedy purges the audience of pity and fear. Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria, 1895) took it into therapy as the cathartic method: in hypnotic states, Breuer's patient Anna O. (Bertha Pappenheim) recalled the painful events behind her symptoms and expressed the feelings tied to them, and the symptoms eased. She called it the 'talking cure' and 'chimney sweeping'. Freud's energy model held that blocked emotion, including aggressive energy, builds up like steam in a boiler and must be discharged, either safely or in symptoms; this hydraulic view was later echoed by Konrad Lorenz (1966). Arjun, furious after a quarrel, pounds a pillow, expecting to let off steam: that is the catharsis idea. Research does not support venting aggression, though: Brad Bushman (2002) found that people who hit a punching bag while thinking about someone who had provoked them became more aggressive, not less. Option c is right because catharsis is the Freudian name for discharging bottled-up emotional energy. Exam trap: catharsis is a release of emotion, while sublimation is redirecting an impulse into a socially valued activity such as sport or art; both deal with drives, but only catharsis means letting the feeling out.", "topic": "Catharsis"}, {"q": "A layer of nerve cells at the back of the eye which converts light waves into electrical impulses is the:", "options": {"a": "Fovea", "b": "Retina", "c": "Cornea", "d": "Iris"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the retina is the layer of nerve cells at the back of the eye that converts light into electrical impulses. Light enters through the cornea, passes through the pupil (the opening in the iris) and is focused by the lens onto the retina. The retina is really part of the brain, pushed outwards during development. Its photoreceptors are the rods (about 120 million, very sensitive, for dim light and black and white) and the cones (about 6 million, for colour and fine detail). When light strikes them, pigments such as rhodopsin change shape and alter the cell's electrical state; this change from light energy into neural signals is called transduction. The signals pass to bipolar cells and then to ganglion cells, whose axons gather at the optic disc to form the optic nerve; because that spot has no receptors, it is the blind spot. Oddly, the receptors sit at the very back of the retina, so light passes through the other cell layers first. If Riya walks from bright sunlight into a dark cinema, she sees little at first until her rods adapt over many minutes (dark adaptation). Option b is right because the retina is the light-sensitive neural layer where transduction happens. Exam trap: the fovea is a tiny pit in the centre of the retina packed with cones, giving the sharpest vision; it is part of the retina, not the whole layer, so do not pick the part for the whole.", "topic": "Retina and transduction"}, {"q": "The process through which the brain uses the two retinal images into one dimensional perception is called:", "options": {"a": "Depth perception", "b": "Stereopsis", "c": "Binocular cues", "d": "Retinal display"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: stereopsis is the process by which the brain fuses the two slightly different retinal images into one single percept with solid, three-dimensional depth. Our eyes sit about 6 to 7 centimetres apart, so each retina receives a slightly different view of the same scene. That difference is called retinal (binocular) disparity, and the nearer an object, the greater the disparity. The visual cortex compares the two images, using cells tuned to disparity, and fuses them into one picture in which near objects stand out from far ones. Charles Wheatstone (1838) proved this with his stereoscope: two flat drawings, each showing the view of one eye, looked solid when viewed together. Bela Julesz (1960) went further with random-dot stereograms, patterns of dots with no visible shape in either image alone, which reveal a floating square only when both eyes fuse them; so stereopsis needs no monocular cues at all. Hold a finger close to your face and close each eye in turn: the finger jumps, showing the two views Meera's brain normally merges without effort. Option b is right because stereopsis is the specific name for fusing the two retinal images into one depth percept. Exam trap: binocular cues (disparity and convergence) are the raw information, depth perception is the whole ability, and stereopsis is the fusion process itself. Key note: the stem means fusing the two retinal images into one single three-dimensional (depth) percept; that fusion with depth from binocular disparity is stereopsis (Charles Wheatstone, 1838, stereoscope). Depth perception is the broader ability, so b stands.", "topic": "Stereopsis and binocular depth"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not an attribute of extraversion in the Big Five factor theory of personality?", "options": {"a": "Sociable", "b": "Talkative", "c": "Self-conscious", "d": "Affectionate"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: being self-conscious is not an attribute of extraversion; in the Big Five it is a facet of neuroticism. The Big Five or five-factor model describes personality with five broad traits, remembered as OCEAN: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism. They emerged from factor analyses of the trait words in everyday language, first reported by Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961), and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992) measured them with the NEO-PI-R, which splits each factor into six facets. The extraversion facets are warmth, gregariousness, assertiveness, activity, excitement-seeking and positive emotions. So a high scorer is sociable (gregariousness), talkative (assertiveness and activity) and affectionate (warmth). The neuroticism facets are anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness and vulnerability. Self-consciousness means shyness and embarrassment in front of others, a sensitivity to being judged. Consider Arjun, who chats with everyone at a wedding, hugs his cousins and is first on the dance floor: high extraversion. His cousin Riya worries that people are watching her and blushes when spoken to: that is high self-consciousness, a neuroticism facet. Option c is right because self-consciousness belongs to the neuroticism domain, not to extraversion. Exam trap: self-conscious sounds like the opposite of sociable, so students treat it as low extraversion; but introversion simply means quiet and reserved, while the anxious discomfort of self-consciousness is neuroticism. A calm introvert need not be self-conscious at all.", "topic": "Big Five extraversion facets"}, {"q": "Which part of personality opposes the irrational desires of the id according to Freud's psychoanalytic theory of personality?", "options": {"a": "Ego", "b": "Psychic energy", "c": "Super Ego", "d": "Libido"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the superego opposes the irrational desires of the id on moral grounds. Sigmund Freud (1923, The Ego and the Id) divided personality into three parts. The id, present from birth, is the reservoir of instinctual drives (sex and aggression); it follows the pleasure principle and demands instant gratification, without logic or morals. The ego develops to deal with reality; it follows the reality principle and finds safe, realistic ways to satisfy the id. The superego forms last, at about age 5 to 6, as the child resolves the Oedipus complex and takes in (internalises) the parents' moral rules. It has two parts: the conscience, which punishes wrongdoing with guilt, and the ego-ideal, the picture of the perfect self, which rewards good behaviour with pride. The superego does not merely delay the id's wishes; it condemns them and tries to block them entirely, pushing the person towards moral perfection. Suppose young Kabir wants to grab his sister's chocolate (id). His ego suggests waiting until she offers to share, but his superego says taking it would be wrong and makes him feel guilty for even wanting it. Option c is right because the superego is the moral opponent of the id. Exam trap: the ego also restrains the id, so students choose a; but the ego is a negotiator, not an opponent. Key note: the ego also restrains the id, but it does so realistically (delays and channels it, the reality principle); the part that opposes the id's desires on moral grounds is the superego. The printed answer is c and stands.", "topic": "Superego versus id"}, {"q": "A psychological test is good only when it measures what it is supposed to measure. This statement refers to which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Degree to which it is objective, precise and short", "b": "Degree to which it is consistent across several administrations", "c": "Degree to which it is norm based", "d": "Degree to which it is psychometrically sound"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: a test that measures what it is supposed to measure is valid, and validity is part of being psychometrically sound. Psychometrics is the science of measuring mental qualities, and a psychometrically sound test has two essential properties. Reliability is consistency: the test gives similar scores on repeated use. Validity is accuracy of meaning: the scores really reflect the trait the test claims to measure. Validity is checked in three classic ways. Content validity asks whether the items cover the whole domain. Criterion-related validity asks whether scores agree with an outside standard, now (concurrent) or later (predictive). Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks whether the scores behave as the theory of the trait predicts, correlating with related measures (convergent) and not with unrelated ones (discriminant). A test can be reliable without being valid: if Meera builds an anxiety scale whose items are so hard to read that it really measures reading skill, students will score consistently, but the test does not measure anxiety. It cannot be valid without being reliable, because random scores predict nothing. Option d is right because, among these choices, only psychometric soundness includes validity, the property the stem defines. Exam trap: option b, consistency across administrations, is reliability, the twin concept students most often swap with validity. Key note: the stem defines validity; 'psychometrically sound' is the only option that covers validity, so d stands.", "topic": "Validity of a test"}, {"q": "Which aspect of social interactions is measured by the theory of planned behaviour?", "options": {"a": "Attachment", "b": "Socialisation", "c": "Group formation", "d": "Attitudes"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: the theory of planned behaviour is a model of attitudes and how they lead to behaviour. Icek Ajzen (1985, 1991) built it on the theory of reasoned action of Martin Fishbein and Icek Ajzen (1975). The problem they tackled was old: Richard LaPiere (1934) toured the United States with a Chinese couple and was refused service only once in about 250 restaurants and hotels, yet when he later wrote to the same places, most replied that they would not serve Chinese guests. Stated attitudes did not predict action. Ajzen's answer is that behaviour follows intention, and intention rests on three things: the attitude towards the behaviour (do I think it is good?), subjective norms (do people who matter to me want me to do it?) and perceived behavioural control (can I actually do it?). The third element is what 'planned' adds to the older model; it covers behaviours not fully under our will. Predictions are best when attitude and behaviour are measured at the same level of specificity. Take Meera, who wants to start running each morning: she believes it is healthy (attitude), her friends run (subjective norm), and she has shoes and a park nearby (control), so her intention is strong and she is likely to run. Option d is right because the theory measures attitudes, together with norms and perceived control, to predict intention and behaviour. Exam trap: the word 'behaviour' in the title leads some students away from attitudes, but this is an attitude and behaviour theory from the attitudes chapter of social psychology.", "topic": "Theory of planned behaviour"}, {"q": "A strategy used by the ego to defend itself against the anxiety provoked by the conflicts of everyday life refers to:", "options": {"a": "Defence mechanism", "b": "Displacement", "c": "Rationalisation", "d": "Ego-ideal"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: a defence mechanism is the general name for a strategy the ego uses to protect itself against the anxiety that conflict produces. In the theory of Sigmund Freud (1894, 1923), the id pushes for gratification, the superego condemns, and reality sets limits. When these pressures clash, the ego feels anxiety, a danger signal. To reduce it, the ego uses defences: unconscious processes that deny, falsify or distort reality so that a threatening impulse or idea stays out of awareness or appears in a safer form. Freud first wrote of 'defence' in 1894, and his daughter Anna Freud (1936, The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence) gave the first systematic account, with repression as the basic defence that pushes threatening material out of consciousness. Later George Vaillant (1977) ranked defences from immature (projection, acting out) to mature (humour, sublimation). Defences are normal and often useful in small doses; they become a problem when rigid or overused. Example: Arjun fails his driving test and tells friends the examiner was biased (rationalisation), then goes home and snaps at his younger brother (displacement). Both are particular defence mechanisms serving one purpose: keeping his anxiety and hurt pride at bay. Option a is right because the stem defines the whole category, and defence mechanism is the name of that category. Exam trap: the distractors are real defences or real Freudian terms, so they feel correct; but a definition of 'a strategy used by the ego to defend itself' wants the general term, not one example of it.", "topic": "Defence mechanisms defined"}, {"q": "Development is about:", "options": {"a": "Age", "b": "Gender", "c": "Change", "d": "Genes"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: development is about change, the orderly and lasting changes (and stabilities) in a person from conception to death. Developmental psychology studies how people grow and change physically, cognitively, emotionally and socially across the lifespan. Not every change counts: development means systematic, relatively lasting change, not a passing mood. Some changes come mainly from maturation, the unfolding of a genetic plan (walking, puberty); others come from learning and experience (reading, language, values). Paul Baltes (1987) set out the lifespan perspective: development is lifelong, multidimensional (many areas at once), multidirectional (gains and losses at every age, such as vocabulary growing while reaction time slows), plastic (open to change) and shaped by its historical and cultural context. Developmentalists also ask the great questions about change: nature versus nurture, continuity versus discontinuity (gradual growth or stages), and stability versus change. Think of Kabir: he crawls at 8 months, walks at about a year, speaks in sentences at 2, reasons abstractly as a teenager and grows more patient in old age. Each step is a change, and that is what development is about. Option c is right because change over time is the core subject matter of development. Exam trap: age is tempting because development is described in age stages, but age is only a marker of time and causes nothing by itself; genes and gender are influences on development, not what development is.", "topic": "Definition of development"}, {"q": "The self-image formed during adolescence that integrates an individual's ideas of what he or she is and wants to be refers to:", "options": {"a": "Ego-ideal", "b": "Ego-identity", "c": "Crisis", "d": "Ego"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: ego-identity is the self-image formed in adolescence that joins together who a person is and who he or she wants to be. Erik Erikson (1950, Childhood and Society; 1968, Identity: Youth and Crisis) described eight psychosocial stages across the lifespan, each with a central conflict. The fifth stage, in adolescence (about 12 to 18 years), is identity versus role confusion. Teenagers try out roles, values, careers, friendships and beliefs, and ask 'Who am I?' and 'Where am I going?'. Success brings ego-identity, a stable and coherent sense of self that links the past, the present and the hoped-for future, along with the virtue of fidelity. Failure brings role confusion, an unclear sense of self. Erikson coined the phrase 'identity crisis' for this turning point. James Marcia (1966) measured identity with two questions: has the person explored options (crisis), and has he or she made a commitment? The answers give four identity statuses: diffusion (neither), foreclosure (commitment without exploration), moratorium (exploring, not yet committed) and achievement (explored, then committed). For example, Riya tries debating, coding and music in college, talks to people in each field and then commits to becoming a psychologist: she reaches identity achievement. Option b is right because Erikson's term for this integrated adolescent self-image is ego-identity. Exam trap: ego-ideal sounds almost the same, but it is Freud's term for the superego's image of the perfect self, a moral standard, not an adolescent's integrated identity.", "topic": "Erikson's ego-identity"}, {"q": "The theory of how people explain others' behaviour by attributing it either to internal disposition or to external situation refers to:", "options": {"a": "Attribution theory", "b": "Dispositional theory", "c": "Situational theory", "d": "Attributing causality"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: attribution theory explains how people account for others' behaviour by attributing it to internal dispositions or to external situations. Fritz Heider (1958, The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations) founded it. He saw every person as a 'naive psychologist' who wants to understand why things happen in order to predict and control the social world. His central distinction was between internal (dispositional) attributions, which put the cause in the person's traits, abilities, effort or mood, and external (situational) attributions, which put it in luck, the task, other people or circumstances. Later models built on his work. Edward E. Jones and Keith Davis (1965), in correspondent inference theory, showed when we infer a trait from an act (a freely chosen, unexpected act tells us most). Harold Kelley (1967), in his covariation model, said we weigh consensus (do others act the same way?), distinctiveness (does this person act this way only here?) and consistency (does the person always act this way?). We also show biases: Lee Ross (1977) named the fundamental attribution error, the tendency to overrate dispositions and underrate situations when judging others. Example: Arjun arrives late to class. Kabir thinks 'he is lazy' (dispositional), while Arjun knows the bus broke down (situational). Option a is right because attribution theory is the name of the whole theory of how people explain behaviour along this internal and external line. Exam trap: dispositional and situational are the two types of attribution inside the theory, not separate theories, and attributing causality is the process, not the theory's name.", "topic": "Attribution theory"}, {"q": "The behavioural approach is to reward and punishment as the cognitive approach is to _____:", "options": {"a": "Biological processes", "b": "The humanistic struggle for self-fulfilment", "c": "Individual genetic makeup", "d": "Internal processes of the mind or mental events"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: just as the behavioural approach explains behaviour through reward and punishment, the cognitive approach explains it through internal processes of the mind, or mental events. The behavioural approach began with John B. Watson (1913), who said psychology should study only observable behaviour, and was developed by B. F. Skinner (1938), for whom behaviour is shaped by its consequences: reinforcement makes it more likely, punishment less likely. The mind was treated as a black box. The cognitive revolution of the 1950s opened that box. Noam Chomsky (1959), in his review of Skinner's Verbal Behavior, argued that language cannot be explained by reinforcement alone, and Ulric Neisser (1967, Cognitive Psychology) named the new field. The cognitive approach studies attention, perception, memory, language, thinking, problem solving and decision-making, often through the information-processing model, which treats the mind like a computer that takes in, stores and retrieves information. Take Riya, who studies hard before an exam. A behaviourist says she studies because past good marks rewarded studying. A cognitive psychologist asks how she encodes the material, organises it in memory and retrieves it in the exam hall, and what she believes about her own ability. Option d is right because internal mental processes are to the cognitive approach what rewards and punishments are to the behavioural approach: its central explanatory idea. Exam trap: biological processes are also internal, but they belong to the biological perspective (neurons, hormones, genes), not to the cognitive one, which works at the level of thoughts and mental events.", "topic": "Behavioural versus cognitive approach"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"rinpas-2019": [{"q": "Which of the following perspectives of psychology is most concerned with individuals reaching their maximum potential?", "concept": "The answer is c: the humanistic perspective is the one most concerned with people reaching their maximum potential. Humanistic psychology grew in the United States in the 1950s and 1960s as a \"third force\", a reaction against both psychoanalysis (people driven by unconscious instincts) and behaviourism (people shaped by rewards and punishments). Its founders argued that people have free will, are basically good and carry an inborn drive to grow. Abraham Maslow (1943) called this drive self-actualisation: once the lower needs in his hierarchy (physiological, safety, belonging and esteem) are met, a person strives to become everything he or she is capable of becoming. He developed the idea fully in Motivation and Personality (1954). Carl Rogers (1951) built client-centred therapy on the same assumption, the actualising tendency, and described the goal of growth as the fully functioning person, someone open to experience and living fully in the present. Example: Meera leaves a secure bank job to train as a classical dancer because dancing is what she feels she is meant to do; a humanistic psychologist sees this as self-actualisation, not as a defence or a conditioned habit. Option c is right because only the humanistic perspective puts growth towards one's full potential at the centre of its theory. Exam trap: the psychodynamic perspective is about inner forces beyond the person's control, and positive psychology (Martin Seligman, 1998) also studies strengths and flourishing, but the classic textbook pairing is fixed: maximum potential, free will and the third force all mean humanistic (Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers).", "traps": {"a": "The behavioural perspective (John B. Watson, 1913; B. F. Skinner) explains behaviour through conditioning, reward and punishment from the environment; it says nothing about an inner drive to fulfil potential.", "b": "The cognitive perspective studies mental processes such as perception, memory and thinking (the information-processing view); it describes how we handle information, not growth towards one's potential.", "d": "Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) sees behaviour as driven by unconscious sexual and aggressive instincts and early childhood conflict, a pessimistic and deterministic view that is the opposite of the humanistic growth model."}}, {"q": "The basic difference between obsession and compulsion is:", "concept": "The answer is a: an obsession is a preoccupation with certain thoughts, while a compulsion is a preoccupation with certain actions. Both are symptoms of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Obsessions are recurrent, persistent and unwanted thoughts, urges or images that intrude into the mind and cause marked anxiety or distress, such as a fear of contamination or a nagging doubt that the door is unlocked. The person usually recognises them as products of his or her own mind and tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (hand washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (counting, silently repeating a prayer) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or by rigid rules, to reduce distress or prevent a feared event. The relief is brief, so the cycle repeats and strengthens. Example: Arjun keeps thinking that his hands carry germs (obsession), so he washes them twenty times before every meal (compulsion). DSM-5 (2013) requires obsessions, compulsions or both that are time-consuming (for example more than one hour a day) or cause clinically significant distress or impairment. Option a is right because it states the classic distinction: the obsession is the thought, the compulsion is the act that answers it. Exam trap: obsession and compulsion are two symptoms of one disorder, not two separate disorders; and compulsions can be silent mental acts such as counting, so a ritual that happens in the head is still a compulsion if it is done to reduce anxiety. OCD was never a mood disorder; it sat with the anxiety disorders through DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) and got its own chapter only in DSM-5 (2013).", "traps": {"b": "Neither is a mood disorder: obsessions and compulsions are both symptoms of OCD, which was grouped with the anxiety disorders through DSM-IV-TR (2000) and now has its own obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter in DSM-5 (2013).", "c": "There is a clear difference: obsessions are intrusive thoughts, urges or images, and compulsions are the repetitive acts performed to neutralise them; they are also symptoms, not two separate disorders.", "d": "This would only fit if no option gave the correct distinction, but option a states the standard textbook difference between thought and action."}}, {"q": "The process by which the egg is matured and released is:", "concept": "The answer is c: ovulation is the process by which a mature egg (ovum) is released from the ovary. Each month, follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the anterior pituitary ripens a follicle in one ovary, and a surge of luteinising hormone (LH) around the middle of a typical 28-day menstrual cycle (about day 14) makes the follicle burst and release its egg. The egg is swept into the fallopian tube, where it can be fertilised for only about a day. The order of early prenatal events is worth memorising. First comes ovulation (the egg is released). Next is fertilisation, when a sperm and the ovum unite, usually in the fallopian tube, to form a single cell, the zygote. The zygote then divides by mitosis as it travels down to the uterus. Finally comes implantation, when the blastocyst embeds in the uterine wall about 1 to 2 weeks after conception, which ends the germinal period. Example: if Riya has a regular 28-day cycle, she would usually ovulate around day 14. Option c is right because it is the only term that names the maturing and release of the egg. Exam trap: examiners place fertilisation and implantation beside ovulation because they come right after it in the sequence; ask whether the stem is about the egg leaving the ovary (ovulation), meeting the sperm (fertilisation) or attaching to the uterus (implantation). Also remember that eggs and sperm are formed by meiosis, not mitosis.", "traps": {"a": "Mitosis is ordinary cell division that makes two identical body cells; the zygote grows by mitosis after fertilisation, while eggs themselves are formed by meiosis, so mitosis does not mature or release the egg.", "b": "Fertilisation is the union of sperm and ovum, usually in the fallopian tube, forming the zygote; it happens after ovulation and does not mature or release the egg.", "d": "Implantation is the embedding of the blastocyst in the uterine wall about 1 to 2 weeks after conception, which ends the germinal period; it comes after fertilisation, not before it."}}, {"q": "The process by which we learn not to respond to similar stimuli in an identical manner is:", "concept": "The answer is c: discrimination is the process by which we learn not to respond to similar stimuli in the same way. Ivan Pavlov (1927) described it in his work on conditioned reflexes in dogs. A dog conditioned to salivate to one tone at first also salivates to other, similar tones; this spread is stimulus generalisation, and it follows a gradient (the more similar the tone, the stronger the response). If the original tone keeps being followed by food while the similar tones never are, the dog learns to salivate only to the original tone: stimulus discrimination. The same process works in operant conditioning, where a discriminative stimulus (B. F. Skinner, 1938) signals that a response will be reinforced, so a pigeon learns to peck only when a green light is on. Discrimination narrows responding; generalisation widens it. When Ivan Pavlov's co-worker made the discrimination impossible (an ellipse was made more and more like the circle), the dog became agitated, the experimental neurosis. Example: little Kabir was bitten by a large black dog and at first feared all dogs (generalisation); after many friendly meetings with his neighbour's small white dog, he now fears only large black dogs (discrimination). Option c is right because learning to respond differently to stimuli that resemble each other is the definition of discrimination. Exam trap: generalisation is the exact reverse (responding in the same way to similar stimuli), and examiners rely on the word \"similar\" appearing in both definitions; check whether the stem says the response is the same or different.", "traps": {"a": "Generalisation is the opposite process: responding in the same way to stimuli that resemble the conditioned stimulus, along a generalisation gradient, as when Ivan Pavlov's dogs salivated to tones similar to the trained one.", "b": "Extinction is the gradual weakening of a conditioned response when the conditioned stimulus is presented repeatedly without the unconditioned stimulus (or an operant response goes unreinforced); it lowers responding overall, not selectively to similar stimuli.", "d": "Generalisation and extinction do not fit the definition, so 'all of these' cannot be right; only discrimination describes learning to respond differently to similar stimuli."}}, {"q": "Which of the following gathers information about the brain and indicates overall activity of the brain?", "concept": "The answer is b: the electroencephalogram (EEG) gathers information about the brain and shows its overall level of activity. Metal electrodes placed on the scalp pick up the summed electrical activity of millions of cortical neurons firing together, and the machine displays it as wave patterns. Hans Berger (1929) published the first human EEG recordings. Because the EEG changes within milliseconds, it has excellent temporal resolution, but it cannot say precisely where the activity comes from (poor spatial resolution). Its wave bands track the brain's overall state: beta waves in alert waking, alpha waves in relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed, theta waves in light sleep and slow delta waves in deep sleep. That is why EEG is the standard tool in sleep research and in the diagnosis of epilepsy, where sudden spikes show abnormal bursts of activity. Example: when Meera is referred after a fit, the neurologist orders an EEG to look at the general pattern of her brain's electrical activity, not a detailed picture of one region. Option b is right because the stem asks for a method that indicates overall brain activity, which is exactly what scalp EEG provides. Exam trap: PET also measures activity, so it is the near-miss; but positron emission tomography injects a radioactive tracer and maps where glucose use or blood flow rises in specific regions, whereas EEG gives the global electrical state. Remember: EEG tells you when, imaging tells you where.", "traps": {"a": "Positron emission tomography (PET) injects a radioactive tracer, such as labelled glucose, and maps metabolic activity in specific regions; it localises activity rather than recording the brain's overall electrical state, and it is slow and invasive.", "c": "Electrical stimulation passes a small current into one brain area to see what movement or experience it produces, as in Wilder Penfield's mapping of the cortex in epilepsy patients; it tests what a spot does, not overall activity.", "d": "Studying brain damage (lesion and case studies, such as Paul Broca's patient Tan in 1861) infers function from what is lost after injury; it does not record ongoing brain activity."}}, {"q": "The physiological and psychological response to stress is referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the physiological and psychological response to stress is called strain. The terms come from engineering. In physics, stress is the load or force applied to a material, and strain is the deformation it produces: a bridge under heavy traffic (stress) bends slightly (strain). Psychology borrowed the analogy. The demand or event is the stressor, and the person's reaction to it (racing heart, raised cortisol, tense muscles, worry, irritability, poor sleep) is the strain. Hans Selye (1936, 1956) described the bodily side of this reaction as the general adaptation syndrome: an alarm stage (fight-or-flight arousal), a resistance stage (the body copes but stays aroused) and an exhaustion stage (resources run down and illness becomes likely). Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984) added the psychological side: how much strain we feel depends on how we appraise the demand and our resources to meet it. Example: Kabir's board examinations are the stressor; his sleeplessness, headaches and constant worry through March are the strain. Option c is right because it is the only option that names the response rather than the cause. Exam trap: option b, stressors, is the demand itself (examinations, bereavement, traffic), and examiners count on students mixing up cause and response; hypertension is one possible long-term result of strain, not the response itself. Key note: many textbooks call the response itself \"stress\" and keep \"strain\" for its wearing effect on the person; \"strain\" is the only option that names the response, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A transition is a life change such as starting college, marriage or retirement; it can act as a stressor (Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe, 1967, scored such life events in life change units), but it is not the response to stress.", "b": "Stressors are the demands or events that trigger the stress response, such as examinations, bereavement or noise; they are the cause, not the person's physiological and psychological reaction.", "d": "Hypertension is chronically raised blood pressure, one possible long-term health consequence of prolonged stress; it is a disease outcome, not the general name for the stress response."}}, {"q": "The behavioural technique for reducing anxiety in which patients practise relaxation while visualising anxiety-provoking situations of increasing intensity is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the technique is systematic desensitisation, developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe (1958) in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition. It rests on reciprocal inhibition: relaxation and anxiety cannot occur at the same time, so if deep relaxation is paired with a feared stimulus, the relaxation inhibits the anxiety. This is a form of counterconditioning, replacing a fear response with a calm one. Therapy has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually a shortened form of Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive muscle relaxation, tensing and releasing one muscle group after another. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, often rated on Joseph Wolpe's Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each item in turn, moving up only when the current step no longer causes anxiety. Example: Riya fears flying; she first imagines looking at a photograph of a plane, then booking a ticket, then waiting at the gate and finally take-off, relaxing at every stage. Option c is right because the stem names all three parts: relaxation, visualisation and situations of increasing intensity. Exam trap: graded exposure while relaxed is desensitisation, but exposure to the most feared situation at full intensity from the start is flooding (implosion when the scene is imagined); and desensitisation works by reciprocal inhibition, not by operant reinforcement, extinction alone or catharsis.", "traps": {"a": "Symptom substitution is the psychoanalytic prediction that removing a symptom without resolving its underlying conflict will make a new symptom appear; it is a criticism aimed at behaviour therapy, not a technique.", "b": "Time-out (from positive reinforcement) is an operant technique that briefly removes a child from a rewarding setting to reduce unwanted behaviour; it uses no relaxation or visualisation.", "d": "A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) is an operant system in which tokens earned for target behaviours are exchanged for rewards, used in wards and classrooms; it builds behaviour rather than reducing anxiety."}}, {"q": "The ability to acquire information from the culture, such as vocabulary and the kind of information learned in schools, is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is c: knowledge acquired from one's culture, such as vocabulary and school learning, is crystallised intelligence. Raymond Cattell (1943, tested 1963), later working with his student John Horn, split Charles Spearman's (1904) general factor g into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, for example spotting the rule in a series of shapes in John Raven's (1938) Progressive Matrices. It is closely tied to the efficiency of the brain, peaks in early adulthood and then slowly declines. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of facts, words, skills and strategies built up through education and experience in a culture. It is measured by vocabulary, general information and comprehension tests, and it stays stable or keeps rising into late adulthood. The two are linked, because fluid ability is invested in learning and so helps produce crystallised knowledge. Example: when Arjun, a retired teacher, beats his grandson at a crossword, he is using crystallised intelligence; when the grandson masters an unfamiliar puzzle game faster, he is using fluid intelligence. Option c is right because the stem's clue words, information from the culture, vocabulary and school learning, define crystallised intelligence. Exam trap: componential and contextual intelligence sound similar but belong to Robert Sternberg's (1985) triarchic theory, not to Raymond Cattell's model; and swapping fluid with crystallised is the commonest slip, so link \"crystallised\" to knowledge that has hardened through learning.", "traps": {"a": "Fluid intelligence (Raymond Cattell and John Horn) is reasoning and problem solving with new material, independent of learned knowledge, as measured by Raven's matrices; the stem describes learned cultural knowledge instead.", "b": "Componential (analytical) intelligence is one of the three subtheories of Robert Sternberg's (1985) triarchic theory, covering the mental processes of planning, monitoring and processing information; it is not stored cultural knowledge.", "d": "Contextual (practical) intelligence is another subtheory of Robert Sternberg's (1985) triarchic theory: adapting to, shaping or selecting one's everyday environment, so it is street sense rather than vocabulary and school facts."}}, {"q": "Critics of Freudian psychoanalysis maintain that this form of therapy:", "concept": "The answer is b: critics argue that psychoanalysis can be harmful because it tolerates or even encourages transference. Transference is the patient's unconscious redirection onto the analyst of feelings, wishes and attitudes first felt towards important people in childhood, usually the parents. The patient may come to love, depend on or resent the analyst as if he or she were that parent. Sigmund Freud (1912) saw transference as the engine of therapy: by interpreting it, the analyst shows the patient old conflicts being replayed in the present, and the resulting transference neurosis can be worked through. Critics see real dangers. Intense dependence can stretch therapy over years, blur professional boundaries, open the way to exploitation and leave the patient worse off when treatment ends, and the analyst's own feelings towards the patient (countertransference) can deepen the problem. More broadly, Hans Eysenck (1952) attacked psychoanalysis on outcome grounds, claiming that about two thirds of neurotic patients improved within two years with or without therapy. Example: Meera, in her third year of analysis, cannot make any decision without first asking her analyst, just as she once relied on her father; a critic would say the method has created a new problem. Option b is right because harm through fostered transference is a genuine and frequently cited criticism. Exam trap: option d is reversed, since the standard complaint is that psychoanalysis is too long and too costly, not too quick; and the usual charge is that Sigmund Freud put too much emphasis on sex, not too little.", "traps": {"a": "Reversed: the usual criticism, made by Alfred Adler and Carl Jung when they broke away (1911 and 1913) and later by the neo-Freudians, is that Sigmund Freud placed too much weight on sexuality.", "c": "Psychoanalysis is not known as a phobia treatment; specific phobias respond best to exposure-based behaviour therapy such as Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation.", "d": "Reversed: classical analysis runs four or five sessions a week for years, so critics call it too long, slow and expensive, not too quick to be effective."}}, {"q": "When Maya first saw a violent movie, she was scared and disgusted. But now that she has seen several such movies, she is barely affected by them. Maya has experienced:", "concept": "The answer is a: Maya has experienced habituation, a decrease in response to a stimulus that is presented again and again. Habituation is the simplest form of learning: the organism learns that a repeated stimulus is harmless or unimportant and stops reacting strongly to it. It is not simple fatigue, because a long break brings the response back (spontaneous recovery), and a new or sudden stimulus can restore it at once (dishabituation). Eric Kandel traced it, in work from the 1960s and 1970s, to weakened transmission at synapses in the sea slug Aplysia. In media research the same process is called desensitisation: repeated exposure to screen violence blunts both the emotional and the physiological arousal it first produced, so later scenes feel less shocking and attract less concern. That is exactly Maya's story: fear and disgust the first time, almost nothing after several such films. Infant researchers use habituation too: Robert Fantz (1964) showed that babies look less and less at a picture they have seen many times and look longer at a new one, which proves they can tell the two apart. Option a is right because the stem describes a strong first reaction that weakens with repeated exposure to the same kind of stimulus. Exam trap: cultivation (option c) is also about heavy media viewing, so it is the near-miss, but George Gerbner's cultivation theory (1970s) concerns changed beliefs about the world (heavy viewers think the world is more dangerous than it is), not a fading emotional reaction.", "traps": {"b": "Displacement is a defence mechanism (Anna Freud, 1936) in which feelings are redirected from a threatening target to a safer one, such as shouting at a younger brother after being scolded by a teacher; it does not describe a fading reaction.", "c": "Cultivation is George Gerbner's theory that heavy television viewing gradually shapes viewers' beliefs about social reality (the 'mean world' effect); it concerns beliefs, not a reduced emotional response to repeated scenes.", "d": "Catharsis is the release of pent-up emotion (Aristotle on tragedy; Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud's cathartic method); the catharsis idea predicts that watching violence drains aggression, which is not what happened to Maya."}}, {"q": "Which of the following approaches to personality is least deterministic?", "concept": "The answer is b: the humanistic approach is the least deterministic approach to personality. Determinism is the view that behaviour is fully caused by forces the person does not choose, such as instincts, childhood experiences or rewards from the environment; its opposite is free will. Duane Schultz and Sydney Ellen Schultz compare personality theories on exactly this question. Sigmund Freud's (1900, 1923) psychoanalysis is strongly deterministic: behaviour is driven by unconscious sexual and aggressive instincts and largely fixed by the first five years of life (psychic determinism). B. F. Skinner's (1953) behaviourism is just as deterministic, but the causes lie in the environment: reinforcement history shapes everything, and in Beyond Freedom and Dignity (1971) he called free will an illusion. Albert Bandura's (1977, 1986) social learning approach softens this with reciprocal determinism, in which person, behaviour and environment influence one another, so people have some self-regulation. Humanists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) go furthest: people have free will, are responsible for their choices, are shaped more by present experience than by the past, and can grow towards self-actualisation. Example: when Kabir leaves engineering to study music, a humanist explains it by his own choice and growth, not by his childhood or past rewards. Option b is right because the humanistic approach gives the most room to choice and the least to outside causes. Exam trap: social learning theory also stresses agency and self-efficacy, so it is the tempting near-miss, but it still explains behaviour through learning history and situations, and its own label is reciprocal determinism; only humanism puts free will at the centre.", "traps": {"a": "The psychoanalytic approach (Sigmund Freud) is the classic deterministic theory: behaviour is caused by unconscious instincts and fixed by early childhood conflicts, leaving little room for conscious choice.", "c": "The behaviourist approach (John B. Watson, 1913; B. F. Skinner) holds that reinforcement and punishment from the environment determine behaviour; B. F. Skinner openly rejected free will, so it is highly deterministic.", "d": "Albert Bandura's social learning (social cognitive) approach allows self-regulation and self-efficacy, but explains behaviour through reciprocal determinism among person, behaviour and environment, so it is less free than humanism."}}, {"q": "To Ellis, an \"irrational\" idea is typically:", "concept": "The answer is d: to Albert Ellis, an irrational idea is one that is unrealistic or untrue. Albert Ellis (1955, 1962) founded rational therapy, later called rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), on the claim that our emotions come mainly from our beliefs about events rather than from the events themselves. His ABC model sets this out: A is the activating event, B the belief about it, and C the emotional and behavioural consequence. Rational beliefs are flexible, logical and consistent with reality, and they help us reach our goals. Irrational beliefs are rigid, illogical, unrealistic and self-defeating. Albert Ellis grouped them around absolutist \"musts\" (\"I must succeed\", \"others must treat me fairly\"), awfulising (\"it would be terrible\"), low frustration tolerance (\"I can't stand it\") and global self-rating (\"I am worthless\"). Therapy adds D, disputing: the therapist actively challenges each belief by asking whether it is logical, whether there is evidence for it and whether it helps, which leads to E, a new and effective belief. Example: Arjun fails one test (A), believes \"I must never fail, so I am a total failure\" (B) and feels depressed (C); disputing shows him that one failure does not make him a failure, and his mood lifts. Option d is right because Albert Ellis defined irrational beliefs by their lack of realism, logic and evidence. Exam trap: option c is reversed, because irrational beliefs are exactly the ones that can and should be disputed; and they are conscious self-statements, not repressed material, which separates REBT from psychoanalysis.", "traps": {"a": "An 'emotional truth' implies a belief that feels deeply valid and should be accepted; Albert Ellis treated such beliefs as hypotheses to be tested and disputed, not as truths.", "b": "Repression is Sigmund Freud's defence of pushing threatening material out of awareness; Albert Ellis's irrational beliefs are conscious, easily reported self-statements, which is why they can be disputed directly.", "c": "Reversed: the whole method of REBT is disputing (the D in the ABCDE model), so irrational beliefs are rigid but very much disputable."}}, {"q": "The release of emotions is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: the release of emotions is called catharsis. The word is Greek for \"purging\" or \"cleansing\". Aristotle (about 335 BC), in the Poetics, used it for the way a tragedy purges the audience's pity and fear. In psychology it entered through Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895), Studies on Hysteria. Breuer's patient Anna O. (Bertha Pappenheim) found that her hysterical symptoms eased when, under hypnosis, she talked through the moment each one began and expressed the feelings she had held back at the time; she called this the \"talking cure\" and \"chimney sweeping\". The procedure became known as the cathartic method. Strictly, abreaction is the reliving and discharge of the emotion tied to a particular memory, and catharsis is the release and relief that follow. Irvin Yalom (1970) later listed catharsis among the therapeutic factors of group therapy. Example: Meera has held in her grief since her grandmother died; when she finally cries and talks about it in a session, she feels lighter, an experience of catharsis. Option d is right because catharsis is the standard term for the release of pent-up emotion. Exam trap: insight is understanding, not emotional release; psychoanalysis holds that lasting change needs insight, while catharsis alone brings only short-lived relief. Also, the catharsis hypothesis for anger (that venting drains aggression) is not supported: Brad Bushman (2002) found that hitting a punching bag while thinking about an insulter increased aggression.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviour change is the outcome that behaviour therapies (Joseph Wolpe's desensitisation, B. F. Skinner's operant methods) aim for directly; it is a goal of therapy, not the name for releasing emotion.", "b": "Insight is an emotionally felt understanding of how present problems repeat past conflicts, the aim of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis; it is understanding, not the discharge of feeling.", "c": "Mastery is the sense of competence and control a client gains by facing problems successfully, close to Albert Bandura's (1977) self-efficacy; it is not emotional release."}}, {"q": "_____ was the first to cite the brain as the source of mental disorder.", "concept": "The answer is c: Hippocrates was the first to cite the brain as the source of mental disorder. Hippocrates (about 460 to 377 BC), the Greek physician called the father of medicine, broke with the supernatural model that blamed gods, demons or sin. He taught that mental illness has natural causes in the brain and body. The Hippocratic text On the Sacred Disease argued that epilepsy is not sacred at all but a disorder of the brain, and that from the brain alone arise our joys and sorrows. His humoral theory held that health is a balance of four bodily fluids, blood, yellow bile, black bile and phlegm, and that an excess of black bile causes melancholia. He described mania, melancholia and phrenitis, one of the first classifications of mental disorder, and recommended rest, diet and a calm life instead of exorcism. Option c is right because Hippocrates is the standard textbook name for the first natural, brain-based explanation of mental disorder. Exam trap: Galen (about AD 129 to 216) is the strong near-miss because he developed the humoral theory into four temperaments, but he lived about six centuries later; Plato and Aristotle were philosophers who wrote about the soul, and Aristotle placed thought in the heart. Key note: Alcmaeon of Croton (about 500 BC) placed the mind in the brain even earlier, but he is not an option and Hippocrates is the standard answer.", "traps": {"a": "Plato (about 427 to 347 BC) was a philosopher who divided the soul into reason, spirit and appetite and saw some madness as divinely inspired; he did not give a medical, brain-based account of mental disorder.", "b": "Aristotle (384 to 322 BC) placed the seat of thought and feeling in the heart and treated the brain mainly as a cooling organ, so he is the opposite of the right answer.", "d": "Galen (about AD 129 to 216), a Greek physician in Rome, developed the Hippocratic humours into temperaments and studied brain anatomy, but he came about six centuries after Hippocrates, so he was not the first."}}, {"q": "In which year was the first mental hospital in the USA opened?", "concept": "The answer is b: the first mental hospital in the USA, in the sense of the first hospital to take in mentally ill patients, opened in 1753. The Pennsylvania Hospital in Philadelphia was founded in 1751 by Benjamin Franklin and the physician Thomas Bond and opened its doors in 1752 to 1753, admitting the sick poor and \"lunatics\", who were kept in basement cells. Benjamin Rush (1745 to 1813), later called the father of American psychiatry, worked there from 1783 and wrote the first American psychiatry textbook, Medical Inquiries and Observations upon the Diseases of the Mind (1812). Link the European dates too: Philippe Pinel (1793) unchained patients at the Bicêtre in Paris, William Tuke (1796) opened the York Retreat in England, and Bethlem (Bedlam) in London was handed to the City by Henry VIII in 1547. Option b is right because 1753 falls in the period when Pennsylvania Hospital began admitting mentally ill patients. Exam trap: 1793 and 1547 are real dates attached to other events (Philippe Pinel's reform in France and Bethlem's transfer in England), and 1693 sits right after the Salem witch trials (1692 to 1693) in colonial Massachusetts, the era of demonology rather than hospitals. Key note: the first hospital built only for the mentally ill was Eastern State Hospital, Williamsburg, Virginia, 1773; that year is not an option, so 1753 stands.", "traps": {"a": "1693 belongs to the end of the Salem witch trials (1692 to 1693) in colonial Massachusetts, a time of witch hunts and demonology, long before any American hospital for the mentally ill.", "c": "1793 is the year Philippe Pinel is said to have unchained patients at the Bicêtre in Paris, the start of moral treatment in France, not the opening of an American hospital.", "d": "1547 is the year Henry VIII handed Bethlem (Bedlam) in London to the City, after which it served as an asylum for the insane; it is an English date, far too early for the USA."}}, {"q": "Who established the first laboratory for the experimental study of psychology?", "concept": "The answer is c: Wilhelm Wundt established the first laboratory for the experimental study of psychology, at the University of Leipzig in Germany, in 1879. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) was trained in medicine and physiology and wanted psychology to become an independent experimental science. In his Leipzig laboratory, students measured reaction times, sensations and feelings under controlled conditions and reported their conscious experience through trained introspection; the first dissertation from the room, Max Friedrich's study of the time needed to apperceive a stimulus, began that winter. Wilhelm Wundt had already written Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) and soon founded the first journal of experimental psychology, Philosophische Studien (1881). This is why 1879 is taken as the birth of scientific psychology and Wilhelm Wundt is called the father of experimental psychology. Option c is right because Leipzig 1879 is the accepted date of the first formal psychology laboratory. Exam trap: William James (1875) had a small demonstration laboratory at Harvard, which is why some sources argue for him, but it was used for teaching, not original research. Francis Galton (1884) set up an anthropometric laboratory in London to measure individual differences, and Ernst Weber (1834) gave his name to Weber's law of the just noticeable difference; both were pioneers, but neither founded the first psychology laboratory.", "traps": {"a": "Francis Galton (1822 to 1911) opened his anthropometric laboratory in London in 1884 to measure sensory acuity and reaction time in the public; he founded the study of individual differences, not the first experimental psychology laboratory.", "b": "Ernst Heinrich Weber (1795 to 1878) was a physiologist known for the just noticeable difference and Weber's law (1834); his work came before psychology had a laboratory of its own.", "d": "Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961) founded analytical psychology (the collective unconscious and archetypes); he was a psychiatrist and clinician, not the founder of the first psychology laboratory."}}, {"q": "The American Psychological Association (APA) was founded in:", "concept": "The answer is a: the American Psychological Association (APA) was founded in July 1892. A small group of psychologists met at Clark University in Worcester, Massachusetts, at the invitation of G. Stanley Hall (1844 to 1924), who became its first president; the first annual meeting followed that December at the University of Pennsylvania. It began with a few dozen members and is now the largest association of psychologists in the world. G. Stanley Hall is worth knowing well: he earned the first American PhD in psychology (Harvard, 1878), opened the first American research laboratory at Johns Hopkins (1883), founded the American Journal of Psychology (1887), became Clark's first president and invited Sigmund Freud and Carl Jung to lecture there in 1909. Option a is right because 1892 is the standard founding year of the APA. Exam trap: the nearby years are real dates for other events. 1893 is when Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud published their preliminary communication on hysteria, and 1896 is when Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania. Also do not confuse the American Psychological Association with the American Psychiatric Association (founded in 1844), which publishes the DSM.", "traps": {"b": "1893 is the year Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud published their preliminary communication on the psychical mechanism of hysterical phenomena, not the founding of the APA.", "c": "1896 is the year Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania, the birth of clinical psychology, which examiners often swap with the APA date.", "d": "1897 marks no landmark here; by then the APA had already been holding annual meetings for five years."}}, {"q": "Who published the mechanism of hysteria in 1893?", "concept": "The answer is b: Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1893) published the mechanism of hysteria. Their paper, \"On the psychical mechanism of hysterical phenomena: preliminary communication\", appeared in January 1893 and argued that \"hysterics suffer mainly from reminiscences\": symptoms come from traumatic memories whose emotion was never expressed, and they disappear when the memory is recalled and the feeling discharged under hypnosis. Josef Breuer (1842 to 1925), a Viennese physician, had developed this cathartic method with his patient Anna O. (Bertha Pappenheim) between 1880 and 1882. The paper became the first chapter of their book Studies on Hysteria (1895), usually taken as the starting point of psychoanalysis. Sigmund Freud later replaced hypnosis with free association, and the two men drifted apart over his growing emphasis on sexuality. Option b is right because the 1893 preliminary communication was written jointly by Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer. Exam trap: Jean-Martin Charcot (1825 to 1893) is the strong distractor, because Sigmund Freud studied with him at the Salpêtrière in Paris (1885 to 1886) and Charcot's work on hysteria and hypnosis inspired him, but he never co-authored with Freud. Alfred Adler and Carl Jung joined Sigmund Freud's circle only later (1902 and 1907) and both broke away.", "traps": {"a": "Jean-Martin Charcot (1825 to 1893), the French neurologist at the Salpêtrière, taught Sigmund Freud about hysteria and hypnosis in 1885 to 1886, but he did not co-author the 1893 paper.", "c": "Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) joined Sigmund Freud's circle in 1902 and left in 1911 to found individual psychology (inferiority feelings, striving for superiority); he never wrote on hysteria with Freud.", "d": "Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961) met Sigmund Freud only in 1907 and broke with him in 1913 to found analytical psychology, so he could not have co-authored an 1893 paper."}}, {"q": "Morton Prince used hypnosis to treat multiple personality disorder in:", "concept": "The answer is d: Morton Prince's use of hypnosis to treat multiple personality is dated 1905, the year usually printed for his famous case. Morton Prince (1854 to 1929) was a Boston neurologist and an early American student of dissociation. His patient, \"Miss Christine Beauchamp\" (really Clara Norton Fowler), was a young student who seemed to switch between several distinct personalities, including a timid, saintly one and a mischievous, childlike one called \"Sally\". Using hypnosis, Morton Prince reached the different personalities, mapped how much each knew of the others and tried to fuse them into one stable self. He reported the case in The Dissociation of a Personality, the classic early account of what DSM-IV (1994) renamed dissociative identity disorder. He also founded the Journal of Abnormal Psychology in 1906. Option d is right because 1905 is the printed date for Morton Prince's hypnotic treatment and its write-up. Exam trap: 1898, when the treatment actually began, is the strongest near-miss, while 1900 and 1902 are fillers. Do not confuse Morton Prince with Pierre Janet (1889), who explained hysteria as dissociation, or with the later famous cases The Three Faces of Eve (1957) and Sybil (1973). Key note: the treatment began in 1898 and the book is usually dated 1906; 1905 is the printed answer and stands.", "traps": {"a": "1898 is when Morton Prince first began hypnotising Clara Norton Fowler, so it is the strongest near-miss; the case is usually dated by its famous write-up, and 1905 is the printed answer.", "b": "1900 marks nothing in the Beauchamp case; it is better remembered as the date on Sigmund Freud's The Interpretation of Dreams.", "c": "1902 is a filler date between the start of treatment (1898) and the book (1905 to 1906); it marks no landmark in Morton Prince's work."}}, {"q": "Ivan Pavlov demonstrated classical conditioning in:", "concept": "The answer is c: Ivan Pavlov demonstrated classical conditioning around 1902. Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936) was a Russian physiologist studying digestion. He collected his dogs' saliva through a cheek tube and noticed that they began to salivate before food reached their mouths, at the sight of the dish or the sound of the attendant's footsteps. He called these \"psychic secretions\" and, with his co-workers, began studying them systematically: a neutral stimulus such as a metronome was sounded just before meat powder was given, and after several pairings the sound alone produced salivation. In this classical (Pavlovian) conditioning, food is the unconditioned stimulus, salivation to food the unconditioned response, the metronome becomes the conditioned stimulus and salivation to it the conditioned response. Ivan Pavlov reported the findings publicly in a lecture in Madrid in 1903 and won the Nobel Prize in 1904, for his work on digestion rather than conditioning. Option c is right because 1902 is the printed date for his demonstration of conditioned reflexes. Exam trap: 1905 is the year of the Binet-Simon intelligence scale and 1900 the date on Sigmund Freud's The Interpretation of Dreams, so examiners scatter famous turn-of-the-century dates; option a (1999) is a misprint-looking distractor, impossible because Ivan Pavlov died in 1936. Key note: dates vary between 1897 and 1903 across books; 1902 is the printed answer and stands.", "traps": {"a": "1999 is impossible, since Ivan Pavlov died in 1936; it looks like a misprint of a nineteenth-century date and should be rejected at once.", "b": "1900 falls within Ivan Pavlov's early work on psychic secretions but is not the printed date; it is better remembered as the date on Sigmund Freud's The Interpretation of Dreams.", "d": "1905 is the year of Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon's first intelligence scale, and it comes after Ivan Pavlov's Madrid lecture (1903) and Nobel Prize (1904), so it is too late."}}, {"q": "Social Learning Theory was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is d: social learning theory was developed by Albert Bandura. Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021), a Canadian-born psychologist at Stanford University, argued that people learn not only from their own rewards and punishments but also by watching others, a process called observational learning or modelling. In the Bobo doll experiment (Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied those acts, while children who saw a calm adult did not. Albert Bandura named four processes behind modelling: attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. He also showed vicarious reinforcement: seeing a model rewarded or punished changes whether the observer performs the act, even though the behaviour may be learned either way. He set out the theory in Social Learning Theory (1977), added self-efficacy (1977) and reciprocal determinism, and renamed the whole approach social cognitive theory in 1986. Example: Riya starts holding her pen exactly the way her favourite teacher does, without ever being rewarded for it. Option d is right because social learning theory is identified above all with Albert Bandura. Exam trap: Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941) wrote an earlier book called Social Learning and Imitation, and Julian Rotter (1954) also called his expectancy theory a social learning theory, but the standard answer is Albert Bandura. The other three options are names of well-known textbook authors and researchers, placed to test whether you know who wrote what.", "traps": {"a": "Pauline V. Young was an American sociologist who wrote Scientific Social Surveys and Research (1939), a classic research methods text; she did not develop a learning theory.", "b": "James C. Coleman wrote the widely used textbook Abnormal Psychology and Modern Life; he is known as an abnormal psychology author, not as the founder of social learning theory.", "c": "Richard Crutchfield (1955) devised the Crutchfield apparatus, a panel of switches and lights for studying conformity without face-to-face groups (a refinement of Solomon Asch's method); he did not develop social learning theory."}}, {"q": "The first intelligence test was published in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the first intelligence test was published in 1905. The French education ministry asked Alfred Binet (1857 to 1911) to find a way of picking out Paris schoolchildren who needed special education. With the physician Theodore Simon, he produced the Binet-Simon scale (1905): 30 items of rising difficulty that tested judgement, comprehension and reasoning, rather than the simple sensory and reaction-time measures that Francis Galton (1884) and James McKeen Cattell (1890) had tried. The 1908 revision grouped the items by the age at which typical children pass them, introducing mental age, and the 1911 revision extended the scale to adults. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet (1916) multiplied the result by 100 to give the intelligence quotient (IQ). Example: a 10-year-old, Arjun, who passes the items typical of 8-year-olds has a mental age of 8 and a ratio IQ of 80. Option b is right because 1905 is the year of the first practical intelligence test. Exam trap: 1904 is close because that is when the ministry commission was set up and when Charles Spearman proposed his g factor; 1908 and 1911 are revisions, and 1916 is the Stanford-Binet, so choose 1905 for the first test.", "traps": {"a": "1904 is the year the French ministry set up the commission that led to Alfred Binet's task, and the year Charles Spearman proposed the g factor; the test itself appeared in 1905.", "c": "1906 falls between the original Binet-Simon scale (1905) and its first revision (1908); it marks no landmark in intelligence testing.", "d": "1907 is not a testing landmark; it is nearer to Lightner Witmer's first printed use of 'clinical psychology' (1907), a different history question."}}, {"q": "The thyroid gland controls:", "concept": "The answer is c: the thyroid gland controls metabolism. The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland in the front of the neck, below the larynx. It releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), hormones made with iodine, which set the body's metabolic rate: how fast cells burn fuel and produce heat and energy. The thyroid is controlled by a feedback loop: the hypothalamus releases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), which makes the anterior pituitary release thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), which drives the thyroid; high thyroid hormone levels then switch the loop down. Too much thyroid hormone (hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease) speeds everything up: weight loss, a racing heart, heat intolerance, restlessness and anxiety. Too little (hypothyroidism) slows everything down: weight gain, tiredness, feeling cold, slowed thinking and low mood, which is why doctors check thyroid function in depression. In infancy, untreated thyroid deficiency causes congenital hypothyroidism (once called cretinism), with stunted growth and intellectual disability; iodine deficiency is a major preventable cause, which is why salt is iodised in India. Example: Riya feels low, has gained weight and always feels cold; a blood test shows low thyroxine. Option c is right because setting the metabolic rate is the thyroid's main job. Exam trap: thyroid problems change mood, which tempts students towards \"emotions\", but emotion is organised by the limbic system and the adrenal stress hormones; and blood glucose is regulated by insulin and glucagon from the pancreas, not by the thyroid.", "traps": {"a": "Glucose is regulated by the pancreas: insulin from the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans lowers blood glucose and glucagon from the alpha cells raises it; the thyroid sets how fast energy is used, not glucose absorption.", "b": "Emotions are organised by the limbic system (amygdala, hypothalamus) and the autonomic nervous system with adrenal hormones; thyroid disease can alter mood, but emotion is not the gland's function.", "d": "Sexuality and reproduction are governed by the gonads (testosterone, oestrogen, progesterone) under the pituitary's FSH and LH; the thyroid is not a sex gland."}}, {"q": "The ability of the eye to distinguish fine details is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: the ability of the eye to distinguish fine detail is called visual acuity. Acuity depends on how sharply the image is focused on the retina and on how densely the receptors are packed. It is best at the fovea, the small pit at the centre of the retina that contains only cones. Each foveal cone connects to its own bipolar and ganglion cell, so fine detail is preserved; in the periphery many rods converge on a single ganglion cell, which gains sensitivity in dim light but loses detail. Acuity is measured with the Snellen chart (Herman Snellen, 1862). Normal vision is written 6/6 in metres (20/20 in feet): you read at 6 metres what a typical eye reads at 6 metres. A score of 6/12 means you must stand at 6 metres to read what a typical eye reads at 12. Refractive errors such as myopia, hypermetropia and astigmatism lower acuity and are corrected with lenses. Example: at the eye clinic Kabir can read only the top three lines of the chart, so his acuity is poor and he needs glasses. Option b is right because acuity literally means sharpness, the power to resolve fine detail. Exam trap: sensitivity is the near-miss, but it means the ability to detect faint light (rods and dark adaptation), which trades off against acuity; and adaptation is a change in sensitivity under steady stimulation, not detail vision.", "traps": {"a": "Visual dilation is not a standard term; dilation refers to the pupil widening in dim light or with sympathetic arousal, which changes how much light enters the eye, not the ability to see fine detail.", "c": "Visual sensitivity is the ability to detect weak light, highest in the rod-rich peripheral retina and raised by dark adaptation; it trades off against acuity rather than being the same thing.", "d": "Adaptation is the change in sensitivity under steady stimulation, such as dark adaptation as rods and cones grow more sensitive over 20 to 30 minutes in the dark; it is not detail vision."}}, {"q": "A disorder called _____ results when fluid pressure builds up inside the eye and causes damage to the optic nerve.", "concept": "The answer is d: glaucoma is the disorder in which fluid pressure builds up inside the eye and damages the optic nerve. The front of the eye is filled with a clear fluid, the aqueous humour, which is constantly produced and drained away. If drainage is blocked, the pressure inside the eye (intraocular pressure) rises and slowly damages the fibres of the optic nerve where they leave the eye at the optic disc. The commonest form, open-angle glaucoma, is painless and gradual: peripheral vision is lost first, leading to tunnel vision, and untreated it can end in blindness. Because damaged optic nerve fibres do not regrow, the loss is permanent, so treatment (eye drops, laser or surgery to lower the pressure) aims to stop it getting worse. Glaucoma is one of the leading causes of irreversible blindness worldwide, which is why eye checks after the age of 40 often include a pressure test. Example: Arjun's father keeps bumping into door frames; tests show high eye pressure and a shrinking field of vision. Option d is right because it is the only option that is a disease of the eye itself caused by raised pressure. Exam trap: prosopagnosia and achromatopsia sound like eye diseases, but in the classic exam sense they are brain disorders with healthy eyes: prosopagnosia is face blindness after damage to the fusiform face area, and achromatopsia is cerebral colour blindness after damage to visual area V4. Dyslexia is a reading disorder, not an eye disease.", "traps": {"a": "Prosopagnosia is face blindness: an inability to recognise familiar faces despite normal vision, after damage to the fusiform face area in the occipitotemporal cortex; the eye and optic nerve are healthy.", "b": "Achromatopsia here means cerebral colour blindness, the loss of colour vision after damage to visual area V4 of the cortex; it involves no rise in eye pressure.", "c": "Dyslexia is a specific learning disorder of reading (accurate, fluent word reading and spelling), usually linked to weak phonological processing; it is not a disease of the eye."}}, {"q": "Our general method for dealing with the environment is known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: our general, habitual method of dealing with the environment, the typical way we perceive, think, remember and solve problems, is called cognitive style. A cognitive style is a preferred way of processing information, not a level of ability. Two people with the same IQ can still differ in style: one takes in the whole scene, the other picks out the parts. The best known style is field dependence versus field independence, described by Herman Witkin (1962) in Psychological Differentiation. In his rod-and-frame test a person in a dark room sets a glowing rod to vertical inside a tilted glowing frame. Field-dependent people let the frame pull the rod off true; field-independent people trust their own body sense and set it nearly upright. In the Embedded Figures Test, field-independent people find a simple shape hidden inside a complex design faster. A second classic style is reflection versus impulsivity, studied by Jerome Kagan (1965) with the Matching Familiar Figures Test: reflective children answer slowly and accurately, impulsive children answer fast and make more errors. Example: Meera reads a whole case history before forming a view, while Kabir seizes one detail and decides; they may score the same on an intelligence test, yet their cognitive styles differ. Option d is right because cognitive style is the name for a person's consistent, general approach to taking in and handling information from the environment. Exam trap: examiners put 'perceptual style' beside it. Field dependence does begin with perception, but the umbrella term in textbooks covers thinking and problem solving too, so it is cognitive style. Intelligence is how well you can think (ability); style is how you prefer to think.", "traps": {"a": "Intelligence is the capacity to learn, reason and solve problems (Alfred Binet, David Wechsler), a matter of how well a person thinks. The stem asks for a habitual manner of dealing with the world, which is style, not ability.", "b": "Perceptual style is a narrower phrase sometimes used for Herman Witkin's field dependence as seen in perception tasks. The general method covering perceiving, thinking and problem solving is called cognitive style.", "c": "Personality is the whole enduring pattern of traits, feelings, motives and behaviour (Gordon Allport, 1937). Cognitive style is only one part of it, the information-handling part, so personality is too broad."}}, {"q": "Most psychologists believe that aggression is:", "concept": "The answer is c: most psychologists believe that aggression is largely a learned response, picked up by watching models and kept up by reinforcement. Option a, an innate response to frustration, loosely reflects the frustration-aggression hypothesis put forward at Yale by John Dollard, Leonard Doob, Neal Miller, O. Hobart Mowrer and Robert Sears (1939). Frustration means the blocking of a goal. Their original claim was double: frustration always leads to some form of aggression, and aggression always comes from frustration. The Yale group made no assumption about whether this link was innate or learned (Neal Miller, 1941). If the source of frustration is too powerful or absent, the aggression is displaced onto a safer target. Example: Arjun's boss cancels his promotion; he cannot shout at the boss, so he snaps at his younger brother at home. Neal Miller (1941) soon softened the theory: frustration creates a readiness for aggression, but it can also lead to sadness or problem solving. Leonard Berkowitz (1989) reformulated it: frustration produces aggression to the extent that it causes negative feeling, and cues such as weapons make an aggressive response more likely (the weapons effect, Berkowitz and Anthony LePage, 1967). Option c is right because most psychologists see aggression as largely learned, as Albert Bandura's social learning view holds; frustration is only one possible trigger, and it does not always lead to aggression. Exam trap: option d also sounds biological, but it is the hydraulic model of Konrad Lorenz (1966) and the death instinct of Sigmund Freud (1920), where aggressive energy builds up on its own and must be released, with or without frustration. Key note: the coaching pack keys a, but the stem is from Charles Morris and Albert Maisto's Psychology: An Introduction, whose text says 'Most psychologists believe that aggression is largely a learned behavior', in line with Albert Bandura's social learning view (Bobo doll studies, 1961; Aggression: A Social Learning Analysis, 1973). So c is used here.", "traps": {"a": "An innate biological response to frustration loosely echoes the frustration-aggression hypothesis of John Dollard and colleagues (1939), whose authors said they made no assumption about whether the link was innate or learned (Neal Miller, 1941). Frustration does not always lead to aggression, and most psychologists see aggression as largely learned, so this is not the majority view.", "b": "Linking aggression to the sex drive comes from early Sigmund Freud, who folded aggression into the sexual instincts before adding a separate death instinct in 1920. Hardly any modern psychologist holds it, and it says nothing about frustration.", "d": "A drive that builds up and must be released is the hydraulic instinct view of Konrad Lorenz (1966) and Freud's Thanatos. It treats aggression as spontaneous energy rather than a reaction to frustration, and catharsis research shows venting tends to raise aggression, not drain it."}}, {"q": "According to Alfred Adler, the prime motivating force in a person's life is:", "concept": "The answer is c: Alfred Adler held that the prime motivating force in every life is striving for superiority. Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) was a Viennese physician who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1911 and founded individual psychology. Where Freud put sex and aggression at the core, Adler put a single upward drive. His starting point was organ inferiority (Study of Organ Inferiority and Its Psychical Compensation, 1907): a weak organ pushes a person to compensate, as the Greek orator Demosthenes overcame a stammer. He then generalised the idea. Every child begins small and dependent among powerful adults, so inferiority feelings are universal, and they spur us to compensate by striving for superiority, meaning completion and perfection, not domination of others. Late in life he spoke of 'striving for perfection' and tied healthy striving to social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), concern for the community. When compensation fails the result is an inferiority complex; when it overshoots, a superiority complex. Example: Riya, the shortest girl in her class, trains hard until she becomes the school's best badminton player; Adler would call this healthy striving for superiority born of inferiority feelings. Option c is right because Adler named striving for superiority (later perfection) as the fundamental fact of life, the master motive behind all others. Exam trap: 'the need for power' tempts because an earlier version of Adler's idea (after his aggression drive of 1908) was the 'will to power', borrowed from the philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche (1883). Adler later dropped that wording for striving for superiority, which is the answer examiners expect.", "traps": {"a": "Physical gratification, the pleasure principle of the id, is Sigmund Freud's emphasis on sexual and bodily drives. Adler rejected this sexual focus in favour of goal-directed striving.", "b": "Existential anxiety, the dread of death, freedom and meaninglessness, belongs to existential thinkers such as Rollo May (1950) and Paul Tillich, not to Adler.", "d": "The need for power echoes Adler's early 'will to power' from Friedrich Nietzsche, and also David McClelland's need for power. Adler's mature named motive is striving for superiority, so d is a near miss."}}, {"q": "Skinner's theory of learning advocates:", "concept": "The answer is b: B. F. Skinner's theory of learning advocates operant conditioning. Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), an American behaviourist, set out the theory in The Behavior of Organisms (1938). He separated respondent behaviour, which is drawn out by a stimulus (as in Pavlov's salivating dogs), from operant behaviour, which the organism emits freely and which operates on the environment. In operant conditioning the likelihood of a response changes with its consequences. Reinforcement, positive (adding something pleasant) or negative (removing something unpleasant), makes the response more likely; punishment makes it less likely. Skinner built on the law of effect of Edward Thorndike (1898, 1911), studied rats and pigeons in the operant chamber or Skinner box, and showed how schedules of reinforcement control the rate and persistence of responding. Shaping, rewarding closer and closer steps towards a target, lets complex behaviour be built up. Example: whenever Kabir finishes his homework before dinner, his mother lets him play cricket, and finishing homework early becomes more frequent. That is operant conditioning by positive reinforcement. Option b is right because Skinner's learning theory explains how consequences control voluntary, emitted behaviour, which he named operant conditioning. Exam trap: Skinner did describe classical conditioning as respondent behaviour, so option a can look plausible, but classical conditioning is the work of Ivan Pavlov (early 1900s). Observational learning belongs to Albert Bandura (1961) and insight learning to Wolfgang Kohler (1925).", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning is Ivan Pavlov's learning by pairing a neutral stimulus with one that already triggers a reflex (bell with food). Skinner called this respondent learning and set it apart from his own operant theory.", "c": "Observational learning (here called observational conditioning) is Albert Bandura's learning by watching models, as in the Bobo doll studies (1961). Skinner explained imitation through reinforcement, not observation alone.", "d": "Insight learning is Wolfgang Kohler's (1925) Gestalt account of chimpanzees such as Sultan suddenly seeing a solution, like stacking boxes to reach bananas. It is the opposite of Skinner's gradual, consequence-driven learning."}}, {"q": "What is eclecticism?", "concept": "The answer is d: eclecticism means using techniques from many different sources, choosing whatever methods best fit a client instead of staying inside one school. The word comes from the Greek eklegein, to pick out or select. An eclectic therapist might use a behavioural technique for one problem, a cognitive one for another and a warm, accepting stance throughout. Surveys of practising therapists have long found 'eclectic' or 'integrative' to be the most common self-description. The best known model is the technical eclecticism of Arnold Lazarus (1932 to 2013), whose multimodal therapy assesses seven areas summed up as BASIC I.D. (Behaviour, Affect, Sensation, Imagery, Cognition, Interpersonal relationships, Drugs and biology) and borrows a technique from any school that has evidence for that area, without having to accept the school's theory. Eclecticism sits under the wider heading of psychotherapy integration, which also includes theoretical integration (fusing theories, as in the cyclical psychodynamics of Paul Wachtel, 1977) and common factors approaches. Example: Meera, a counsellor, treats a student with exam panic. She teaches breathing and graded exposure (behaviour therapy), questions catastrophic thoughts (the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck, 1979) and listens with empathy (Carl Rogers, 1951). She is working eclectically. Option d is right because eclecticism is by definition the selection and combination of methods drawn from several theories or schools. Exam trap: option b gives the exact opposite, strict loyalty to one school, which is a purist approach. Critics say careless eclecticism can become a vague grab-bag (option a), but vagueness is a criticism of it, not its definition.", "traps": {"a": "Vagueness is a criticism sometimes made of unsystematic eclecticism, not what the word means. The stem asks for the definition.", "b": "Adherence to one specific school is purism, the opposite of eclecticism; a classical analyst who uses only free association and interpretation is an example.", "c": "Shock therapy is electroconvulsive therapy, first given by Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini (1938). It is a physical treatment, unrelated to the meaning of eclecticism."}}, {"q": "Which of the following did Carl Rogers believe fosters a congruent self-concept?", "concept": "The answer is d: Carl Rogers believed that unconditional love, which he called unconditional positive regard, fosters a congruent self-concept. Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), the founder of person-centred therapy, set out his theory of personality in 1959. Every child has a need for positive regard: love and acceptance from important others. If parents give love only when the child behaves in approved ways, the child learns conditions of worth, such as 'I am worthwhile only when I come first in class' or 'only when I hide my anger'. The child then denies or distorts experiences that clash with these conditions, and the self-concept drifts away from actual experience. This gap is incongruence, which Rogers saw as the root of anxiety and maladjustment. When love is unconditional, the person is valued even when a particular behaviour is disapproved. With no conditions of worth to defend, the child can let all experiences into awareness, so self-concept and experience match. That match is congruence, the mark of Rogers' fully functioning person. Example: when Arjun fails his maths test, his mother says she is unhappy about the mark but still loves him. He can accept 'I failed' without feeling worthless, so his self-concept stays congruent. Option d is right because unconditional love is exactly the condition that prevents conditions of worth and so allows a congruent self. Exam trap: option a, conditional love, is the reverse, the very thing Rogers said creates incongruence. In therapy the counsellor supplies the same ingredient as one of the three core conditions, alongside empathy and genuineness (congruence).", "traps": {"a": "Conditional love is love given only for approved behaviour; Rogers said it creates conditions of worth and therefore incongruence, the opposite of what the stem asks.", "b": "Appropriate role models are central to Albert Bandura's (1977) social learning theory, where we learn by observing others. Rogers did not make modelling the source of a congruent self.", "c": "Immediate-need gratification is the id's pleasure principle in Sigmund Freud's theory. Rogers was concerned with acceptance of the whole person, not instant satisfaction of needs."}}, {"q": "_____ is distinguished for the major stages of cognitive development: the sensorimotor, the preoperational, the concrete operational and the formal operational stage.", "concept": "The answer is a: Jean Piaget is known for the four major stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational. Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) was a Swiss biologist turned psychologist who studied how children think by watching and questioning them, including his own three children. He saw children as active little scientists who build knowledge through schemes, adapting them by assimilation (fitting a new experience into an existing scheme) and accommodation (changing the scheme), driven by equilibration. The four stages come in a fixed order. Sensorimotor (birth to about 2): the infant learns through senses and actions and gains object permanence. Preoperational (about 2 to 7): symbols and language appear, but thought is egocentric and the child cannot conserve. Concrete operational (about 7 to 11): logical thinking about concrete objects, with conservation and classification. Formal operational (about 11 onwards): abstract and hypothetical reasoning. Example: four-year-old Riya thinks a tall thin glass holds more juice than a short wide one (preoperational); at eight she knows the amount is the same (concrete operational). Option a is right because these four named stages are the core of Piaget's theory, set out in books such as The Origins of Intelligence in Children (1936). Exam trap: the other three options are famous developmental figures too, so examiners rely on recall. Sigmund Freud (1905) also had stages, but psychosexual ones (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital), not cognitive ones.", "traps": {"b": "John Bowlby (1969) developed attachment theory, describing the infant's bond with a caregiver and the phases of attachment, not stages of thinking.", "c": "Sigmund Freud (1905) proposed psychosexual stages (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital) based on where libido is focused, not cognitive stages.", "d": "Harry Harlow (1958) showed with infant rhesus monkeys that contact comfort from a cloth mother mattered more than food from a wire mother. He studied attachment and love, not cognitive stages."}}, {"q": "Kohlberg's theory of moral development was criticised by Gilligan primarily because:", "concept": "The answer is b: Carol Gilligan criticised Lawrence Kohlberg's theory of moral development mainly because it was based on a study of boys only. Lawrence Kohlberg (1927 to 1987) built his six-stage theory from his 1958 doctoral study at the University of Chicago, in which he interviewed 72 boys aged 10, 13 and 16 about moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma, and then followed many of them for about 20 years. No girls or women were in the original sample. When the scoring system was later used with women, many scored at stage 3 (the good boy, good girl orientation), apparently below men, who more often reached stage 4. Carol Gilligan (born 1936), who had worked with Kohlberg at Harvard, argued in In a Different Voice (1982) that this showed bias, not a real deficit. Kohlberg's scale measured an ethic of justice (rights, rules, fairness), while many women reason from an ethic of care (relationships, responsibility, not hurting anyone). In her example, 11-year-old Jake solves the Heinz dilemma like a maths problem (life outweighs property), while Amy looks for a way for Heinz and the druggist to talk it out so nobody is hurt. Example: Meera decides whether to report her friend's cheating by thinking about how her friend and her friend's family will be affected; Gilligan would call this care reasoning, not a lower stage. Option b is right because Gilligan's central complaint was the all-male sample and the male standard it produced. Exam trap: a later review by Lawrence J. Walker (1984) found few real sex differences in Kohlberg scores, so option a can sound like a fair criticism; but that point was turned against Gilligan's claims, and the question asks why she criticised Kohlberg.", "traps": {"a": "That the data did not support the conclusions is a general methodological complaint, and Lawrence J. Walker's (1984) review actually raised it against Gilligan's own claim of sex differences. It is not her primary objection to Kohlberg.", "c": "The age of the study was never Gilligan's point; In a Different Voice (1982) came only about 24 years after Kohlberg's 1958 study.", "d": "Kohlberg's boys did come from the Chicago area, but Gilligan objected to the missing gender, not the urban setting. Cultural bias was raised by other critics, such as Richard Shweder and colleagues (1987) with their study in Odisha."}}, {"q": "The rapid increase in weight and height that occurs around the onset of puberty is known as the:", "concept": "The answer is c: the rapid gain in height and weight around the start of puberty is called the adolescent growth spurt. Puberty is the period of biological change that brings sexual maturity. It begins when the hypothalamus signals the pituitary gland, which in turn switches on the ovaries or testes to release oestrogens or testosterone. One of the first visible results is a sudden speeding up of growth. After slow, steady growth through middle childhood, a teenager may grow about 8 to 10 cm in the peak year. The spurt starts about two years earlier in girls (around 10 to 11) than in boys (around 12 to 13), which is why many girls in Class 6 or 7 are taller than the boys; the boys catch up and usually end taller. Growth is also uneven: hands and feet grow first, then arms and legs, then the trunk, which can make young teenagers look gangly for a while. Weight rises too, with more muscle in boys and more fat in girls. Example: Kabir is the shortest boy in his class at 12, then shoots up 10 cm in the year after he turns 13; that is his adolescent growth spurt. Option c is right because 'adolescent growth spurt' is the standard textbook term for the rapid rise in height and weight at the onset of puberty. Exam trap: menarche (option d) also happens at puberty and is often listed next to it, but it is the first menstrual period, a single event that usually comes after a girl's peak growth, not the growth itself.", "traps": {"a": "Maturation is the general biological unfolding of a genetic plan across the lifespan, stressed by Arnold Gesell (1920s). It is not the name of the rapid growth at puberty.", "b": "'Physical development stage' is not a technical term; physical development is a domain that runs from conception to old age, not a stage at puberty.", "d": "Menarche is a girl's first menstrual period, on average around 12 to 13, usually after her peak growth. It is one marker of puberty, not the rapid increase in height and weight."}}, {"q": "The _____ stage is characterised by an ability to use abstract concepts.", "concept": "The answer is b: the formal operational stage is characterised by the ability to use abstract concepts. It is the fourth and final stage in the theory of Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), beginning around age 11 or 12. Piaget and Barbel Inhelder described it in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence (1958). In the earlier concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11), children think logically, but only about real objects and events. In the formal operational stage thinking frees itself from the concrete. The adolescent can reason about abstract ideas such as justice, freedom or infinity, think about possibilities that do not exist, and use hypothetico-deductive reasoning: form a hypothesis, then test it systematically. In Inhelder and Piaget's pendulum task, the formal thinker varies one factor at a time (length of string, weight, height of release) to find what controls the speed of the swing, while a younger child changes several things at once. Formal thinkers can also judge an argument by its logic alone, even when its premises are false. Example: asked 'If all elephants were pink, and Raju is an elephant, what colour is Raju?', 14-year-old Riya answers 'pink' at once, while her 8-year-old brother insists that elephants are grey. Option b is right because abstract and hypothetical thought is the defining feature of Piaget's formal operational stage. Exam trap: 'concrete operational' sounds close because both stages are 'operational' and logical, but concrete operations are tied to real, tangible things. 'Operational' alone (option d) is not a stage name at all.", "traps": {"a": "The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) brings logic, conservation and classification, but only for concrete, real objects and events, not abstract ideas.", "c": "The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) brings symbols and language but is marked by egocentrism, centration and failure to conserve; abstract reasoning is far beyond it.", "d": "'Operational' alone is not one of Piaget's stages; it is a fragment of two real names, concrete operational and formal operational."}}, {"q": "A therapist who encourages clients to relate their dreams and searches for the unconscious roots of their problems is drawing from:", "concept": "The answer is a: a therapist who asks clients to report their dreams and searches for the unconscious roots of their problems is drawing on psychoanalysis. Psychoanalysis was developed by Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) in Vienna from the 1890s, after his work with Josef Breuer on hysteria (Studies on Hysteria, 1895). Its core assumption is that symptoms come from unconscious conflicts, often formed in childhood, that are kept out of awareness by repression and other defences. Treatment aims to make the unconscious conscious, so that the patient gains insight. Freud's main tools were free association (saying whatever comes to mind without censoring it), dream analysis, and the interpretation of resistance and of transference (feelings about parents shifted onto the analyst). In The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) he called the interpretation of dreams 'the royal road to the unconscious'. He separated the manifest content (the dream as remembered) from the latent content (its hidden wish), disguised by dream work such as condensation, displacement and symbolism. Example: Arjun tells his analyst he keeps dreaming of missing a train; through Arjun's associations the analyst links it to a buried fear of disappointing his father. Option a is right because dream reports and the search for unconscious causes are the trademark techniques of Freud's psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung (after his break with Freud in 1913) and Fritz Perls (Gestalt Therapy, 1951) also used dreams, but neither is offered here. Humanistic, person-centred and cognitive therapies work with present, conscious experience or thoughts, not unconscious roots.", "traps": {"b": "Humanistic therapies (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) focus on present, conscious experience, free will and growth rather than digging for unconscious childhood causes.", "c": "Person-centred therapy is Carl Rogers' (1951) humanistic method: the therapist offers empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness and follows the client's lead, without interpreting dreams or the unconscious.", "d": "Cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron T. Beck (1967, 1979), targets conscious automatic thoughts and distorted beliefs in the here and now, not unconscious roots revealed in dreams."}}, {"q": "Compared to psychoanalytic therapists, humanistic therapists are more likely to emphasise:", "concept": "The answer is d: compared with psychoanalytic therapists, humanistic therapists put far more weight on self-fulfilment and growth. Humanistic psychology arose in the United States in the 1950s and 1960s as a 'third force' against psychoanalysis and behaviourism. Its two leaders were Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) and Abraham Maslow (1908 to 1970). They held that people have free will and an inborn tendency to grow towards their full potential: Rogers called it the actualising tendency, and Maslow put self-actualisation at the top of his hierarchy of needs (1943, 1954). Problems arise when this growth is blocked, for example by conditions of worth. Therapy therefore looks at the client's present, conscious experience, seen from the client's own point of view, and tries to free that growth. In person-centred therapy (Rogers, 1951) the therapist offers empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness and trusts the client to find the way. A psychoanalyst, by contrast, looks backwards and inwards: to repressed feelings, unconscious conflict and early childhood, and interprets these for the patient. Example: Riya feels stuck in a career her parents chose. A psychoanalyst might explore her childhood relationship with her father; a humanistic counsellor would help her discover what she herself values and wants to become. Option d is right because self-fulfilment and personal growth are the central aims of humanistic therapy and are much less stressed in psychoanalysis. Exam trap: options a and b are real features of therapy, but they are psychoanalytic emphases; examiners reverse the comparison so that the psychoanalytic features look like humanistic ones.", "traps": {"a": "Hidden or repressed feelings are the focus of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, which tries to bring the repressed into awareness. Humanistic therapists work with feelings the client can reach now.", "b": "Childhood experiences are central to psychoanalysis, which traces adult problems to psychosexual conflicts in the early years. Humanistic therapy concentrates on the here and now.", "c": "Psychological disorders as diagnosable illnesses fit the medical model and, historically, psychoanalysis. Humanistic therapists avoid labels and see a whole person whose growth is blocked, not a disorder."}}, {"q": "Behaviour therapies apply learning principles to:", "concept": "The answer is c: behaviour therapies apply the principles of learning to eliminate unwanted behaviour (and to build desirable behaviour in its place). Behaviour therapy treats problem behaviour as learned, so it can be unlearned or replaced. It grew in the 1950s from three roots: the reciprocal inhibition and systematic desensitisation of Joseph Wolpe (1958) in South Africa, the group of Hans Eysenck (1952) at the Maudsley Hospital in London, and the operant work of B. F. Skinner (1953) in the United States. Behaviour therapists focus on the symptom itself, here and now, instead of hunting for hidden causes; Eysenck's motto was that if you get rid of the symptom you have eliminated the neurosis. A functional analysis asks what triggers the behaviour and what consequences keep it going. Classical conditioning methods include systematic desensitisation, exposure, flooding and aversion therapy; operant methods include token economies, shaping, extinction and time out; modelling, from Albert Bandura (1969), adds learning by observation. Example: Kabir is terrified of dogs. His therapist builds an anxiety hierarchy from a picture of a puppy up to stroking a real dog, and pairs each step with relaxation until the fear response is gone. Option c is right because the stated goal of behaviour therapy is to change, and usually remove, the unwanted behaviour through relearning. Exam trap: option a, treating the underlying causes of dysfunction, is the aim of psychoanalysis. Behaviourists argued that the symptoms are the disorder, and research has found little evidence that removing a symptom simply produces a new one (symptom substitution).", "traps": {"a": "Identifying and treating the underlying causes of dysfunction is the aim of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis and other insight therapies. Behaviour therapy targets the behaviour itself.", "b": "Improving learning and insight sounds close, but insight is the goal of psychodynamic therapy. Behaviour therapy uses learning principles as a tool for change, not to produce self-understanding.", "d": "Improving communication and social sensitivity describes sensitivity training and encounter groups (Kurt Lewin's T-groups, 1946) and interpersonal approaches. Social skills training exists in behaviour therapy, but it is one technique, not the general aim."}}, {"q": "An example of a cognitive therapy is rational-emotive therapy, which was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is c: rational-emotive therapy was developed by Albert Ellis. Albert Ellis (1913 to 2007), an American psychologist first trained in psychoanalysis, grew frustrated with its slowness and introduced rational therapy in 1955. It was later called rational-emotive therapy (RET) and, from 1993, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). He set it out in Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy (1962), and it is usually counted as the first of the modern cognitive therapies. Its centre is the ABC model. A is an activating event, B is the belief about it, C is the emotional and behavioural consequence. People assume A causes C, but Ellis argued that B does. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('I must succeed', 'others must treat me fairly'), awfulising, low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it') and global self-rating. The therapist then actively Disputes the belief (D), which leads to an Effective new philosophy (E). Example: Arjun fails one test (A) and feels worthless (C). Ellis would expose the belief 'I must never fail, or I am a total failure' (B) and dispute it until Arjun holds a preference instead: 'I would like to pass, but failing once does not make me worthless.' Option c is right because Ellis created RET, the classic cognitive therapy named in the stem. Exam trap: Aaron T. Beck (1967) developed the other main cognitive therapy (cognitive triad, automatic thoughts), and exam setters often swap the two. Joseph Wolpe (1958) belongs to behaviour therapy and Carl Rogers (1951) to person-centred therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Rogers (1951) developed client-centred, later person-centred, therapy, a humanistic approach that avoids the direct disputing Ellis used.", "b": "Joseph Wolpe (1958) developed systematic desensitisation based on reciprocal inhibition, a behaviour therapy rather than a cognitive one.", "d": "Allen Bergin is known for psychotherapy outcome research (the Handbook of Psychotherapy and Behavior Change, edited with Sol Garfield from 1971) and for work on religion and values in therapy, not for creating RET."}}, {"q": "The explicit and implicit rules that a society establishes to govern conduct are referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the explicit and implicit rules a society sets to govern conduct are called norms. A social norm is a shared expectation about how people should behave in a given situation. Explicit norms are stated openly, often in writing: laws, school rules, a 'No Smoking' sign. Implicit norms are unspoken, yet everyone knows them: you do not talk loudly in a temple, you face the door in a lift, and in many Indian families you touch an elder's feet. Norms make social life predictable, and breaking them brings sanctions, from a disapproving look to a fine. Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936) showed a group norm forming in the laboratory: people judging how far a still dot of light seemed to move in a dark room (the autokinetic effect) converged on a shared estimate, which they kept when later tested alone. Robert Cialdini, Raymond Reno and Carl Kallgren (1990) separated descriptive norms (what most people do) from injunctive norms (what people approve of). Sociologists also split norms into folkways (mild customs) and mores (strong moral rules), terms from William Graham Sumner (1906). Example: no written rule tells Meera to silence her phone in the cinema, but when it rings people glare; she has broken an implicit norm. Option a is right because 'norms' is the standard term for both the explicit and implicit rules that govern conduct in a society. Exam trap: culture (option b) contains norms but is much wider, the whole way of life of a group. Remember too that violating norms is what the social-norm criterion of abnormality calls deviance.", "traps": {"b": "Culture is the whole shared way of life of a group: values, beliefs, language, customs, art and technology. Norms are one part of culture, the rules for conduct, so culture is too broad.", "c": "Morality is a sense of right and wrong, studied by Jean Piaget and Lawrence Kohlberg. Many norms have nothing to do with morality (which side of the road to walk on), so morality is not the general term.", "d": "Conventions are one kind of norm, arbitrary customs agreed for convenience such as table manners; Elliot Turiel (1983) separated them from moral rules. The umbrella term for all explicit and implicit rules is norms."}}, {"q": "The violation of a society's explicit and implicit norms can be viewed as not normal in the sense that the violation is:", "concept": "The answer is a: behaviour that violates a society's explicit and implicit norms is called deviant. There is no single test for abnormality, so textbooks such as Abnormal Psychology by Ronald Comer (1992) sum up the usual features as the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Deviance means behaviour that is different, extreme, unusual or bizarre. It can be judged in two ways. The statistical criterion calls abnormal whatever is rare, far from the average; its flaw is that rare talent, such as an IQ of 150, would also count. The social-norm criterion calls abnormal whatever breaks a society's norms, both the explicit rules (laws) and the implicit ones (unspoken customs). Its flaw is cultural relativity: norms differ across cultures and eras. A possession trance at a temple festival may be accepted custom in one setting and a symptom in another, and homosexuality was listed as a disorder in DSM-II (1968) until 1973. So deviance alone never proves a disorder. Example: Kabir walks to college in winter wearing only shorts and talks loudly to himself on the bus. People stare because he is violating norms; his behaviour is deviant, but a clinician would still ask whether he is distressed, dysfunctional or dangerous. Option a is right because 'deviant' is the term for breaking a society's norms. Exam trap: the other three Ds are also features of abnormality, so all four options look correct. The test is which D the stem defines: norm violation is deviance, suffering is distress, inability to cope with daily life is dysfunction, and risk of harm is danger.", "traps": {"b": "Distress is the D that covers unpleasant, upsetting feelings such as unhappiness and anxiety, the person's own suffering. Breaking norms can happen without any distress.", "c": "Dysfunction is behaviour that interferes with daily functioning: work, study, relationships and self-care. A norm-breaker may function perfectly well.", "d": "Danger to self or others covers suicidal or violent risk. Most norm violations, such as odd dress, carry no danger at all."}}, {"q": "Which aspect of the definition of abnormality includes unhappiness?", "concept": "The answer is a: the part of the definition of abnormality that includes unhappiness is distress. Distress refers to unpleasant, upsetting feelings: sadness, unhappiness, anxiety, fear, guilt or emotional pain. It is one of the four Ds that Ronald Comer (1992) uses to sum up abnormality, along with deviance, dysfunction and danger. The personal distress criterion treats behaviour as abnormal when it causes the person great suffering. It matters because many disorders, such as depression and the anxiety disorders, are felt mainly as suffering, and DSM-5 (2013) builds 'clinically significant distress or impairment' into its definition of a mental disorder. But distress is neither necessary nor sufficient on its own. Some people with a disorder feel little distress: a person in a manic episode may feel wonderful, and someone with antisocial personality disorder may feel no guilt. And distress can be normal: grief after a parent's death, or nerves before a viva, is not a disorder. That is why clinicians weigh all four Ds together and ask whether the distress is out of proportion to its cause and lasting. Example: Riya has felt deeply unhappy and hopeless for two months since moving to a new city, cries most evenings and has lost interest in everything. Her unhappiness is the distress feature of abnormality. Option a is right because unhappiness is a form of personal suffering, which is exactly what the distress criterion describes. Exam trap: dysfunction can sound close, because unhappy people often function worse, but dysfunction refers to impaired daily functioning (work, study, relationships, self-care), not to the feeling itself.", "traps": {"b": "Deviance is behaviour that is different, extreme or that violates social norms; it concerns how the behaviour looks to others, not how the person feels.", "c": "Dysfunction means behaviour that interferes with daily life, such as being unable to study, work or care for oneself; it is about impaired functioning, not unhappiness.", "d": "Danger to self or others covers suicidal or violent risk. It is the least common of the four Ds and has nothing specific to do with unhappiness."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not true regarding people with a mental illness?", "concept": "The answer is c: the statement that is not true is that people with a mental illness usually pose a danger to themselves or others. Danger is one of the four Ds of abnormality described by Ronald Comer (1992), with deviance, distress and dysfunction, but it applies to the fewest people. Most people with a mental illness are never violent. The MacArthur Violence Risk Assessment Study (Henry Steadman and colleagues, 1998) found that discharged psychiatric patients without substance abuse were no more violent than other people in their neighbourhoods; the strongest predictors of violence were substance misuse and past violence, not diagnosis itself. People with severe mental illness are in fact more often victims of violence than perpetrators. Suicide risk is real and raised in depression, but it still affects a minority. The image of the 'dangerous madman' comes largely from news stories and films, and it feeds stigma, which makes people delay seeking help. The other three statements are broadly true: many people with mental illness are in real distress; society, at least in principle, views them as in need of help and treatment; and their behaviour usually differs in some way from what is typical, which is the deviance feature. Example: Arjun's neighbours are frightened when they learn he has schizophrenia, though he has never harmed anyone and is far more likely to be bullied than to hurt someone. Option c is right because 'usually dangerous' is a well-known myth, contradicted by research on violence and mental illness. Exam trap: the word 'usually' is what matters; danger is a real criterion of abnormality, but it fits only a small number of people. Key note: The printed answer in the coaching copy is a, but distress is common in mental illness and the statement that is clearly not true is c, so c is used here.", "traps": {"a": "This is true: distress, such as sadness, anxiety and fear, is one of the four Ds and is common in mental illness. So it cannot be the statement that is not true.", "b": "Broadly true: modern society, through the medical and mental health models, views people with mental illness as needing help and treatment, even though stigma persists.", "d": "True in general: the deviance criterion says abnormal behaviour differs in some way from what is typical or expected, so 'usually different behaviour' fits mental illness."}}, {"q": "Dementia is a condition that is characterised by:", "concept": "The answer is c: dementia is characterised by a general intellectual decline. Dementia is an acquired, usually progressive loss of cognitive abilities from a previously higher level, severe enough to interfere with independence in daily life. It is not present from birth (that is intellectual disability), and it is not a normal part of ageing. Memory is usually hit first, especially for recent events, followed by language (aphasia), skilled movement (apraxia), recognition (agnosia), judgement, abstract thinking and planning. In DSM-5 (2013) the term dementia was replaced by major neurocognitive disorder: a significant decline in one or more of six domains (complex attention, executive function, learning and memory, language, perceptual-motor function and social cognition) that costs independence; mild neurocognitive disorder is the milder form. The commonest cause is Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), with amyloid plaques and tau tangles; others include vascular, Lewy body and frontotemporal dementia. Unlike delirium, dementia develops slowly and consciousness stays clear. Example: Meera's grandfather, a retired teacher, first forgot recent conversations, then lost his way in his own lane and could no longer manage his pension papers; his doctor diagnosed Alzheimer's dementia. Option c is right because a broad, acquired decline in intellect and cognition is the defining feature of dementia. Exam trap: general paresis (option a) did cause dementia in the 1800s, but it is neurosyphilis, one specific cause, not the definition. Loss of contact with reality (option d) is psychosis, the hallmark of schizophrenia.", "traps": {"a": "General paresis (general paralysis of the insane) is the late brain stage of syphilis; Hideyo Noguchi (1913) found the syphilis germ in the brains of such patients. It can produce dementia, but it is one cause, not the defining feature.", "b": "Physical ailments are bodily illnesses. Dementia often comes from brain disease, but it is defined by cognitive decline, not by physical complaints.", "d": "Loss of contact with reality is psychosis, seen in schizophrenia and severe mood disorders. People with early dementia usually stay in contact with reality while their memory and reasoning fail."}}, {"q": "The ancient Greek physicians characterised hysteria as:", "concept": "The answer is d: the ancient Greek physicians described hysteria as a physical ailment without any real physical cause. Hysteria meant dramatic bodily symptoms, such as fits, paralysis, loss of voice, a choking feeling or blindness, with no disease that could account for them. The word comes from the Greek hystera, the uterus. Hippocrates (born about 460 BC) and his followers, and later Plato in the Timaeus, thought the womb could wander through a woman's body, pressing on other organs and causing symptoms; treatments included marriage, pregnancy and scents to coax the womb back into place. So hysteria was pictured as a bodily illness, but with no actual damage to the part that seemed affected, and it was long thought to strike only women. In the 1800s Jean-Martin Charcot (1825 to 1893) at the Salpêtrière showed that hysterical symptoms could be produced and removed by hypnosis, and Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895) explained them as psychological conflict 'converted' into bodily form. Today the label is gone: DSM-5 (2013) calls these presentations conversion disorder, or functional neurological symptom disorder, and they occur in men too. Example: after a frightening accident, Riya suddenly cannot move her right arm, yet every scan and nerve test is normal; the Greeks would have called this hysteria. Option d is right because it captures the defining feature: real-seeming physical complaints without an underlying bodily disease. Exam trap: options b and c describe 'hysterical' in today's everyday sense, being emotionally out of control, which is not the classical medical meaning. Key note: the answer means physical complaints with no real bodily disease, which is how the term hysteria came to be used. Strictly, the Greeks did name a physical cause, the wandering womb, but no actual organ damage was ever found, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Euphoric behaviour is elevated, excessive happiness, typical of what the Greeks called mania. It is not what hysteria meant.", "b": "Uncontrollable sobbing is the modern everyday sense of 'hysterical', being emotionally overwhelmed. The Greek medical term referred to bodily symptoms without disease.", "c": "An inability to control emotional responses is again the popular meaning of 'hysterical' today, closer to emotional dysregulation. The classical concept was about physical complaints, not emotions."}}, {"q": "What model of mental illness did most people hold during the Middle Ages?", "concept": "The answer is d: none of these, because in the Middle Ages most people held a supernatural, demonological model of mental illness, which is not among the options. Demonology is the belief that abnormal behaviour is caused by evil spirits, the devil or witchcraft. It is much older than the Middle Ages: some Stone Age skulls show trephining, holes cut perhaps to let a spirit out. Hippocrates (born about 460 BC) and later Galen (born AD 129) had replaced it with natural, bodily causes. But in medieval Europe, after the fall of Rome, the Church dominated thought and explanations swung back to possession and sin. Treatment was exorcism: prayer, holy water and at times starving or flogging to drive the demon out. There were outbreaks of mass madness, such as tarantism in Italy and St Vitus's dance. Late in the period came the witch hunts: the Malleus Maleficarum (1486) of Heinrich Kramer and Jacob Sprenger was a manual for finding and punishing witches, and many disturbed people were among the accused. The physician Johann Weyer (1563) argued that many so-called witches were mentally ill, an early step back towards a medical view. Modern historians point out that medieval views were mixed and that the worst witch hunts came in the 1500s and 1600s, but textbooks sum up the era as demonological. Example: in a medieval village, if Meera's grandmother began hearing voices, the priest would be called to cast out the demon, not a physician. Option d is right because the dominant medieval view, demonology, is not the moral, medical or psychogenic model. Exam trap: the medical model sounds historical because the Greeks used it, but the Middle Ages largely abandoned it; the moral model is moral treatment, which came much later with Philippe Pinel (1793).", "traps": {"a": "The moral model, moral treatment, belongs to the late 1700s and 1800s: Philippe Pinel (1793) in Paris and William Tuke (1796, the York Retreat) treated patients with kindness, work and respect. It came after the Middle Ages.", "b": "The medical (somatogenic) model of Hippocrates and Galen sees mental illness as bodily disease. It dominated ancient Greek medicine and returned from the Renaissance onwards, but most medieval people did not hold it.", "c": "The psychogenic model, that mental illness has psychological causes, arose in the late 1800s with Jean-Martin Charcot's hypnosis work and Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis."}}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled by the:", "concept": "The answer is c: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan or the knee jerk when a doctor taps just below the kneecap. The pathway it follows is the reflex arc, analysed in detail by Charles Sherrington in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System (1906), work that later earned him a Nobel Prize (1932). In a typical spinal reflex the signal runs from a receptor in the skin, along a sensory (afferent) neuron entering the spinal cord through the dorsal root, to an interneuron in the grey matter of the cord, then out along a motor (efferent) neuron through the ventral root to the effector muscle. Because the loop closes inside the spinal cord, the brain is not needed to start the response, so the hand moves before you feel the pain; the message reaches the brain a moment later. The withdrawal reflex is polysynaptic (it uses interneurons), while the knee jerk, a stretch reflex, is monosynaptic: the sensory neuron synapses directly on the motor neuron. Example: Arjun touches a hot tawa while making rotis and jerks his hand away, and only then says 'ouch'. His spinal cord ran the reflex before his brain registered the pain. Option c is right because most simple body reflexes are organised in the spinal cord, without needing the brain. Exam trap: the medulla does run some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, swallowing and vomiting, and controls breathing and heart rate, so option a is tempting; but the word 'usually' points to the spinal cord, which handles the classic limb reflexes.", "traps": {"a": "The medulla oblongata in the hindbrain controls vital functions (breathing, heart rate) and some brainstem reflexes such as coughing, sneezing and swallowing. Most body reflexes, though, are spinal.", "b": "The frontal lobe handles planning, decision making and voluntary movement (it contains the primary motor cortex). Reflexes happen without it, which is why they are automatic.", "d": "The hypothalamus regulates hunger, thirst, temperature, sex and the pituitary gland to keep homeostasis. It does not run the fast reflex arcs of the limbs."}}, {"q": "A part of the brain that sends 'alert' signals to higher centres of the brain in response to incoming messages is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the reticular formation sends 'alert' signals to the higher centres of the brain in response to incoming messages. The reticular formation is a net-like web of about 100 small nuclei running through the core of the brainstem, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain (reticulum means 'little net'). Its ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), receives branches from almost every sensory pathway and projects through the thalamus to the whole cortex. It controls arousal, wakefulness and attention, and acts as a filter, letting important signals rouse the cortex while screening out dull, repetitive ones. That is why a parent can sleep through traffic noise yet wake at the baby's cry. Damage to it can cause coma. The classic study came from Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949): electrically stimulating the reticular formation of drowsy or lightly anaesthetised cats switched their EEG from slow waves to the fast pattern of alertness. Hans Eysenck (1967) later built his biological theory of extraversion on differences in this arousal system. Example: Kabir is half asleep in a lecture, but when the teacher says his name, his reticular formation alerts his cortex and he sits up at once. Option b is right because arousing the higher brain in response to sensory input is the defining job of the reticular formation and its activating system. Exam trap: the limbic system, amygdala and hippocampus are real structures listed together to confuse. The amygdala handles fear and emotion, the hippocampus forms new memories, and the limbic system is the emotional circuit as a whole, not the alerting system.", "traps": {"a": "The limbic system (James Papez's circuit, 1937; named by Paul MacLean, 1952) is the ring of structures around the brainstem that handles emotion, motivation and memory. It does not send general alert signals to the cortex.", "c": "The amygdala, part of the limbic system in the temporal lobe, detects threat and drives fear and emotional learning (Joseph LeDoux, 1996). It responds to danger, not to every incoming message.", "d": "The hippocampus consolidates new explicit memories and spatial maps; patient H.M. (Henry Molaison) could not form new long-term memories after its removal in 1953. It is not the brain's arousal system."}}, {"q": "Perceptual constancies are:", "concept": "The answer is d: perceptual constancies help us perceive a stable and consistent world. Perceptual constancy is the tendency to see objects as keeping the same size, shape, brightness and colour even though the image they cast on the retina keeps changing. Without it, the world would seem to shrink, stretch and flicker every time we moved. Size constancy: a friend walking away casts a smaller and smaller retinal image, yet she does not seem to shrink, because the brain scales retinal size by perceived distance (size-distance invariance, shown in Emmert's law by Emil Emmert, 1881). Shape constancy: an opening door projects a trapezium on the retina, yet we see a rectangle. Brightness (lightness) constancy: coal in bright sunlight reflects more light than white paper in shade, yet still looks black, because we judge the ratio of an object's light to that of its surroundings. Colour constancy: a red apple looks red under yellow kitchen light and in blue daylight. Some constancy is present early in infancy (Tom Bower, 1966, found size constancy in babies of 6 to 8 weeks) and experience refines it. Example: as Meera watches her brother's bus drive away, its image on her retina gets tiny, but she still sees a full-sized bus moving farther off, not a toy bus. Option d is right because the function of perceptual constancies is to give a stable, consistent picture of a world whose sensory input is always changing. Exam trap: option a describes illusions. Constancy can produce illusions when distance cues mislead (the Ponzo and moon illusions), but constancy itself is an aid to accurate perception, not an error.", "traps": {"a": "This is the definition of an illusion, a perception that does not match the physical reality, such as the Muller-Lyer illusion (Franz Muller-Lyer, 1889). Constancy is the opposite: it keeps perception true to the real object.", "b": "Constancies reduce confusion rather than cause it; they help the perceiver work out what really exists despite changing retinal images.", "c": "Constancies are partly present early in infancy but are shaped by experience. Colin Turnbull (1961) reported that Kenge, a BaMbuti man from the dense forest, saw distant buffalo on open plains as insects, showing that size constancy at long distances depends on learning."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a subdivision of the autonomic nervous system?", "concept": "The answer is a: the autonomic nervous system has two main subdivisions, the sympathetic and the parasympathetic nervous systems. The nervous system first divides into the central nervous system (CNS), the brain and spinal cord, and the peripheral nervous system (PNS), all the nerves outside them. The PNS has two parts: the somatic nervous system, which carries sensations in and commands out to the skeletal muscles under voluntary control, and the autonomic nervous system (ANS), which runs the internal organs, smooth muscle, heart and glands largely automatically. John Newport Langley (1898) coined the term autonomic and later named the parasympathetic division. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action, the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915): the heart speeds up, pupils widen, airways open, digestion slows and the liver releases glucose. Its fibres leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord. The parasympathetic division does the reverse, rest and digest: it slows the heart, narrows the pupils and restarts digestion, conserving energy. Its fibres leave the brainstem (mainly through the vagus nerve) and the sacral cord. The two usually work in opposition to keep balance (homeostasis). Example: Riya's heart pounds as she walks into her viva (sympathetic); afterwards, over lunch, her heart rate drops and she digests her food calmly (parasympathetic). Option a is right because the ANS is made up of both divisions, not just one. Exam trap: options b and c each name only one half, a trap of incompleteness. Option d, the brain and spinal cord, is the central nervous system, not part of the ANS at all. Many books add the enteric nervous system of the gut wall as a third autonomic division.", "traps": {"b": "The sympathetic system (fight or flight) is one subdivision of the ANS, but 'only the sympathetic' leaves out the parasympathetic half.", "c": "The parasympathetic system (rest and digest) is also only half of the ANS, so 'only the parasympathetic' is incomplete.", "d": "The brain and spinal cord make up the central nervous system. The ANS is part of the peripheral nervous system, so this option names the wrong system entirely."}}, {"q": "Reports on the sexual behaviour of men were published by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the reports on the sexual behaviour of men were published by Alfred Kinsey. Alfred Charles Kinsey (1948) was an American zoologist at Indiana University who had spent years classifying gall wasps before he turned to human sexuality. With Wardell Pomeroy and Clyde Martin he published Sexual Behavior in the Human Male (1948), based on detailed face-to-face interviews (sex histories) with thousands of men. A companion volume, Sexual Behavior in the Human Female (1953), added Paul Gebhard as an author. Together they are called the Kinsey Reports. Their main finding was that ordinary sexual behaviour was far more varied than people admitted. Same-sex experience, for example, was common and graded, which Kinsey captured in his 0 to 6 scale, from exclusively heterosexual (0) to exclusively homosexual (6). Critics pointed out that his samples were volunteers rather than random, so some percentages may be inflated, but the reports changed public debate and made sex research a science. Option b is right because the stem asks for published reports on men's sexual behaviour, which were survey data, not a theory. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud (1905) also wrote about sexuality, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, so examiners use him as bait; but Freud built a theory of infantile sexuality from clinical cases and never surveyed men's sexual behaviour. Do not confuse Kinsey's interview surveys with William Masters and Virginia Johnson (1966), who observed the physiology of the sexual response cycle in the laboratory.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud wrote Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905), a theory of infantile sexuality and psychosexual stages drawn from case material, not a survey report on how men actually behave sexually.", "c": "Anna Freud, Sigmund Freud's daughter, is known for The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936) and for child analysis, not for research on sexual behaviour.", "d": "Karen Horney was a neo-Freudian who answered penis envy with womb envy and wrote on basic anxiety and feminine psychology; she published no surveys of men's sexual behaviour."}}, {"q": "_____ proposed rational emotive therapy.", "concept": "The answer is b: rational emotive therapy was proposed by Albert Ellis. Albert Ellis (1955) was an American clinical psychologist who trained in psychoanalysis but found that insight alone rarely made his clients better. Drawing on Stoic philosophy, especially the first-century Stoic Epictetus (people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them), he began 'rational therapy' in 1955. It was later renamed rational emotive therapy (RET) and, in 1993, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), to recognise its behavioural homework. His classic book is Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy (1962), and REBT is usually called the first cognitive-behavioural therapy. Its core is the ABC model: A is the activating event, B the belief about it, and C the emotional and behavioural consequence. Common sense says A causes C; Ellis said B is the main cause. Therapy adds D, disputing the irrational belief, and E, the new effective belief. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('I must', 'others should'), awfulising and low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it'). Example: Arjun scores poorly in a mock test (A). He believes 'I must never fail, or I am worthless' (B), so he feels depressed and stops studying (C). His therapist disputes the 'must' until Arjun holds a flexible preference: 'I would like to do well, but one test does not define me.' Option b is right because RET and its ABC model are Ellis's own creation. Exam trap: examiners swap Ellis with Aaron Beck (1967), who developed cognitive therapy with the cognitive triad and automatic thoughts. Ellis disputes beliefs directly and forcefully; Beck tests thoughts collaboratively, like a scientist.", "traps": {"a": "Joseph Wolpe (1958) developed systematic desensitisation from reciprocal inhibition, pairing relaxation with an anxiety hierarchy; it is a behaviour therapy, not a method of disputing beliefs.", "c": "Carl Rogers founded client-centred (person-centred) therapy, built on empathy, congruence and unconditional positive regard; he avoided directive disputing of a client's beliefs.", "d": "Jean-Paul Sartre was a French existentialist philosopher ('existence precedes essence', Being and Nothingness, 1943), not a therapist; existential therapy draws on him, but rational emotive therapy does not."}}, {"q": "_____ published the cognitive theory and therapy for depression.", "concept": "The answer is a: the cognitive theory and therapy for depression was published by Aaron Beck. Aaron Temkin Beck (1967) was a psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania, trained in psychoanalysis. In the late 1950s he tried to show that depressed patients' dreams contained anger turned inward, as Freudian theory predicted. Instead he found themes of loss and defeat, and he noticed a stream of quick, self-critical automatic thoughts that came just before his patients' sad feelings. He set out the theory in Depression (1967) and the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979), written with John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery. He also built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961), still a standard self-report scale. The model has three levels: core beliefs or schemas formed in childhood ('I am unlovable'), intermediate beliefs or rules ('If I am not perfect, I will be rejected'), and automatic thoughts. At its centre is the cognitive triad: a negative view of the self, the world and the future. Cognitive distortions keep it going, such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, arbitrary inference and personalisation. Example: Meera's friend forgets to call her back. Her automatic thoughts are 'Nobody really likes me' (self), 'people always let me down' (world) and 'it will always be like this' (future). In therapy she tests each thought against the evidence, a method Beck called collaborative empiricism. Option a is right because Aaron Beck is the only real cognitive therapist among the options. Exam trap: the other three are invented Becks built on the famous surname. Do not confuse Aaron with his daughter Judith S. Beck (Cognitive Therapy: Basics and Beyond, 1995), and remember the triad is self, world and future, not past, present and future.", "traps": {"b": "William Beck is not a known figure in cognitive therapy; the name is a distractor built on the famous surname.", "c": "Carl Beck has no link to the cognitive theory of depression; the first name is borrowed to tempt candidates who recall only 'Beck' (and may half-remember Carl Rogers).", "d": "John Beck has no link to cognitive therapy; Aaron Beck's co-author on the 1979 manual was John Rush, which may make this name feel familiar."}}, {"q": "_____ developed a social adjustment rating scale to measure life stress.", "concept": "The answer is d: the life-stress scale was developed by Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe. Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe (1967), two American psychiatrists at the University of Washington, published the Social Readjustment Rating Scale (SRRS); the stem's 'social adjustment rating scale' is a slightly garbled version of that name. Their idea was that any life change, good or bad, forces a person to readjust, and that piling up readjustment strains the body and raises the risk of illness. The scale lists 43 life events, each weighted in life change units (LCU). Death of a spouse is the anchor at 100; divorce scores 73, marriage 50, being fired at work 47, a change in residence 20 and a minor violation of the law 11. Even pleasant events such as a holiday or Christmas count, because change itself is the stressor. A person adds up the units for the past year, and high totals (above about 300) were linked with a greater chance of illness in the following period. Example: in one year Kabir marries (50), loses his job (47) and moves house (20), so he scores 117 LCU, even though one of those events was happy. Critics note that the scale ignores how a person appraises an event, and Allen Kanner, Richard Lazarus and colleagues (1981) found that daily hassles predict symptoms better than major events. Option d is right because Holmes and Rahe built the best-known scale of life events. Exam trap: Thomas Szasz (1961) is tempting because he wrote on psychiatry and society, but his book was The Myth of Mental Illness and he built no stress scale; also, the SRRS measures major life events, not daily hassles.", "traps": {"a": "Joseph Wolpe (1958) developed systematic desensitisation and used subjective units of distress to rank fears in an anxiety hierarchy; he did not build a life-events scale.", "b": "Carl Rogers founded client-centred therapy and studied the gap between the real and ideal self (often measured with Q-sorts); he did not measure life stress.", "c": "Thomas Szasz was a critic of psychiatry who argued in The Myth of Mental Illness (1961) that mental illness is a metaphor for problems in living; he made no stress scale."}}, {"q": "Who is the founder of Positive Psychology?", "concept": "The answer is d: the founder of positive psychology is Martin Seligman. Martin Elias Peter Seligman (1998) launched positive psychology in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association in 1998. He argued that psychology had spent decades repairing damage (depression, anxiety, disorder) and had neglected what makes life worth living. Positive psychology is the scientific study of positive experiences (happiness, hope, flow), positive traits (character strengths and virtues) and positive institutions (families, schools and workplaces that help people thrive). With Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (2000), the researcher of flow, he edited a special issue of American Psychologist that set out the field. Seligman later proposed the PERMA model of well-being (2011): positive emotion, engagement, relationships, meaning and accomplishment. Earlier in his career he had discovered learned helplessness (1967) in dogs given inescapable shocks, and he moved from there to learned optimism. Example: instead of only treating Riya's exam anxiety, a positive psychologist would also build her strengths, gratitude and sense of meaning. Option d is right because Seligman founded the movement and named its agenda. Exam trap: Abraham Maslow (1954) had used the phrase 'positive psychology' as a chapter title in Motivation and Personality, and humanistic psychology was a forerunner, but the founder of the modern field is Seligman.", "traps": {"a": "Howard Gardner (1983) proposed the theory of multiple intelligences in Frames of Mind; he did not found positive psychology.", "b": "Steven Pinker is a cognitive and evolutionary psychologist known for The Language Instinct (1994) and How the Mind Works (1997), not for positive psychology.", "c": "Albert Bandura developed social learning theory (Bobo doll, 1961) and self-efficacy (1977); self-efficacy is used in positive psychology, but Bandura did not found the field."}}, {"q": "The theory of multiple intelligences was propounded by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the theory of multiple intelligences was propounded by Howard Gardner. Howard Gardner (1983), a Harvard developmental psychologist, set out the theory in Frames of Mind. He argued that intelligence is not one general ability (the g of Charles Spearman, 1904) but several relatively independent intelligences. His original seven were linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal. He added naturalist intelligence in 1999 and considered, without fully accepting, an existential intelligence. Gardner did not rely on factor analysis. He used other evidence: abilities that are lost on their own after brain damage, savants and prodigies who excel in one area, separate developmental histories and distinct symbol systems. Critics reply that most of these abilities correlate with one another and with g, and that some are better called talents. Example: Meera struggles with algebra but sings perfectly in tune and reads her friends' moods well; Gardner would say her musical and interpersonal intelligences are strong even though her logical-mathematical intelligence is weaker. Option c is right because Frames of Mind and the multiple intelligences are Gardner's work. Exam trap: Louis Thurstone (1938) also listed seven abilities, his primary mental abilities, but he found them by factor analysis; and Robert Sternberg's (1985) triarchic theory has three parts (analytical, creative, practical), not multiple intelligences.", "traps": {"a": "Martin Seligman founded positive psychology (1998) and described learned helplessness; he did not propose multiple intelligences.", "b": "Albert Bandura is known for social learning theory (Bobo doll, 1961) and self-efficacy (1977), not for a theory of intelligence.", "d": "Option d is blank in the printed paper."}}, {"q": "The study of language and mind is related to:", "concept": "The answer is d: the study of language and mind is linked with Noam Chomsky. Noam Chomsky (1957) is an American linguist at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology whose Syntactic Structures (1957) began transformational generative grammar. His book Language and Mind (1968) argued that language is a window on the mind: the rules of grammar are mental structures, and the capacity to acquire them is innate. In his 1959 review of Burrhus Frederic Skinner's Verbal Behavior (1957) he argued that reinforcement cannot explain language, because children produce sentences they have never heard and make errors no adult models ('I goed'). He proposed a language acquisition device (LAD) holding a universal grammar, the principles shared by all human languages, and argued from the 'poverty of the stimulus': the speech children hear is too sparse to teach them the grammar they reach. This attack on behaviourism helped launch the cognitive revolution. Example: three-year-old Kabir says 'two foots', a form no one taught him; Chomsky would say he is applying a rule his mind has extracted. Option d is right because Language and Mind is the title of Chomsky's book and the idea behind his whole programme. Exam trap: Steven Pinker (1994) is the near-miss, because his The Language Instinct also defends an innate language faculty. Key note: Pinker also works on language and mind, but 'Language and Mind' is Chomsky's book title, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Gessmann is not a recognised name in the psychology of language; it looks like a misprint, and no psychologist of that name wrote on language and mind.", "b": "Bovver is not a recognised psychologist either; it appears to be a misprinted name with no link to the study of language and mind.", "c": "Steven Pinker wrote The Language Instinct (1994) and How the Mind Works (1997) and builds on Chomsky's nativism, but the classic work titled Language and Mind is Chomsky's."}}, {"q": "_____ is called the Marco Polo of Neuroscience.", "concept": "The answer is b: the 'Marco Polo of neuroscience' is V. S. Ramachandran. Vilayanur Subramanian Ramachandran (born 1951) is an Indian-born neuroscientist who directs the Center for Brain and Cognition at the University of California, San Diego. The biologist Richard Dawkins (The Selfish Gene, 1976) called him 'a latter-day Marco Polo', because, like the explorer, he travels into strange territory, here the odd syndromes of the brain, and comes back with simple, striking findings. His best-known work is on phantom limbs. In the 1990s he touched the face of an arm amputee with a cotton bud, and the man felt the touch on specific fingers of the missing hand. Ramachandran explained this as cortical reorganisation: in the somatosensory cortex the face area lies next to the hand area, so it invades the hand's unused territory. He then invented the mirror box, in which the reflection of the intact hand makes the brain 'see' the phantom move, easing phantom pain. He also studied synaesthesia and Capgras delusion and wrote Phantoms in the Brain (1998, with Sandra Blakeslee). Example: after losing his left arm in an accident, Arjun feels his missing thumb whenever his cheek is touched; this is the remapping Ramachandran described. Option b is right because the Marco Polo description belongs to him. Exam trap: the other three are names Indian students also meet in exams, but in very different fields: psychiatry textbooks or sex therapy (Kaplan), an Indian socio-economic status scale (Kuppuswamy) and organisational psychology (Udai Pareek).", "traps": {"a": "Kaplan most likely points to Harold Kaplan, co-author with Benjamin Sadock of the Synopsis of Psychiatry, or to Helen Singer Kaplan, the sex therapist (triphasic model, 1979); neither is called the Marco Polo of neuroscience.", "c": "B. Kuppuswamy is known for the Kuppuswamy socio-economic status scale used widely in Indian research, not for neuroscience.", "d": "Udai Pareek was an Indian organisational psychologist known for role stress and organisational culture (OCTAPACE) research, not for neuroscience."}}, {"q": "In which year was the first department of psychology in India established?", "concept": "The answer is b: the first department of psychology in India was set up in 1916. It was the Department of Experimental Psychology at the University of Calcutta, founded under the vice-chancellor Sir Ashutosh Mukherjee with support from the philosopher Brajendranath Seal. It was led by Narendra Nath Sengupta (1889 to 1944), who had trained at Harvard under Hugo Munsterberg (brought to Harvard in 1892), himself a student of Wilhelm Wundt (1879, the Leipzig laboratory). So the department began with experimental, laboratory psychology on the German and American model. Sengupta set up India's first psychology laboratory there in 1915, and the separate department followed. Girindrasekhar Bose, later the father of psychoanalysis in India, joined it and later headed it. Sengupta is called the father of modern psychology in India. Other universities followed: Mysore began psychology in 1924 under M. V. Gopalaswamy, then Madras and Pune. Option b is right because 1916 fits the founding of the Calcutta department and no other option comes near it. Exam trap: 1924 is the Mysore date (and close to the founding of the Indian Psychological Association), and 1922 is when Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society; examiners swap these dates. Key note: sources differ, many giving 1915 (the year the laboratory opened) and some 1916; 1916 is the printed answer and the closest option, so it stands.", "traps": {"a": "1912 is too early: no Indian university had a psychology laboratory or department then; Calcutta's laboratory dates from 1915.", "c": "1922 is the year Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in Calcutta, not the year of the first department.", "d": "1924 is when psychology began at Mysore under M. V. Gopalaswamy, and close to the founding of the Indian Psychological Association; Mysore was the second centre, not the first."}}, {"q": "The Indian Psychological Association was formed in:", "concept": "The answer is c: the Indian Psychological Association was formed in 1924. It grew out of the Indian Science Congress, and its first president was Narendra Nath Sengupta (1889 to 1944), head of the Calcutta department and the father of modern psychology in India. The association gave Indian psychologists a national body for meetings and publication, and its Indian Journal of Psychology appeared in the mid-1920s (1925 or 1926, sources differ), closely linked with Girindrasekhar Bose (1887 to 1953) of Calcutta. Keep the early Indian timeline in order: Calcutta laboratory 1915, Calcutta department 1916, Indian Psychoanalytical Society (Bose) 1922, Mysore department (M. V. Gopalaswamy) 1924, Indian Psychological Association 1924 to 1925, and its journal soon after. A useful memory hook is that Calcutta leads every early first: the first laboratory, the first department, the first psychoanalytical society and the first national association all came from the same small group of Calcutta scholars. Option c is right because the association was founded in the mid-1920s and 1924 is the only option in that window. Exam trap: 1922 is the Indian Psychoanalytical Society, a different body with a similar-sounding name, while 1918 and 1928 are near-miss years on either side of the true period. Key note: some sources, including many textbooks, date the founding to 1925; 1924 is the printed answer and stands.", "traps": {"a": "1918 is too early: India had only just got its first psychology department (Calcutta, 1916), and there was no national association yet.", "b": "1922 is the year Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society, a separate body that candidates often confuse with the Indian Psychological Association.", "d": "1928 is too late; by then the association already existed and its journal was running."}}, {"q": "The first edition of DSM was published by the APA in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the first edition of the DSM was published in 1952. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM) is the classification of mental disorders published by the American Psychiatric Association (APA). DSM-I (1952) was a slim manual of about 130 pages listing 106 disorders. It grew out of a US Army scheme from the Second World War and used the psychobiological language of Adolf Meyer (dominant in American psychiatry in the early twentieth century), so many disorders were called 'reactions', such as 'schizophrenic reaction'. Like DSM-II, it gave short prose descriptions rather than explicit criteria, so two psychiatrists often disagreed on a diagnosis. The editions to learn in order are DSM-I 1952, DSM-II 1968, DSM-III 1980 (explicit criteria and the multiaxial system, under Robert Spitzer), DSM-III-R 1987, DSM-IV 1994, DSM-IV-TR 2000, DSM-5 2013 and DSM-5-TR 2022. Its international partner is the World Health Organization's ICD, whose sixth revision (ICD-6, 1948) was the first to include a section on mental disorders. Option b is right because DSM-I appeared in 1952. Exam trap: the APA here is the American Psychiatric Association, not the American Psychological Association; and examiners place one-year near misses (1951, 1953) beside the true date to test exact recall.", "traps": {"a": "1951 is a one-year near miss; the manual itself came out in 1952.", "c": "1953 is a one-year near miss on the other side; DSM-I had already appeared in 1952.", "d": "1966 is close to the ICD-8 revision of the mid-1960s, which DSM-II (1968) was designed to match; it is not the date of any DSM edition."}}, {"q": "DSM-5 was published in:", "concept": "The answer is a: DSM-5 was published in 2013. The American Psychiatric Association released it on 18 May 2013 at its annual meeting in San Francisco, nineteen years after DSM-IV (1994). It dropped Roman numerals for an Arabic 5 so that later revisions could be numbered more simply, and the first full text revision, DSM-5-TR, followed in 2022. The main changes were these. DSM-5 abolished the five-axis system, listing clinical, personality and medical conditions together. It ordered its chapters roughly along the lifespan, starting with neurodevelopmental disorders. It merged autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder and related diagnoses into one autism spectrum disorder; moved obsessive-compulsive disorder and posttraumatic stress disorder out of the anxiety chapter into chapters of their own; removed the bereavement exclusion for major depression; dropped the classic subtypes of schizophrenia; and replaced the Global Assessment of Functioning with the WHO Disability Assessment Schedule. Example: a child called Riya might have been diagnosed with Asperger's disorder under DSM-IV-TR; under DSM-5 she would receive autism spectrum disorder with a severity level. Option a is right because 2013 is the publication year. Exam trap: the draft criteria were posted online for public comment in 2010, so 2010 looks plausible; and DSM-5-TR (2022) is a text revision, not a new edition.", "traps": {"b": "2015 is when the United States moved its health-care billing codes to ICD-10-CM, which DSM-5 codes had to match; it is not the year DSM-5 was published.", "c": "2017 has no DSM landmark; in India it is the year of the Mental Healthcare Act, a law, not a diagnostic manual.", "d": "2010 is when the draft DSM-5 criteria were first posted online for public comment; the finished manual came three years later."}}, {"q": "Who coined the term neurosis?", "concept": "The answer is b: the term neurosis was coined by William Cullen. William Cullen (1769) was a Scottish physician and teacher at the University of Edinburgh, one of the leading medical schools of the eighteenth century. In his classification of diseases, Synopsis Nosologiae Methodicae (1769), he used 'neurosis' for disorders of sense and motion caused by a general affection of the nervous system, without fever or any local disease of an organ. His class was very broad: it took in conditions now seen as neurological, such as palsies and epilepsy, as well as melancholia and hysteria. Later, Sigmund Freud (1895) narrowed the word to non-psychotic disorders arising from unconscious conflict, with insight preserved, as in his 'anxiety neurosis' (1895). DSM-III (1980) dropped neurosis as a formal category because it implied Freud's theory, though ICD-10 kept it in the block 'neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders'. Option b is right because Cullen introduced the word in 1769. Exam trap: neurosis came first; the word psychosis came about seventy years later and was popularised by Ernst von Feuchtersleben (1845). Examiners also offer famous early psychiatrists such as Benjamin Rush (1812) or Jean-Etienne Dominique Esquirol (1838), who are known for other firsts.", "traps": {"a": "Jean-Etienne Dominique Esquirol, Philippe Pinel's student, gave the classic definition of hallucination (1838) and described monomania; he did not coin neurosis.", "c": "Benjamin Rush is called the father of American psychiatry and wrote the first American psychiatry textbook (1812); he did not coin neurosis.", "d": "William Sargant was a twentieth-century British psychiatrist known for physical treatments and for Battle for the Mind (1957), on brainwashing; he came far too late to coin neurosis."}}, {"q": "Who coined the term psychiatry?", "concept": "The answer is b: the term psychiatry was coined by Johann Christian Reil. Johann Christian Reil (1808) was a German physician and anatomist at Halle who introduced the word 'Psychiaterie' in 1808, soon shortened to 'Psychiatrie'. It joins the Greek psyche (soul or mind) and iatreia (healing), so it means the medical treatment of the mind. Reil argued that physicians, not jailers or clergy, should treat mental illness, and that they should use psychological methods. His earlier book, Rhapsodies on the Application of Psychological Methods of Cure to Mental Disturbances (1803), proposed humane and even theatrical psychological treatment in asylums. Anatomists also know him for the insula, once called the 'island of Reil'. Psychiatry then grew into a medical speciality during the nineteenth century. Option b is right because Reil introduced the word itself. Exam trap: examiners mix up 'coined the term' with 'father of' titles. Johann Weyer (1563) is called by some the father of modern psychiatry for arguing that many accused witches were mentally ill; Philippe Pinel (1793) is famous for unchaining patients at Bicêtre; and John Conolly (1839) championed non-restraint in England. None of them coined the word.", "traps": {"a": "Johann Weyer, in De Praestigiis Daemonum (1563), argued that many accused witches were mentally ill; he is sometimes called the father of modern psychiatry but did not coin the word.", "c": "John Conolly introduced non-restraint at the Hanwell asylum near London (1839); he was an asylum reformer, not the coiner of psychiatry.", "d": "Philippe Pinel unchained patients at Bicêtre (1793) and began moral treatment in France; he is a founding figure of psychiatry, but the term came from Reil."}}, {"q": "Who was the founder of Clinical Psychology?", "concept": "The answer is a: the founder of clinical psychology is Lightner Witmer. Lightner Witmer (1896) was an American psychologist who earned his PhD under Wilhelm Wundt (1879, the Leipzig laboratory) and then taught at the University of Pennsylvania. In 1896 he opened the first psychological clinic there, mainly for children with learning and behaviour problems referred by schools. His first case was a fourteen-year-old 'chronic bad speller'. Instead of labelling the boy, Witmer tested his vision, memory and reading, found poor eyesight behind part of the problem, and helped him through corrected vision and individual teaching. In 1907 he founded the journal The Psychological Clinic and in it first used the term 'clinical psychology' in print. His model, careful assessment first and then a practical remedy, is the root of the profession. Option a is right because Witmer both founded the first clinic and named the field. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud (psychoanalysis, 1896) and Emil Kraepelin (psychiatric classification, 1883) are often offered as 'fathers' of clinical work, but they founded psychoanalysis and modern psychiatric classification; and do not swap Witmer's clinic (1896) with Wundt's laboratory (1879).", "traps": {"b": "Francis Galton opened the anthropometric laboratory in London (1884) and is the father of individual differences; he did not found clinical psychology.", "c": "William James wrote The Principles of Psychology (1890) and is the father of American psychology and functionalism, not of clinical psychology.", "d": "Wilhelm Griesinger, a German psychiatrist, held that mental diseases are brain diseases (1845); he belongs to the history of biological psychiatry, not clinical psychology."}}, {"q": "Who coined the terms 'catatonia', 'cyclothymia', 'verbigeration' and 'symptom complex'?", "concept": "The answer is a: these terms were coined by Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum. Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum (1874) was a German psychiatrist who ran a private asylum at Görlitz. He argued that mental illnesses should be classified not by single symptoms but by the whole clinical picture over time: the pattern of symptoms that occur together (the 'symptom complex') and the course and outcome of the illness. In his monograph on catatonia (1874), which he called 'tension insanity', he described patients with stupor, mutism, rigid posturing, waxy flexibility and bouts of excitement. Verbigeration, the repetition of meaningless words or phrases, comes from his descriptions of these patients, and cyclothymia, a milder cyclic mood disorder, is credited to him and his colleague Ewald Hecker. Example: Kabir sits motionless for hours, keeps his arm wherever the doctor places it, and repeats one phrase again and again; these are stupor, waxy flexibility and verbigeration. Option a is right because catatonia, verbigeration, cyclothymia and the symptom-complex approach all go back to Kahlbaum. Exam trap: Ewald Hecker (1871) described hebephrenia, and Emil Kraepelin (1893) later folded both catatonia and hebephrenia into dementia praecox, so both names sit close to Kahlbaum in history questions. Today catatonia is a specifier that can occur in schizophrenia, mood disorders and medical conditions.", "traps": {"b": "Emil Kraepelin (1893, 1899) borrowed Kahlbaum's catatonia and Hecker's hebephrenia and grouped them under dementia praecox; he did not coin these terms.", "c": "Ewald Hecker, Kahlbaum's colleague, described hebephrenia (1871) and later wrote on cyclothymia, but catatonia, verbigeration and the symptom-complex idea are Kahlbaum's.", "d": "Jean-Etienne Dominique Esquirol, Philippe Pinel's student, defined hallucination (1838) and described monomania; he did not coin catatonia."}}, {"q": "Who coined the term dementia praecox?", "concept": "The answer is a: the term dementia praecox is credited to Emil Kraepelin. Emil Kraepelin (1893) was a German psychiatrist, once a student of Wilhelm Wundt (1879), who classified mental disorders by their symptoms, course and outcome. In the fourth edition of his textbook (1893) he used the Latin dementia praecox, 'premature dementia', and in the sixth edition (1899) he made it one of psychiatry's two great psychoses. He united Ewald Hecker's hebephrenia (1871), Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum's catatonia (1874) and paranoid forms into one illness that began in youth and ran a deteriorating course. He set it against manic-depressive insanity, which came in episodes with recovery in between. This 'Kraepelinian dichotomy' still divides schizophrenia from bipolar disorder in DSM and ICD. Eugen Bleuler (1908, 1911) later renamed the illness schizophrenia, because not every patient deteriorated or fell ill young. Option a is right because Kraepelin made dementia praecox a diagnosis and is the standard answer for the term. Exam trap: Benedict Morel's (1850s) French démence précoce sounds identical, so examiners use Morel as bait, and some exams key him. Key note: Benedict Morel used the French 'démence précoce' (1852 to 1860) for young people with early deterioration; Arnold Pick used the Latin 'dementia praecox' in 1891, and Emil Kraepelin made it a diagnosis (1893, then 1899). Exams that want 'coined the French term' key Morel; the printed answer is Kraepelin and stands.", "traps": {"b": "Benedict Morel used the French démence précoce in the 1850s for young patients who deteriorated; it was a description, not the diagnostic category Kraepelin built.", "c": "Carl Wernicke described sensory aphasia (1874) and Wernicke's encephalopathy (1881); he was a brain-localisation neuropsychiatrist, not the author of dementia praecox.", "d": "Karl Kleist, a follower of Carl Wernicke, divided the psychoses into many brain-based forms (a line later carried on by Karl Leonhard); he did not coin dementia praecox."}}, {"q": "Who coined the term hypnotism?", "concept": "The answer is a: the term hypnotism was coined by James Braid. James Braid (1843) was a Scottish surgeon working in Manchester. In 1841 he watched a stage demonstration of mesmerism and set out to explain it without any magnetic fluid. He found he could induce the trance simply by having a person stare fixedly at a bright object, which he put down to fatigue of the eyes and nervous system. In Neurypnology (1843) he called it neuro-hypnotism, soon shortened to hypnotism, from the Greek hypnos, sleep. He later realised that hypnosis is not sleep but intense concentration on a single idea, and tried to rename it 'monoideism', but the word hypnotism had stuck. Before him, Franz Anton Mesmer (1770s) had claimed an 'animal magnetism' that a French royal commission in 1784 put down to imagination. After him, Jean-Martin Charcot (1880s) treated hypnotisability as a sign of hysteria at the Salpêtrière, and the Nancy school of Ambroise-Auguste Liebeault and Hippolyte Bernheim (1880s) showed it was normal suggestion. Option a is right because Braid introduced both the word and a natural, non-magnetic account of the trance. Exam trap: mesmerism is named after Mesmer, but hypnotism was named by Braid; John Elliotson (1830s and 1840s), who used mesmerism in London, and Charcot, who used hypnosis on hysteria, are the usual near-miss names.", "traps": {"b": "John Elliotson was a London physician who championed mesmerism in the 1830s and 1840s and founded the journal The Zoist (1843); he used mesmeric trance but did not coin hypnotism.", "c": "Jean-Martin Charcot used hypnosis to study hysteria at the Salpêtrière in Paris in the 1870s and 1880s and taught Sigmund Freud (1885 to 1886); he came after the word was coined.", "d": "Cohen has no place in the history of hypnosis; it is a filler name."}}, {"q": "Who is the father of psychoanalysis?", "concept": "The answer is b: the father of psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud. Sigmund Freud (1896) was an Austrian neurologist in Vienna who first used the word 'psychoanalysis' in 1896. His path ran through hysteria. He studied with Jean-Martin Charcot in Paris (1885 to 1886), saw Hippolyte Bernheim's work on suggestion at Nancy (1889), and with Josef Breuer published Studies on Hysteria (1895), built around Anna O. and the 'talking cure'. Freud then dropped hypnosis for free association, in which the patient says whatever comes to mind, and added dream analysis (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), the analysis of slips, and the handling of resistance and transference. Psychoanalysis is at once a theory of personality (unconscious conflict among id, ego and superego, and the psychosexual stages), a method of investigation and a form of therapy. Example: when Meera, secretly angry at her sister, says 'I'm glad you're leaving' when she meant 'sad', a psychoanalyst would read the slip as a hidden wish breaking through. Option b is right because Freud created the theory, the method and the name. Exam trap: Alfred Adler and Carl Jung were early followers who broke away (1911 and 1913) to found individual psychology and analytical psychology; they are not founders of psychoanalysis. In India the father of psychoanalysis is Girindrasekhar Bose (Indian Psychoanalytical Society, 1922), a separate question.", "traps": {"a": "Hippolyte Bernheim led the Nancy school, which showed that hypnosis works through suggestion; Freud learned from him in 1889 and translated his work, but Bernheim did not found psychoanalysis.", "c": "Alfred Adler left Freud's circle in 1911 and founded individual psychology (inferiority feelings, striving for superiority).", "d": "Carl Jung, once Freud's chosen heir, broke with him in 1913 and founded analytical psychology (collective unconscious, archetypes)."}}, {"q": "The one who coined the term schizophrenia is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the term schizophrenia was coined by Eugen Bleuler. Eugen Bleuler (1908) was a Swiss psychiatrist who directed the Burghölzli hospital in Zurich, where Carl Jung (from 1900) trained under him. He introduced 'schizophrenia' in a 1908 lecture and developed it in his 1911 monograph, Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. He rejected the older name of Emil Kraepelin (1893) because many patients did not deteriorate, and some fell ill later in life. The new word comes from the Greek schizein (to split) and phren (mind): a splitting of the links between thought, emotion and behaviour, not a split or multiple personality. Bleuler's fundamental symptoms, the four As, were loosened associations, blunted or incongruous affect, ambivalence and autism (withdrawal into an inner world); he coined ambivalence and used autism in this sense. He counted hallucinations and delusions as only accessory symptoms, because they occur in other illnesses too. He also spoke of a 'group' of schizophrenias, an early version of today's spectrum idea. Option a is right because Bleuler coined the word. Exam trap: 'split mind' is often wrongly read as dissociative identity disorder; and keep the chain of names in order: Benedict Morel's démence précoce (1850s), Kraepelin's dementia praecox (1893, 1899), Bleuler's schizophrenia (1908, 1911).", "traps": {"b": "Emil Kraepelin named the illness dementia praecox (1893, 1899) and separated it from manic-depressive insanity; Bleuler later renamed it schizophrenia.", "c": "Benedict Morel used démence précoce in the 1850s for early deterioration in young people, the oldest name in the chain, not schizophrenia.", "d": "Jean-Etienne Dominique Esquirol defined hallucination (1838) and described monomania; he had no role in naming schizophrenia."}}, {"q": "Who pioneered insulin coma therapy for schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is b: insulin coma therapy for schizophrenia was pioneered by Manfred Sakel. Manfred Sakel (1933) was an Austrian physician who had used insulin to ease morphine withdrawal and noticed that low blood sugar seemed to calm some patients. In Vienna in 1933 he began giving patients with schizophrenia large doses of insulin to produce a hypoglycaemic coma, repeated day after day for weeks, ending each coma with glucose. Insulin coma therapy spread across the world in the 1930s and 1940s, even though it was dangerous and demanding to run, and some patients died. Later work suggested that much of its benefit came from the intense nursing care and attention the patients received rather than from the coma itself, and after chlorpromazine (1952) it was abandoned. It was one of a cluster of 1930s 'shock' treatments. Ladislas von Meduna (1934) induced convulsions with camphor and then Metrazol (cardiazol), wrongly believing that epilepsy and schizophrenia were antagonistic, and Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini (1938) introduced electroconvulsive therapy. Option b is right because the insulin coma method is Sakel's. Exam trap: the three shock therapies are easily swapped: insulin coma is Sakel (1933), chemical convulsions Meduna (1934) and electrical convulsions Cerletti and Bini (1938). Julius Wagner-Jauregg's (1917) malaria fever therapy, for general paresis rather than schizophrenia, came before all three.", "traps": {"a": "Ugo Cerletti, with Lucio Bini, gave the first electroconvulsive therapy in Rome (1938); that used electric current, not insulin.", "c": "Ladislas von Meduna produced therapeutic convulsions with camphor (1934) and then Metrazol (cardiazol), a chemical convulsive therapy, not insulin coma.", "d": "Julius Wagner-Jauregg gave malaria fever therapy (1917) for general paresis (neurosyphilis) and won the Nobel Prize in 1927; it was not for schizophrenia and did not use insulin."}}, {"q": "Who is the founder of the electrical apparatus for producing convulsions?", "concept": "The answer is b: the electrical method of producing convulsions was founded by Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini. Ugo Cerletti (1938), a neuropsychiatrist at the University of Rome, and his assistant Lucio Bini (1938) gave the first electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) in April 1938. Convulsions were already being used to treat schizophrenia after Ladislas von Meduna (1934) induced them with camphor and Metrazol, but drug-induced fits were frightening and unpredictable. Cerletti, who had studied electrically induced seizures in animals, wanted a cleaner and more controllable method; Bini built the apparatus, which passed a brief current between electrodes placed on the temples. Their first patient, a man with schizophrenia, improved after a series of treatments, and ECT spread quickly. Modern ECT is 'modified': it is given under general anaesthesia with a muscle relaxant, so there is no violent convulsion, and it is used mainly for severe or treatment-resistant depression, catatonia and some cases of mania. India's Mental Healthcare Act (2017) forbids unmodified ECT. Option b is right because Cerletti and Bini invented the electrical apparatus and method. Exam trap: Meduna is the tempting option because he started convulsive therapy, but his convulsions were chemical, not electrical; and Scribonius Largus (about AD 46) used electricity from a torpedo fish for headache, not to produce convulsions.", "traps": {"a": "Ladislas von Meduna began convulsive therapy for schizophrenia (1934) using camphor and then Metrazol (cardiazol); his convulsions were induced chemically, not by an electrical apparatus.", "c": "Scribonius Largus, physician to the Roman emperor Claudius (about AD 46), applied a live electric torpedo fish to the head to relieve headache; that was electrical treatment but not convulsive therapy.", "d": "Antonio Egas Moniz introduced prefrontal leucotomy (1935), a brain operation, and won the Nobel Prize in 1949; he did not produce convulsions."}}, {"q": "Illnesses that seem to result from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called:", "concept": "The answer is b: illnesses that result from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called psychosomatic. The word joins the Greek psyche (mind) and soma (body) and is usually traced to the German psychiatrist Johann Christian August Heinroth (1818). A psychosomatic disorder is a real physical illness, with real tissue damage or measurable bodily change, whose onset or course is shaped by psychological factors such as stress, emotion and coping. Franz Alexander (1950), in Psychosomatic Medicine, named seven classic examples, the 'holy seven': peptic ulcer, essential hypertension, bronchial asthma, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis, neurodermatitis and thyrotoxicosis. His idea that each is caused by a specific unconscious conflict failed (most ulcers proved to be caused by the bacterium Helicobacter pylori), but the broader point survived: stress acts on the body through the autonomic nervous system, stress hormones and the immune system. George Engel's (1977) biopsychosocial model extended this to all illness. DSM-II called these psychophysiological disorders, and DSM-5 uses 'psychological factors affecting other medical conditions'. Example: Arjun has genuine asthma, and his attacks become more frequent and severe in the weeks before his board examinations. Option b is right because only psychosomatic names an illness produced by mind and body acting together. Exam trap: psychosomatic does not mean imaginary. In conversion disorder (the old hysteria) there is no physical disease behind the symptom, whereas in a psychosomatic illness the disease is real.", "traps": {"a": "Hysterical is the old name for symptoms such as paralysis or blindness with no physical disease, explained psychologically by Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895); it does not involve a real bodily illness.", "c": "Somatic simply means bodily; it names the physical side alone and leaves out the psychological interaction the stem describes.", "d": "Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5) is a loss of motor or sensory function that does not fit any neurological disease; it is not a real physical illness worsened by stress."}}, {"q": "Freud published The Interpretation of Dreams in:", "concept": "The answer is a: Sigmund Freud published The Interpretation of Dreams in 1900. Sigmund Freud (1900) wrote the book, Die Traumdeutung, after years of analysing his own dreams, a self-analysis he pursued after his father's death in 1896. It was actually printed in November 1899, but the publisher, Franz Deuticke, dated it 1900 so that it would belong to the new century; exams therefore use 1900. Only about 600 copies were printed and they took years to sell, yet the book became the founding text of psychoanalysis. Freud called the interpretation of dreams 'the royal road to a knowledge of the unconscious'. Each dream is the disguised fulfilment of a repressed wish. The manifest content is the dream as remembered; the latent content is its hidden meaning; and the dream work turns one into the other by condensation, displacement, symbolisation and secondary revision. Example: Riya dreams that she keeps missing a train; a Freudian would look past the train (manifest content) for the hidden wish it disguises (latent content). Option a is right because 1900 is the publication date printed on the book. Exam trap: the near-miss years belong to other events: 1902 is when Freud's Wednesday Psychological Society began meeting, and 1904 is when The Psychopathology of Everyday Life appeared as a book (it was first published in a journal in 1901).", "traps": {"b": "1898 is too early: Freud was still writing the book then, and it was printed only in November 1899.", "c": "1902 is the year Freud's Wednesday Psychological Society first met in his home, the seed of the Vienna Psychoanalytic Society; it is not the year of the dream book.", "d": "1904 is when The Psychopathology of Everyday Life, Freud's book on slips and forgetting, came out in book form; the dream book had appeared four years earlier."}}, {"q": "In which way is behaviour different from mental processes?", "concept": "The answer is b: behaviour differs from mental processes in that behaviour can be observed directly. Psychology today is defined as the scientific study of behaviour and mental processes. Behaviour is anything an organism does that others can see, hear or record: speaking, walking, blushing, a change in heart rate on a monitor. It is overt, public and open to observation and measurement. Mental processes, such as perceiving, thinking, remembering, feeling and dreaming, are internal and covert. No one else can watch them directly; they are known only through the person's own report (introspection) or inferred from behaviour, such as reaction times, test answers or brain activity. This difference shaped the history of psychology. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) and Edward Bradford Titchener (1898) studied conscious experience through trained introspection, but reports varied between laboratories. John B. Watson (1913) therefore argued in his behaviourist manifesto that psychology should study only observable behaviour. After the cognitive revolution of the 1950s, psychologists brought mental processes back, studying them through careful observation of behaviour. Example: when Riya bites her nails before an exam, the nail-biting is behaviour her friend can observe; her worry that she will forget the answers is a mental process only she experiences directly. Option b is right because observability is what separates behaviour from mental processes. Exam trap: introspection is tempting because it is linked with the mind, but it is the route to mental processes, so it marks them off from behaviour rather than describing behaviour.", "traps": {"a": "Introspection, looking inward at one's own experience (Wilhelm Wundt, Edward Titchener), is the route to mental processes; it is what mental processes require, not what makes behaviour different.", "c": "Research applies to both behaviour and mental processes, since psychology studies both scientifically, so it cannot be the difference between them.", "d": "Psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud's method of reaching unconscious mental processes through free association and dreams; it does not mark out behaviour."}}, {"q": "Who is particularly interested in studying patterns of behaviour, beliefs and values which are shared by people?", "concept": "The answer is a: a social psychologist is the specialist most interested in the patterns of behaviour, beliefs and values that people share. Social psychology is the scientific study of how individuals think, feel and act in relation to other people. Gordon Allport (1954) gave the classic definition: it studies how a person's thoughts, feelings and behaviour are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of others. Shared patterns of behaviour, beliefs and values are what social psychologists call norms, attitudes and culture. They ask how such patterns form, how they spread through a group and why people go along with them. The classic evidence is Muzafer Sherif's (1936) autokinetic study: in a dark room a still point of light seems to move, and when people judged its movement together, their separate estimates soon converged on a shared group norm, which they kept even when later tested alone. Think of Riya, who joins a new college where everyone dresses formally on Mondays; within weeks she does too, without anyone telling her. A social psychologist would study how that shared habit arose and why she adopted it. Subfields such as cultural and cross-cultural psychology push the same question across whole societies. Option a is right because only social psychology takes shared, group-level patterns of thought and action as its central subject. Exam trap: the wording about shared beliefs and values sounds like a definition of culture, and students drift towards a familiar individual-focused field; clinical psychology and psychoanalysis study one person's problems or unconscious, and experimental psychology is defined by its method, not by this topic.", "traps": {"b": "An experimental psychologist studies basic processes such as sensation, perception, learning and memory through controlled laboratory experiments, in the tradition of Wilhelm Wundt's Leipzig laboratory (1879). The field is defined by its method, not by the study of beliefs and values people share.", "c": "A clinical psychologist assesses, diagnoses and treats psychological disorders in individuals, a profession Lightner Witmer began with his clinic in 1896. The focus is one person's distress, not group-wide patterns.", "d": "A psychoanalyst practises Sigmund Freud's method of exploring one person's unconscious conflicts through free association and dream analysis. It looks inward at a single mind, not at shared beliefs and values."}}, {"q": "Which area of the hypothalamus is responsible for satiety behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is b: the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) is the classic satiety centre, the area that signals 'stop eating'. The hypothalamus is a small structure below the thalamus that controls hunger, thirst, body temperature, sexual behaviour and the pituitary gland. The classic evidence came from lesion studies in rats. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) found that destroying the VMH made rats overeat (hyperphagia) and become very obese. Then the Indian physiologist Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck (1951) found the opposite: lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (LH) made rats stop eating (aphagia) and drinking (adipsia), sometimes until they starved. Eliot Stellar (1954) joined the two findings in the dual-centre theory: the LH is the hunger or feeding centre and the VMH is the satiety centre. Picture Kabir finishing a large lunch: as blood glucose and gut signals rise, the satiety system tells him he is full; a rat with a damaged VMH never gets that stop message and keeps eating. Option b is right because damage to the VMH removes satiety and produces overeating, which shows that its normal job is to end a meal. Exam trap: students reverse the pair. Lateral lesion, lose appetite; ventromedial lesion, very hungry. Key note: the modern view is more complex, because VMH lesions also raise insulin and fat storage, and signals such as leptin from fat cells act on the arcuate and paraventricular nuclei, so no single centre controls eating. The classic dual-centre answer still stands, and the VMH is the satiety area.", "traps": {"a": "The lateral hypothalamus is the classic hunger or feeding centre: Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck (1951) showed that LH lesions cause aphagia (no eating), so it starts eating rather than stopping it.", "c": "'Hyper Phobia' is not an area of the hypothalamus at all; it looks like a garbled form of hyperphagia (overeating), which is the result of VMH damage, not a brain region.", "d": "Antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin) is a hormone made in the hypothalamus and released from the posterior pituitary to make the kidneys retain water. It governs water balance, not satiety, and it is a hormone, not a brain area."}}, {"q": "Which perspective of psychology focuses on determining the extent to which psychological characteristics such as memory and intelligence are influenced by heredity?", "concept": "The answer is a: the neuroscience (biological) perspective asks how far traits such as memory and intelligence are shaped by heredity. This perspective explains behaviour through the body: genes, the brain, hormones and evolution. Its branch called behaviour genetics measures heredity's share by comparing people who differ in how many genes they share. Sir Francis Galton (1869, Hereditary Genius) first argued that ability runs in families and later turned to twins. Identical (monozygotic) twins share all their genes; fraternal (dizygotic) twins share about half, like ordinary siblings. If identical twins are much more alike in IQ than fraternal twins, heredity matters. Adoption studies add a second test: are adopted children more like their birth parents or the parents who raised them? The Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart, led by Thomas Bouchard (from 1979), found that identical twins raised in different homes were still strikingly alike in intelligence and personality. The result is a heritability estimate, the share of variation in a group that is linked to genetic differences; it describes groups, not one person. For example, Meera and her identical twin score within a few points of each other on an IQ test, while Meera and her fraternal brother differ more. Option a is right because the stem follows Benjamin Lahey's account of the neuroscience perspective, the biological view that also asks how far psychological characteristics are inherited. Exam trap: 'biogenic' sounds like the right biological word, but it means 'of biological origin' and is the abnormal psychology label for biological causes of disorders (as against psychogenic causes), not one of the perspectives of psychology. Key note: the coaching pack keys b (biogenic), but the stem follows Benjamin Lahey's Psychology: An Introduction, where neuroscientists 'also seek to determine the extent to which our psychological characteristics, such as intelligence and emotional stability, are influenced by heredity', so a is used here.", "traps": {"b": "'Biogenic' means having a biological origin; abnormal psychology texts use it for biological explanations of disorders, set against psychogenic ones. It is not one of the perspectives of psychology, and introductory texts such as Benjamin Lahey's place the heredity question under the neuroscience perspective.", "c": "The cognitive perspective, named by Ulric Neisser (1967), studies internal mental processes such as memory, attention and thinking as information processing. It asks how memory works, not whether it is inherited.", "d": "The socio-cultural perspective, rooted in Lev Vygotsky's work in the 1920s and 1930s, explains behaviour through social context and culture. It stresses environment and upbringing, the opposite pole from heredity."}}, {"q": "Which of the following relates to disorders in the comprehension or production of speech?", "concept": "The answer is d: dysphasia, aphasia and Broca's aphasia all describe disorders in understanding or producing speech, so 'All of these' fits. Aphasia is a loss of language caused by brain damage, usually a stroke in the left hemisphere. It is a language problem, not a problem with the muscles of the mouth or with hearing. Dysphasia is the partial or milder form; many clinicians, especially in Britain, use the two words almost as synonyms. The classic evidence comes from two nineteenth-century cases. Paul Broca (1861) studied a patient nicknamed 'Tan', who could say little more than that one syllable but understood speech; at autopsy he had damage in the left inferior frontal lobe, now called Broca's area. Broca's aphasia is therefore expressive or non-fluent aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with fairly good comprehension. Carl Wernicke (1874) then described the reverse: fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension, after damage to the left posterior superior temporal lobe (Wernicke's area). Imagine Arjun's grandfather after a stroke: he understands his family but can only say 'want... tea... now', which is Broca's aphasia. Option d is right because each term names a disorder of the comprehension or production of speech: aphasia (the general loss), dysphasia (the partial form) and Broca's aphasia (a specific production type). Exam trap: look-alike words. Dysphagia (with a g) is difficulty in swallowing, dysarthria is slurred speech from weak or poorly controlled speech muscles, and dyslexia is a specific difficulty with reading; none of them belongs to the aphasia family.", "traps": {"a": "Dysphasia is a partial impairment of language after brain damage, so it does fit the stem, but choosing it alone ignores that aphasia and Broca's aphasia fit as well.", "b": "Aphasia, the loss of language after brain damage described by Paul Broca and Carl Wernicke, fits the stem, but it is only one of three correct terms, so picking it alone misses 'All of these'.", "c": "Broca's aphasia is the non-fluent production type caused by left frontal damage (Broca's area). It is correct but narrower than the stem, and the other two options fit too."}}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is involved in planning, decision-making and some aspects of language?", "concept": "The answer is a: the frontal lobe handles planning, decision-making and some aspects of language. The frontal lobe is the largest lobe, lying in front of the central sulcus. Its front part, the prefrontal cortex, runs the executive functions: setting goals, planning steps, weighing choices, holding information in working memory and holding back impulses. Just in front of the central sulcus lies the primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus), which commands voluntary movement. In the left hemisphere of most people, the inferior frontal gyrus contains Broca's area, named after Paul Broca (1861), which produces fluent speech; that is the 'some aspects of language' in the stem. The classic case is the railway foreman Phineas Gage (1848): an iron rod passed through his frontal lobes and he survived, but his doctor John Harlow (1868) reported that he became impulsive, rude and unable to keep to his plans. For example, when Kabir draws up a revision timetable, chooses which subject to study first and resists checking his phone, his prefrontal cortex is doing the work. Option a is right because planning, decision-making and speech production (Broca's area) all sit in the frontal lobe. Exam trap: language is spread over two lobes. Speech production (Broca's area) is frontal, while understanding speech (Wernicke's area) is temporal, so the word 'language' can tempt students to choose the temporal lobe.", "traps": {"b": "The parietal lobe, behind the central sulcus, holds the primary somatosensory cortex (touch, pain, body position) and handles spatial attention; damage causes problems such as neglect, not loss of planning.", "c": "The temporal lobe handles hearing, memory (the hippocampus on its inner side) and the understanding of speech (Wernicke's area). It deals with comprehension, not planning or decision-making.", "d": "The occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, contains the primary visual cortex and is devoted to vision. It has no role in planning or decisions."}}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is involved in listening?", "concept": "The answer is c: the temporal lobe is the lobe most involved in listening (hearing). The temporal lobe lies below the lateral (Sylvian) fissure, on each side of the head near the ears. Sound waves are turned into nerve signals in the cochlea of the inner ear; the auditory nerve carries them through the brainstem and the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus (Brodmann areas 41 and 42), on the upper surface of the temporal lobe. This cortex is tonotopic: like a piano keyboard, neighbouring cells respond to neighbouring pitches. Next to it, in the left hemisphere, lies Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874), which lets us understand spoken words; damage there leaves hearing intact but makes speech meaningless to the listener. Classic evidence came from Wilder Penfield (from the 1930s), who electrically stimulated the temporal lobes of awake patients during epilepsy surgery; many reported hearing sounds, music or voices. When Meera listens to a raga and recognises it, her temporal lobes hear the notes and match them to memory, since the inner part of the temporal lobe (the hippocampus) also supports memory. Option c is right because the primary auditory cortex and the area for understanding speech both lie in the temporal lobe. Exam trap: this paper runs a series of lobe questions with the same four options. Use a simple map: frontal plans and speaks, parietal feels touch and space, temporal hears and remembers, occipital sees.", "traps": {"a": "The frontal lobe plans, decides and produces speech through Broca's area; it helps you reply, but it does not receive or decode sounds.", "b": "The parietal lobe processes touch, body position and spatial awareness in the somatosensory cortex of the postcentral gyrus; it is not the hearing area.", "d": "The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex and processes sight, not sound."}}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is involved in vision?", "concept": "The answer is d: the occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, is the lobe devoted to vision. Light is turned into nerve signals by the rods and cones of the retina. The two optic nerves meet at the optic chiasm, where fibres from the inner half of each retina cross, so each side of the brain receives the opposite half of the visual field. The signals pass to the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus and then, through the optic radiations, to the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) along the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe. Classic evidence came from David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959), who recorded single cells in the cat's visual cortex and found feature detectors that fire to lines and edges at particular angles; they shared the Nobel Prize in 1981. From V1, information flows along two streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe and a dorsal 'where' stream into the parietal lobe. If Riya is hit hard on the back of her head, she may 'see stars' or lose part of her visual field even though her eyes are unharmed. Option d is right because the primary visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: the eyes are at the front of the head but vision is processed at the back; and recognising faces and objects (the ventral stream) uses the temporal lobe, so do not confuse seeing with recognising.", "traps": {"a": "The frontal lobe plans, decides and controls movement, including eye movements through the frontal eye fields, but it does not do the basic processing of what is seen.", "b": "The parietal lobe receives the dorsal 'where' stream and handles spatial attention and touch; it uses visual information only after the occipital lobe has processed it.", "c": "The temporal lobe hears and remembers, and its lower part recognises objects and faces through the ventral stream, but the primary visual cortex itself is occipital."}}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter plays an important role in the experience of pleasure?", "concept": "The answer is b: dopamine plays the central role in the experience of pleasure and reward. Dopamine is a neurotransmitter made mainly in two midbrain areas: the substantia nigra, which serves movement, and the ventral tegmental area (VTA). The VTA sends fibres along the mesolimbic pathway to the nucleus accumbens, the brain's reward hub, and on to the prefrontal cortex. When something good happens, such as food when hungry, praise, sex, music or a drug, dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens rises and marks the experience as worth repeating. The classic evidence came from James Olds and Peter Milner (1954), who found that rats would press a lever again and again, even ignoring food, to receive electrical stimulation in this reward system. Addictive drugs act on the same circuit: cocaine and amphetamine raise dopamine at the synapse. For example, when Arjun's phone buzzes with a 'like' on his post, a small burst of dopamine makes checking the phone feel rewarding, so he checks again. Kent Berridge and Terry Robinson (1998) argued that dopamine drives 'wanting' (the urge to get a reward) more than 'liking' itself, but textbooks still link dopamine with pleasure and reward. Option b is right because dopamine in the mesolimbic reward pathway is the neurotransmitter most tied to pleasure. Exam trap: serotonin is often called the 'happy chemical' because SSRIs treat depression, but serotonin regulates mood, sleep and impulse control; reward and pleasure belong to dopamine.", "traps": {"a": "Serotonin regulates mood, sleep, appetite and impulse control, and low serotonin is linked to depression (the target of SSRIs). It affects wellbeing but is not the main pleasure and reward transmitter.", "c": "Norepinephrine (noradrenaline), mainly from the locus coeruleus, is the arousal transmitter for alertness, attention and the fight-or-flight response. It energises rather than rewards.", "d": "Acetylcholine carries signals to muscles at the neuromuscular junction and supports memory and attention; its loss marks Alzheimer's disease. It was the first transmitter discovered (Otto Loewi, 1921), not the pleasure chemical."}}, {"q": "The hormone cortisol produced by the adrenal gland is responsible for:", "concept": "The answer is b: cortisol activates the body to prepare for and cope with a stressful situation. The body has two stress systems. The fast one is the sympathetic nervous system with the adrenal medulla, which releases adrenaline within seconds (the fight-or-flight response named by Walter Cannon, 1915). The slower one is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), the pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and ACTH makes the adrenal cortex release cortisol. Cortisol raises blood glucose by breaking down stored protein and fat, keeps energy flowing through a long stressor, and damps inflammation and immune activity. Hans Selye (1936, 1956) described the general adaptation syndrome: alarm, resistance and exhaustion. Cortisol carries the body through the resistance stage, but if stress never ends, high cortisol harms memory, sleep and immunity. Cortisol also feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary to switch the axis off once the threat passes. Suppose Meera wakes on the morning of her entrance exam: her cortisol peaks, giving her the energy and alertness to face the paper. Option b is right because the main job of cortisol is to mobilise energy and keep the body ready for a stressor. Exam trap: cortisol and adrenaline both come from the adrenal gland, but adrenaline comes from the inner medulla (fast) and cortisol from the outer cortex (slow); a question naming the adrenal cortex or the HPA axis means cortisol.", "traps": {"a": "Reducing body activity so that we can rest is the job of the parasympathetic nervous system (rest and digest, through the vagus nerve and acetylcholine), the opposite of cortisol's mobilising role.", "c": "Cortisol's negative feedback does help switch off the stress response, but keeping homeostasis is the general job of the hypothalamus and the whole autonomic and endocrine system; the specific role of cortisol is mobilising the body for stress.", "d": "Planning and decision-making are functions of the prefrontal cortex in the frontal lobe, not of any hormone."}}, {"q": "The process through which a learned response stops occurring due to the non-availability of reinforcement is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: extinction is the process by which a learned response stops occurring because reinforcement is no longer given. In classical conditioning, Ivan Pavlov (1927, Conditioned Reflexes) found that once a dog salivated to a bell paired with food, ringing the bell again and again without food made the salivation fade. Pavlov called the food the 'reinforcement', so extinction there means the conditioned stimulus (CS) appears without the unconditioned stimulus (UCS). In operant conditioning, B. F. Skinner (1938) showed that a rat stops pressing a lever once presses no longer bring food. Often there is first an extinction burst, a short spurt of harder or more varied responding, before the behaviour fades. Extinction is not forgetting: it is new learning that the cue or action no longer predicts the reward. The proof is spontaneous recovery, when the extinguished response suddenly returns after a rest. Example: Riya's dog begs at the dinner table; when the whole family stops giving it scraps, it begs harder for a few days (the burst), then gives up. Option d is right because the stem describes a learned response dying out when its reinforcement is withdrawn. Exam trap: punishment also makes behaviour decrease, but punishment adds an unpleasant consequence or removes a pleasant one; extinction simply stops delivering the reinforcer that kept the behaviour going.", "traps": {"a": "Generalisation is responding to stimuli similar to the original conditioned stimulus, as when Little Albert (John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner, 1920) came to fear a rabbit and a fur coat after being conditioned to fear a white rat. It spreads a response; it does not end it.", "b": "Punishment, in B. F. Skinner's terms, is a consequence that lowers behaviour by adding something unpleasant or removing something pleasant. It decreases behaviour actively, whereas extinction works by withholding reinforcement.", "c": "Spontaneous recovery is the sudden return of an extinguished response after a rest period, first shown by Ivan Pavlov. It happens after extinction and is the opposite of the response stopping."}}, {"q": "When a teacher appreciates his students every time they ask questions, with the hope that this will enable them to handle more technical questions in the future, he is using:", "concept": "The answer is d: praising students every time they ask a question is reinforcement, specifically positive reinforcement. Reinforcement is any consequence that makes a behaviour more likely to happen again. B. F. Skinner (1938, The Behavior of Organisms) built operant conditioning on this idea, extending Edward Thorndike's (1898) law of effect, which said that responses followed by satisfying results are stamped in. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, marks, a sweet); negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache goes after a tablet), and both increase behaviour. When every single response is rewarded, the schedule is continuous reinforcement, also written FR-1. It builds a new habit fastest, though the habit also dies out fastest if the praise stops; partial schedules (fixed or variable ratio and interval) build behaviour that lasts longer. Here the teacher praises Kabir and his classmates each time they raise a question, so asking questions becomes more frequent, and the teacher hopes the confidence will carry over to harder, technical questions later. Option d is right because the teacher strengthens question-asking by following it, every time, with a pleasant consequence. Exam trap: the phrase about handling more technical questions in future tempts students towards shaping, but the stem never describes rewarding closer and closer steps; and reinforcement always increases behaviour, so do not confuse it with punishment. Key note: praising every question is continuous positive reinforcement. Shaping would mean reinforcing closer and closer approximations to the harder questions; the stem hints at that goal but describes praising every question, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation, 1958) pairs relaxation with a graded anxiety hierarchy to remove fear. The students are not anxious and no relaxation is used, so it does not fit.", "b": "Shaping, from B. F. Skinner, reinforces successive approximations to a target behaviour, raising the standard step by step. The teacher praises every question equally, so he is not selectively rewarding closer steps.", "c": "Modelling is Albert Bandura's (1961, 1977) learning by watching a model. The students learn from praise for their own behaviour, not by observing someone else ask questions."}}, {"q": "In classical conditioning, the organism creates an association between:", "concept": "The answer is c: in classical conditioning the organism learns an association between two stimuli. Ivan Pavlov (1927, Conditioned Reflexes) discovered it while studying digestion in dogs. Food in the mouth is an unconditioned stimulus (UCS) that triggers salivation, an unconditioned response (UCR), with no learning needed. A bell is at first a neutral stimulus. After the bell is repeatedly sounded just before the food, the bell alone makes the dog salivate: the bell is now a conditioned stimulus (CS) and the salivation a conditioned response (CR). What has been learned is that one stimulus (the bell) signals another (the food). Robert Rescorla (1968) sharpened this: the CS must predict the UCS, not merely occur near it; if food comes just as often without the bell, little conditioning happens. Timing matters too: the CS should come shortly before the UCS (forward conditioning). For example, Meera once ate prawns and fell ill; now even the smell of prawns makes her feel sick, because she linked the smell (CS) with the illness the food caused (UCS). Option c is right because classical conditioning pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus until the first comes to predict the second. Exam trap: operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner, 1938) links a response with its consequence, so options about responses or consequences belong to the operant side. Remember: classical is stimulus with stimulus, before the behaviour; operant is behaviour with consequence, after the behaviour.", "traps": {"a": "'Two situations' is too vague: classical conditioning is defined by pairing two specific stimuli (the CS and the UCS), not two whole settings, even though a context can act as a cue.", "b": "Consequences belong to operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner), where a behaviour is linked with the reinforcement or punishment that follows it; classical conditioning works through signals that come before any consequence.", "d": "The responses (UCR and CR) are the outcome of classical conditioning, not the things being associated; the dog does not link one response with another but links the bell with the food."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of self-actualised people according to Maslow?", "concept": "The answer is d: self-actualised people do not feel ashamed of their negative emotions, so this is not one of their characteristics. Abraham Maslow (1943, 1954) placed self-actualisation at the top of his hierarchy of needs: becoming everything one is capable of becoming. To describe it he studied people he judged psychologically healthy and fulfilled, including historical figures such as Abraham Lincoln, Eleanor Roosevelt and Albert Einstein, first in a 1950 study and later in Motivation and Personality (1970 edition). He listed about fifteen traits. They have an efficient perception of reality: they see people and events accurately and are quick to detect the fake, the phony and the dishonest. They accept themselves, others and nature, including their own flaws and weaknesses, without guilt, anxiety or shame. They are spontaneous, problem-centred rather than self-centred and autonomous; they need privacy, keep a fresh appreciation of ordinary things, have peak experiences, form few but deep friendships, hold democratic attitudes, enjoy an unhostile philosophical humour, are creative and resist blind conformity to their culture. Imagine Kabir, a mature doctor, who feels angry at an unfair decision: he admits the anger openly and deals with it, rather than hiding it in shame. Option d is right because self-acceptance, the opposite of shame about one's feelings, is a core feature of self-actualisers. Exam trap: option c sounds like a suspicious or negative trait, so students pick it as the odd one out, but sensitivity to the fake and dishonest is Maslow's own description of their efficient perception of reality.", "traps": {"a": "Living creatively and fully using one's potentials is the very definition of self-actualisation, Maslow's top need, so it is a characteristic, not the exception.", "b": "Efficient perception of reality, self and others heads Maslow's list of traits: self-actualisers see people and situations accurately.", "c": "Being sensitive to the fake and dishonest is part of that same efficient perception of reality; Maslow said self-actualisers readily detect the spurious and dishonest in people, so this is a genuine characteristic."}}, {"q": "According to Freud, the process through which individuals release their excessive aggressive energy is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: catharsis is the process through which, in the Freudian view, a person releases pent-up emotional and aggressive energy. The word is Greek for purging or cleansing. Aristotle (Poetics, about 335 BC) used it for the way watching a tragedy purges the audience of pity and fear. Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria, 1895) took it into therapy as the cathartic method: in hypnotic states, Breuer's patient Anna O. (Bertha Pappenheim) recalled the painful events behind her symptoms and expressed the feelings tied to them, and the symptoms eased. She called it the 'talking cure' and 'chimney sweeping'. Freud's energy model held that blocked emotion, including aggressive energy, builds up like steam in a boiler and must be discharged, either safely or in symptoms; this hydraulic view was later echoed by Konrad Lorenz (1966). Arjun, furious after a quarrel, pounds a pillow, expecting to let off steam: that is the catharsis idea. Research does not support venting aggression, though: Brad Bushman (2002) found that people who hit a punching bag while thinking about someone who had provoked them became more aggressive, not less. Option c is right because catharsis is the Freudian name for discharging bottled-up emotional energy. Exam trap: catharsis is a release of emotion, while sublimation is redirecting an impulse into a socially valued activity such as sport or art; both deal with drives, but only catharsis means letting the feeling out.", "traps": {"a": "Dream work is Sigmund Freud's (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) term for how the mind disguises a dream's latent content as its manifest content through condensation, displacement and symbolisation. It disguises wishes; it does not release aggressive energy.", "b": "Psychoanalysis is Freud's whole theory of personality and method of therapy (free association, dream analysis, interpretation of transference), not the specific process of releasing aggressive energy.", "d": "Slips of the tongue are parapraxes, described in Freud's The Psychopathology of Everyday Life (1901), where a hidden wish leaks out in a mistaken word. They reveal the unconscious rather than discharge aggression."}}, {"q": "A layer of nerve cells at the back of the eye which converts light waves into electrical impulses is the:", "concept": "The answer is b: the retina is the layer of nerve cells at the back of the eye that converts light into electrical impulses. Light enters through the cornea, passes through the pupil (the opening in the iris) and is focused by the lens onto the retina. The retina is really part of the brain, pushed outwards during development. Its photoreceptors are the rods (about 120 million, very sensitive, for dim light and black and white) and the cones (about 6 million, for colour and fine detail). When light strikes them, pigments such as rhodopsin change shape and alter the cell's electrical state; this change from light energy into neural signals is called transduction. The signals pass to bipolar cells and then to ganglion cells, whose axons gather at the optic disc to form the optic nerve; because that spot has no receptors, it is the blind spot. Oddly, the receptors sit at the very back of the retina, so light passes through the other cell layers first. If Riya walks from bright sunlight into a dark cinema, she sees little at first until her rods adapt over many minutes (dark adaptation). Option b is right because the retina is the light-sensitive neural layer where transduction happens. Exam trap: the fovea is a tiny pit in the centre of the retina packed with cones, giving the sharpest vision; it is part of the retina, not the whole layer, so do not pick the part for the whole.", "traps": {"a": "The fovea is a small central pit of the retina, packed with cones and free of rods, where vision is sharpest. It is only a part of the retina, not the whole layer of nerve cells.", "c": "The cornea is the clear, curved front window of the eye that does most of the focusing (refraction) of light; it has no receptors and does not convert light into impulses.", "d": "The iris is the coloured ring of muscle that widens or narrows the pupil to control how much light enters. It regulates light; it does not convert it into nerve signals."}}, {"q": "The process through which the brain uses the two retinal images into one dimensional perception is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: stereopsis is the process by which the brain fuses the two slightly different retinal images into one single percept with solid, three-dimensional depth. Our eyes sit about 6 to 7 centimetres apart, so each retina receives a slightly different view of the same scene. That difference is called retinal (binocular) disparity, and the nearer an object, the greater the disparity. The visual cortex compares the two images, using cells tuned to disparity, and fuses them into one picture in which near objects stand out from far ones. Charles Wheatstone (1838) proved this with his stereoscope: two flat drawings, each showing the view of one eye, looked solid when viewed together. Bela Julesz (1960) went further with random-dot stereograms, patterns of dots with no visible shape in either image alone, which reveal a floating square only when both eyes fuse them; so stereopsis needs no monocular cues at all. Hold a finger close to your face and close each eye in turn: the finger jumps, showing the two views Meera's brain normally merges without effort. Option b is right because stereopsis is the specific name for fusing the two retinal images into one depth percept. Exam trap: binocular cues (disparity and convergence) are the raw information, depth perception is the whole ability, and stereopsis is the fusion process itself. Key note: the stem means fusing the two retinal images into one single three-dimensional (depth) percept; that fusion with depth from binocular disparity is stereopsis (Charles Wheatstone, 1838, stereoscope). Depth perception is the broader ability, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Depth perception is the broad ability to see in three dimensions using both monocular cues (linear perspective, interposition, texture gradient) and binocular cues, famously tested with Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk's (1960) visual cliff. It is wider than the specific process of fusing the two images.", "c": "Binocular cues are the depth signals that need two eyes, retinal disparity and convergence; they are the information stereopsis uses, not the fusing process itself.", "d": "'Retinal display' is not a standard term in perception; it looks like a distortion of retinal disparity, the difference between the two images, which is the input to stereopsis rather than the fusion."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not an attribute of extraversion in the Big Five factor theory of personality?", "concept": "The answer is c: being self-conscious is not an attribute of extraversion; in the Big Five it is a facet of neuroticism. The Big Five or five-factor model describes personality with five broad traits, remembered as OCEAN: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism. They emerged from factor analyses of the trait words in everyday language, first reported by Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961), and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992) measured them with the NEO-PI-R, which splits each factor into six facets. The extraversion facets are warmth, gregariousness, assertiveness, activity, excitement-seeking and positive emotions. So a high scorer is sociable (gregariousness), talkative (assertiveness and activity) and affectionate (warmth). The neuroticism facets are anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness and vulnerability. Self-consciousness means shyness and embarrassment in front of others, a sensitivity to being judged. Consider Arjun, who chats with everyone at a wedding, hugs his cousins and is first on the dance floor: high extraversion. His cousin Riya worries that people are watching her and blushes when spoken to: that is high self-consciousness, a neuroticism facet. Option c is right because self-consciousness belongs to the neuroticism domain, not to extraversion. Exam trap: self-conscious sounds like the opposite of sociable, so students treat it as low extraversion; but introversion simply means quiet and reserved, while the anxious discomfort of self-consciousness is neuroticism. A calm introvert need not be self-conscious at all.", "traps": {"a": "Sociable is the core of the gregariousness facet of extraversion in the NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, 1992), so it is an attribute of extraversion.", "b": "Talkative is a standard marker adjective for extraversion, linked to its assertiveness and activity facets; extraverts talk readily and take the lead.", "d": "Affectionate matches the warmth facet of extraversion, the friendly closeness extraverts show; it is an extraversion attribute even though it sounds like agreeableness."}}, {"q": "Which part of personality opposes the irrational desires of the id according to Freud's psychoanalytic theory of personality?", "concept": "The answer is c: the superego opposes the irrational desires of the id on moral grounds. Sigmund Freud (1923, The Ego and the Id) divided personality into three parts. The id, present from birth, is the reservoir of instinctual drives (sex and aggression); it follows the pleasure principle and demands instant gratification, without logic or morals. The ego develops to deal with reality; it follows the reality principle and finds safe, realistic ways to satisfy the id. The superego forms last, at about age 5 to 6, as the child resolves the Oedipus complex and takes in (internalises) the parents' moral rules. It has two parts: the conscience, which punishes wrongdoing with guilt, and the ego-ideal, the picture of the perfect self, which rewards good behaviour with pride. The superego does not merely delay the id's wishes; it condemns them and tries to block them entirely, pushing the person towards moral perfection. Suppose young Kabir wants to grab his sister's chocolate (id). His ego suggests waiting until she offers to share, but his superego says taking it would be wrong and makes him feel guilty for even wanting it. Option c is right because the superego is the moral opponent of the id. Exam trap: the ego also restrains the id, so students choose a; but the ego is a negotiator, not an opponent. Key note: the ego also restrains the id, but it does so realistically (delays and channels it, the reality principle); the part that opposes the id's desires on moral grounds is the superego. The printed answer is c and stands.", "traps": {"a": "The ego, ruled by the reality principle, mediates between the id, the superego and reality: it delays and channels the id's wishes into realistic outlets rather than opposing them on moral grounds.", "b": "Psychic energy is Freud's term for the fuel that powers all mental activity; it is not a structure of personality and opposes nothing.", "d": "Libido is the energy of the life instincts (Eros), mainly sexual, and it belongs to the id; it drives the desires rather than opposing them."}}, {"q": "A psychological test is good only when it measures what it is supposed to measure. This statement refers to which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is d: a test that measures what it is supposed to measure is valid, and validity is part of being psychometrically sound. Psychometrics is the science of measuring mental qualities, and a psychometrically sound test has two essential properties. Reliability is consistency: the test gives similar scores on repeated use. Validity is accuracy of meaning: the scores really reflect the trait the test claims to measure. Validity is checked in three classic ways. Content validity asks whether the items cover the whole domain. Criterion-related validity asks whether scores agree with an outside standard, now (concurrent) or later (predictive). Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks whether the scores behave as the theory of the trait predicts, correlating with related measures (convergent) and not with unrelated ones (discriminant). A test can be reliable without being valid: if Meera builds an anxiety scale whose items are so hard to read that it really measures reading skill, students will score consistently, but the test does not measure anxiety. It cannot be valid without being reliable, because random scores predict nothing. Option d is right because, among these choices, only psychometric soundness includes validity, the property the stem defines. Exam trap: option b, consistency across administrations, is reliability, the twin concept students most often swap with validity. Key note: the stem defines validity; 'psychometrically sound' is the only option that covers validity, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Being objective, precise and short describes objectivity and practicality (ease of use), helpful features of a test, but a short objective test can still measure the wrong thing.", "b": "Consistency across several administrations is test-retest reliability, not validity; a test can give the same wrong answer every time.", "c": "Being norm based means scores are compared with a standardisation sample so that a raw score can be interpreted; norms show where a person stands, not whether the test measures the right trait."}}, {"q": "Which aspect of social interactions is measured by the theory of planned behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is d: the theory of planned behaviour is a model of attitudes and how they lead to behaviour. Icek Ajzen (1985, 1991) built it on the theory of reasoned action of Martin Fishbein and Icek Ajzen (1975). The problem they tackled was old: Richard LaPiere (1934) toured the United States with a Chinese couple and was refused service only once in about 250 restaurants and hotels, yet when he later wrote to the same places, most replied that they would not serve Chinese guests. Stated attitudes did not predict action. Ajzen's answer is that behaviour follows intention, and intention rests on three things: the attitude towards the behaviour (do I think it is good?), subjective norms (do people who matter to me want me to do it?) and perceived behavioural control (can I actually do it?). The third element is what 'planned' adds to the older model; it covers behaviours not fully under our will. Predictions are best when attitude and behaviour are measured at the same level of specificity. Take Meera, who wants to start running each morning: she believes it is healthy (attitude), her friends run (subjective norm), and she has shoes and a park nearby (control), so her intention is strong and she is likely to run. Option d is right because the theory measures attitudes, together with norms and perceived control, to predict intention and behaviour. Exam trap: the word 'behaviour' in the title leads some students away from attitudes, but this is an attitude and behaviour theory from the attitudes chapter of social psychology.", "traps": {"a": "Attachment is the emotional bond between infant and caregiver studied by John Bowlby (1969) and Mary Ainsworth (Strange Situation, 1978); it belongs to developmental psychology, not to the theory of planned behaviour.", "b": "Socialisation is the lifelong process by which people learn the norms and roles of their society from family, school and peers; subjective norms in the model are one product of it, but the theory does not measure socialisation.", "c": "Group formation is described by models such as Bruce Tuckman's (1965) forming, storming, norming and performing; the theory of planned behaviour concerns one person's attitudes and intentions, not how groups form."}}, {"q": "A strategy used by the ego to defend itself against the anxiety provoked by the conflicts of everyday life refers to:", "concept": "The answer is a: a defence mechanism is the general name for a strategy the ego uses to protect itself against the anxiety that conflict produces. In the theory of Sigmund Freud (1894, 1923), the id pushes for gratification, the superego condemns, and reality sets limits. When these pressures clash, the ego feels anxiety, a danger signal. To reduce it, the ego uses defences: unconscious processes that deny, falsify or distort reality so that a threatening impulse or idea stays out of awareness or appears in a safer form. Freud first wrote of 'defence' in 1894, and his daughter Anna Freud (1936, The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence) gave the first systematic account, with repression as the basic defence that pushes threatening material out of consciousness. Later George Vaillant (1977) ranked defences from immature (projection, acting out) to mature (humour, sublimation). Defences are normal and often useful in small doses; they become a problem when rigid or overused. Example: Arjun fails his driving test and tells friends the examiner was biased (rationalisation), then goes home and snaps at his younger brother (displacement). Both are particular defence mechanisms serving one purpose: keeping his anxiety and hurt pride at bay. Option a is right because the stem defines the whole category, and defence mechanism is the name of that category. Exam trap: the distractors are real defences or real Freudian terms, so they feel correct; but a definition of 'a strategy used by the ego to defend itself' wants the general term, not one example of it.", "traps": {"b": "Displacement is one particular defence: redirecting an impulse from a threatening target to a safer one, such as shouting at a younger brother instead of the boss. It is an example of the category, not its name.", "c": "Rationalisation is another particular defence: giving a plausible, acceptable reason in place of the real, painful one, like the fox's 'sour grapes'. It is one member of the category the stem defines.", "d": "The ego-ideal is part of the superego in Freud's structural model, the image of the perfect self that the person strives to match; it is a standard, not a defensive strategy."}}, {"q": "Development is about:", "concept": "The answer is c: development is about change, the orderly and lasting changes (and stabilities) in a person from conception to death. Developmental psychology studies how people grow and change physically, cognitively, emotionally and socially across the lifespan. Not every change counts: development means systematic, relatively lasting change, not a passing mood. Some changes come mainly from maturation, the unfolding of a genetic plan (walking, puberty); others come from learning and experience (reading, language, values). Paul Baltes (1987) set out the lifespan perspective: development is lifelong, multidimensional (many areas at once), multidirectional (gains and losses at every age, such as vocabulary growing while reaction time slows), plastic (open to change) and shaped by its historical and cultural context. Developmentalists also ask the great questions about change: nature versus nurture, continuity versus discontinuity (gradual growth or stages), and stability versus change. Think of Kabir: he crawls at 8 months, walks at about a year, speaks in sentences at 2, reasons abstractly as a teenager and grows more patient in old age. Each step is a change, and that is what development is about. Option c is right because change over time is the core subject matter of development. Exam trap: age is tempting because development is described in age stages, but age is only a marker of time and causes nothing by itself; genes and gender are influences on development, not what development is.", "traps": {"a": "Age is only the time scale on which development is plotted; developmental psychologists study what changes with age and why, so age is a marker, not the subject itself.", "b": "Gender is one influence on development and one area that develops (gender identity and gender roles), but development as a whole is not about gender.", "d": "Genes are the nature side of the nature and nurture debate, one cause of developmental change alongside environment; development is the change itself, not its genetic basis."}}, {"q": "The self-image formed during adolescence that integrates an individual's ideas of what he or she is and wants to be refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: ego-identity is the self-image formed in adolescence that joins together who a person is and who he or she wants to be. Erik Erikson (1950, Childhood and Society; 1968, Identity: Youth and Crisis) described eight psychosocial stages across the lifespan, each with a central conflict. The fifth stage, in adolescence (about 12 to 18 years), is identity versus role confusion. Teenagers try out roles, values, careers, friendships and beliefs, and ask 'Who am I?' and 'Where am I going?'. Success brings ego-identity, a stable and coherent sense of self that links the past, the present and the hoped-for future, along with the virtue of fidelity. Failure brings role confusion, an unclear sense of self. Erikson coined the phrase 'identity crisis' for this turning point. James Marcia (1966) measured identity with two questions: has the person explored options (crisis), and has he or she made a commitment? The answers give four identity statuses: diffusion (neither), foreclosure (commitment without exploration), moratorium (exploring, not yet committed) and achievement (explored, then committed). For example, Riya tries debating, coding and music in college, talks to people in each field and then commits to becoming a psychologist: she reaches identity achievement. Option b is right because Erikson's term for this integrated adolescent self-image is ego-identity. Exam trap: ego-ideal sounds almost the same, but it is Freud's term for the superego's image of the perfect self, a moral standard, not an adolescent's integrated identity.", "traps": {"a": "The ego-ideal is the part of Freud's superego that holds the image of the perfect self and rewards living up to it with pride; it is a moral standard formed in early childhood, not an adolescent self-image.", "c": "Crisis is Erikson's word for the turning point or conflict of each stage (and James Marcia's word for the period of exploration); it is the struggle that leads to identity, not the self-image itself.", "d": "The ego is Freud's executive part of personality, following the reality principle; it is a mental structure present from infancy, not the integrated identity formed in adolescence."}}, {"q": "The theory of how people explain others' behaviour by attributing it either to internal disposition or to external situation refers to:", "concept": "The answer is a: attribution theory explains how people account for others' behaviour by attributing it to internal dispositions or to external situations. Fritz Heider (1958, The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations) founded it. He saw every person as a 'naive psychologist' who wants to understand why things happen in order to predict and control the social world. His central distinction was between internal (dispositional) attributions, which put the cause in the person's traits, abilities, effort or mood, and external (situational) attributions, which put it in luck, the task, other people or circumstances. Later models built on his work. Edward E. Jones and Keith Davis (1965), in correspondent inference theory, showed when we infer a trait from an act (a freely chosen, unexpected act tells us most). Harold Kelley (1967), in his covariation model, said we weigh consensus (do others act the same way?), distinctiveness (does this person act this way only here?) and consistency (does the person always act this way?). We also show biases: Lee Ross (1977) named the fundamental attribution error, the tendency to overrate dispositions and underrate situations when judging others. Example: Arjun arrives late to class. Kabir thinks 'he is lazy' (dispositional), while Arjun knows the bus broke down (situational). Option a is right because attribution theory is the name of the whole theory of how people explain behaviour along this internal and external line. Exam trap: dispositional and situational are the two types of attribution inside the theory, not separate theories, and attributing causality is the process, not the theory's name.", "traps": {"b": "'Dispositional' describes one kind of attribution in Fritz Heider's scheme, the internal cause; there is no separate dispositional theory of how people explain behaviour (trait theories of personality are a different field).", "c": "'Situational' is the other kind of attribution in Heider's scheme, the external cause; it is a category inside attribution theory, not a theory of its own.", "d": "Attributing causality is the act that attribution theory describes, not the name of the theory; the stem asks for the theory."}}, {"q": "The behavioural approach is to reward and punishment as the cognitive approach is to _____:", "concept": "The answer is d: just as the behavioural approach explains behaviour through reward and punishment, the cognitive approach explains it through internal processes of the mind, or mental events. The behavioural approach began with John B. Watson (1913), who said psychology should study only observable behaviour, and was developed by B. F. Skinner (1938), for whom behaviour is shaped by its consequences: reinforcement makes it more likely, punishment less likely. The mind was treated as a black box. The cognitive revolution of the 1950s opened that box. Noam Chomsky (1959), in his review of Skinner's Verbal Behavior, argued that language cannot be explained by reinforcement alone, and Ulric Neisser (1967, Cognitive Psychology) named the new field. The cognitive approach studies attention, perception, memory, language, thinking, problem solving and decision-making, often through the information-processing model, which treats the mind like a computer that takes in, stores and retrieves information. Take Riya, who studies hard before an exam. A behaviourist says she studies because past good marks rewarded studying. A cognitive psychologist asks how she encodes the material, organises it in memory and retrieves it in the exam hall, and what she believes about her own ability. Option d is right because internal mental processes are to the cognitive approach what rewards and punishments are to the behavioural approach: its central explanatory idea. Exam trap: biological processes are also internal, but they belong to the biological perspective (neurons, hormones, genes), not to the cognitive one, which works at the level of thoughts and mental events.", "traps": {"a": "Biological processes such as neurons, neurotransmitters and hormones are the explanatory focus of the biological perspective, not the cognitive approach.", "b": "The struggle for self-fulfilment is the humanistic approach of Abraham Maslow (self-actualisation) and Carl Rogers, the 'third force', not cognitive psychology.", "c": "Individual genetic makeup is the focus of behaviour genetics within the biological perspective, which asks how heredity shapes traits, not how mental events work."}}]});
